window.TEACHER={"built":"2 November 2026","machines":{"socials":{"id":"socials","title":"The social studies exam machine","href":"m/socials.html","desc":"A 3D exam desk for the Year 9 social studies paper: percentage bar, multiple bar and double line graphs, reading cartoons, photographs and infographics, a values continuum on the voting age, New Zealand's system of government, three Kiwi innovations, definition flash cards and two paragraphs built part by part. Practice questions you can work through with Allen.","blurb":"Graphs, sources, values, Government and Innovation on one exam desk, with Allen.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Social studies"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Social studies · Government and innovation","crumb":"Year 9 end-of-year social studies: graphs, cartoons, photographs and infographics, note-taking, values continuums, Government and Innovation, definitions and two-paragraph answers","badge":{"label":"Year 9 social studies"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA past exams: Social Studies","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"}],"truth":"Written for practice from a Year 9 exam outline. The graph data, the cartoon, the photograph illustration, the infographic layout and the people on the continuum are invented. Facts about government and the three innovations are kept to well-documented points; check names, dates and your class's own examples against your notes. Non-partisan: no real parties or politicians.","footer":"Covers Year 9 social studies exam skills (percentage bar, multiple bar and double line graphs; cartoon, photograph and infographic interpretation; note-taking and summarising; values continuums) and knowledge (Government and Innovation definitions, features of New Zealand's system of government, and two paragraphs on an innovative idea).","mainLabel":"The exam desk: a clock, a graph, sources, a values continuum, the Beehive, an innovation and a paragraph builder","kupu":[{"mi":"manapori","en":"democracy","ta":11071,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kāwanatanga","en":"government","ta":2472,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"Pāremata","en":"Parliament","ta":5295,"note":"New Zealand's Parliament calls itself Pāremata Aotearoa.","chip":[]},{"mi":"pōti","en":"to vote; a vote, an election","ta":5918,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"pire","en":"bill (a proposed law)","ta":13133,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"ture","en":"law, act of Parliament","ta":8768,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"tāpaetanga","en":"submission","ta":38468,"note":"","chip":[]}],"steps":[{"k":"plan","lab":"Plan the exam","cap":"Two hours, two sections. Split your time, then check your clear plastic bag.","exam":"Read the whole paper first and plan your time.","trap":"Running out of time for the two paragraphs: they carry a lot of marks. Plan the minutes before you start.","big":"Section A is skills: graphs, sources, notes and values. Section B is knowledge: definitions, Government and Innovation, and two paragraphs.","kupu":[]},{"k":"graph","lab":"Draw the graph","cap":"Pick the graph type that fits the data, then switch on everything a full-marks graph needs.","trap":"Uneven scales and missing keys lose easy marks. Count your scale steps.","big":"A percentage bar shows parts of one whole. A multiple bar compares two sets of values. A double line shows change over time.","kupu":[]},{"k":"source","lab":"Read the source","cap":"Cartoon, photograph or infographic: look closer at each clue, then state the message.","trap":"Describing the picture without explaining the message gets fewer marks.","big":"Describe what you can see, explain what it means, then give the message and the creator's purpose.","kupu":["pire","ture","tāpaetanga"]},{"k":"continuum","lab":"Values continuum","cap":"Place yourself on the line, then tap a person to see the value behind their view.","trap":"Saying “I agree because I think so” is a reason without a value. Name the value.","big":"A value is a belief about what is important. A perspective is a way of seeing an issue shaped by your values and experiences.","kupu":["pōti"]},{"k":"gov","lab":"Government","cap":"Tap through the parts of New Zealand's system of government.","exam":"New Zealand is a constitutional monarchy and a parliamentary democracy with three branches of government.","trap":"Mixing up Parliament (all MPs, makes laws) with the government (PM and ministers).","big":"Three branches: the legislature makes law, the executive puts it into action, the judiciary interprets it.","kupu":["kāwanatanga","Pāremata","manapori"]},{"k":"inno","lab":"Innovation","cap":"Pick an innovation and look through each lens. For the evaluation, load the balance.","trap":"Listing strengths only. Evaluation means weighing both sides and making a judgement.","big":"An innovation adds value: it solves a problem in a new or better way.","kupu":[]},{"k":"words","lab":"Definitions","cap":"Flash cards for both topics. Say the definition, then flip to check.","trap":"Using the word in its own definition (“democracy is a democratic system”).","big":"A strong definition says what it is in your own words, and can give an example.","kupu":["manapori","kāwanatanga","Pāremata"]},{"k":"para","lab":"Two paragraphs","cap":"Build the two paragraphs one part at a time: the exam's three bullet points, in order.","trap":"Forgetting the person or group who created the idea, or the economic value.","big":"Paragraph 1 describes and explains. Paragraph 2 evaluates: strengths, weaknesses and your judgement.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Seven challenges across both sections. Use the controls, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Show the e-scooter and bike-share data on the right type of graph, with every feature a full-marks graph needs.","check":"()=>{const m=graphMarks();return s.data===\"trips\"&&m.fit&&m.got===m.of}","ok":"Yes: a double line graph shows two things changing over time, with a title, labelled axes, an even scale and a key.","no":"()=>s.data!==\"trips\"?\"Set Data to Scooter and bike trips first.\":!graphMarks().fit?\"Change over time calls for a different graph type.\":\"Something on the checklist is still off. Look at the close-up card.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Show the travel-to-school survey as a full-marks percentage bar graph.","check":"()=>{const m=graphMarks();return s.data===\"travel\"&&s.gtype===\"pbar\"&&m.got===m.of}","ok":"Yes: one bar for one whole, 100% split into parts, with a key.","no":"()=>\"Data: Travel to school; type: percentage bar; then switch on the title, axis labels, even scale and key.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"On the cartoon, find the clue that uses exaggeration.","check":"()=>s.src===\"cartoon\"&&SOURCES.cartoon.clues[s.clue].k===\"exaggeration\"","ok":"Yes: the scroll of promises is far too long, which makes the point that politicians promise a lot.","no":"()=>s.src!==\"cartoon\"?\"Choose the cartoon first.\":\"Move through the clues with Look closer at.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Stand at “unsure” on the continuum and select the person whose view comes from the value of tradition.","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.you)<=.3&&s.sel===\"tom\"","ok":"Yes: Tom values tradition: 18 is when the law treats you as an adult for most things.","no":"()=>Math.abs(s.you)>.3?\"Move yourself to the middle first.\":\"Tap through the people and read their values.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Show the branch of government that interprets the law.","check":"()=>s.feature===\"jud\"","ok":"Yes: the judiciary, the independent courts.","no":"That one doesn't interpret the law. Think about who decides court cases.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Load the jet boat's balance with at least two strengths and two weaknesses.","check":"()=>s.idea===\"jet\"&&s.str>=2&&s.weak>=2","ok":"Yes: a balanced evaluation weighs both sides before making a judgement.","no":"()=>s.idea!==\"jet\"?\"Choose the jet boat first.\":\"Add more to the lighter pan.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Assemble all six parts of the two paragraphs.","check":"()=>s.build===6","ok":"Yes: description, creator, reason, value, strengths, weaknesses and a judgement.","no":"()=>`${s.build} of 6 so far.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"range","id":"minsA","label":"Section A minutes"},{"type":"select","id":"gtype","label":"Graph type"},{"type":"select","id":"data","label":"Data"},{"type":"toggle","id":"gTitle","label":"Title on"},{"type":"toggle","id":"gAxes","label":"Axis labels on"},{"type":"toggle","id":"gEven","label":"Even scale"},{"type":"toggle","id":"gKey","label":"Key on"},{"type":"select","id":"src","label":"Source"},{"type":"range","id":"clue","label":"Look closer at"},{"type":"range","id":"you","label":"Where you stand"},{"type":"select","id":"sel","label":"Person"},{"type":"select","id":"feature","label":"Feature"},{"type":"select","id":"idea","label":"Innovation"},{"type":"select","id":"lens","label":"Lens"},{"type":"range","id":"str","label":"Strengths on the balance"},{"type":"range","id":"weak","label":"Weaknesses on the balance"},{"type":"select","id":"topic","label":"Topic"},{"type":"button","id":"flip","label":"↻ Flip the card"},{"type":"button","id":"next","label":"Next card →"},{"type":"button","id":"known","label":"✓ I knew it"},{"type":"range","id":"build","label":"Parts built"}],"shell":"kit"},"heart":{"id":"heart","title":"Heart Pump Lab","href":"m/heart.html","desc":"Trace two linked pumps, compare heart rate and forward stroke volume, and explain your A/B evidence.","blurb":"Two pumps, one circulation. Explore valves and compare forward output.","tags":["Science","Biology","Circulation","Year 9","Year 10"],"built":true,"shell":"own","kupu":[],"steps":[],"challenges":[],"papers":[]},"inanga":{"id":"inanga","title":"The īnanga machine","href":"m/inanga.html","desc":"An estuary-bank rig: a moon dial drives spring and neap tides, īnanga spawn at the top of a spring tide, the eggs hatch on the next one, larvae grow at sea and whitebait come back past a culvert. Fence, stop mowing, plant and fix the culvert to restore the cycle.","blurb":"Moon, tides and the īnanga life cycle on an estuary bank you can restore.","tags":["Year 7","Year 8","Year 9","Year 10","Science","Ecology"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science · Life cycles and ecosystems","crumb":"Years 7–10 Science · life cycles, ecosystems, human impacts and restoration","badge":{"label":"Years 7–10 Science"},"papers":[],"truth":"A simplified model for learning. From the sources: īnanga spawn in bankside vegetation at the upper edge of spring tides, mainly in late summer and autumn (DOC: January to June; Mountains to Sea Conservation Trust: mostly February to May), generally 1 or 2 days after new or full moon (NIWA); the eggs develop out of the water for 2–4 weeks and hatch on the next spring tide (NIWA; Waikato Regional Council); larvae grow at sea for about six months (NIWA; about 20 weeks, Waikato Regional Council) and return as whitebait in spring; most adults live about a year, some up to three (NIWA; Waikato Regional Council); adults rarely exceed 110 mm and are classed At Risk – Declining (NIWA); spawning habitat is estimated at under 100 ha nationally (DOC). Spring tides come about every two weeks, around new and full moon (Ministry for the Environment; NIWA). The machine's own, illustrative numbers: the tide heights on the staff, the 13 nights its eggs need, the rule that eggs flooded before they're ready are washed out, the 1,000 eggs, the share of eggs that hatch with each bank setting, the 20% of whitebait that get past a perched culvert, and the run index. The rig is not to scale.","footer":"Covers Years 7–10 science: life cycles, habitats and ecosystems, the Moon and tides, human impacts on biodiversity and restoration (the Year 9 and 10 ecosystems topics in the refreshed curriculum). Sources: DOC: find, protect and create īnanga spawning habitat; NIWA: īnanga (Taonga Species Series); NIWA: A guide to restoring inanga habitat; Whitebait Connection: īnanga spawning. Add ?lite to the address for the drawing instead of the 3D model.","mainLabel":"A cut-away of an estuary bank with a moon dial, tide gauge, sea tank and culvert","kupu":[{"mi":"īnanga","en":"inanga, whitebait (Galaxias maculatus)","ta":1724,"note":"Te Aka's entry notes that adults migrate downstream on new or full moons to spawn when spring tides flood the plants at the water's edge.","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":"Te Aka's entry notes that adults migrate downstream on new or full moons to spawn when spring tides flood the plants at the water's edge."},{"mi":"marama","en":"moon","ta":3673,"note":"Also means month.","chip":["moon"],"evidence":"moon","stepsNote":"Also means month."},{"mi":"tai nunui","en":"spring tide","ta":40269,"note":"From Te Reo Pūtaiao (2009).","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":"From Te Reo Pūtaiao (2009)."},{"mi":"tai ririki","en":"neap tide","ta":40270,"note":"From Te Reo Pūtaiao (2009).","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":"From Te Reo Pūtaiao (2009)."},{"mi":"toene","en":"spawn, roe (of fish)","ta":40356,"note":"From Te Reo Pūtaiao (2009).","chip":["eggs"],"evidence":"eggs","stepsNote":"From Te Reo Pūtaiao (2009)."},{"mi":"paopao","en":"to hatch (of eggs)","ta":15278,"note":"","chip":["hatch"],"evidence":"hatch","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"ngutuawa","en":"river mouth, estuary","ta":4687,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"mataora","en":"life cycle","ta":39882,"note":"Also means life, or alive.","chip":["stage"],"evidence":"stage","stepsNote":"Also means life, or alive."},{"mi":"karohe","en":"inanga migrating upstream","ta":41749,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"kaitiakitanga","en":"guardianship, stewardship","ta":2018,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""}],"steps":[{"k":"bank","lab":"The estuary bank","cap":"Whitebait are the young of five native fish, and īnanga are one of them. Adults live in lowland streams, but to breed they need one thin strip of habitat: the grassy bank of an estuary, right where the biggest tides reach. This rig is a cut-away of that bank, with a moon dial, a tide gauge, a sea tank and a stream behind a culvert.","exam":"Īnanga (Galaxias maculatus) live in lowland rivers and streams as adults. They lay their eggs in bankside vegetation in the estuary, at the upper edge of the spring tides, and their larvae grow at sea before coming back up the rivers as whitebait. A life cycle that uses both fresh water and the sea is called diadromous.","trap":"Thinking whitebait is one species. Whitebait are the young of five native galaxiid fish, and īnanga is one of them.","big":"The big idea: īnanga use fresh water and the sea in one life cycle. Each stage needs its own habitat, and the eggs need the smallest one: a strip of bank only as wide as the top of the tide.","kupu":["īnanga","ngutuawa","mataora"]},{"k":"tides","lab":"Moon and tides","cap":"Turn the moon dial, or press Play. The gauge shows each night's highest tide. Around new and full moon the Sun, Earth and Moon line up, their pulls add, and high tides are biggest: spring tides. At first and last quarter they pull at right angles: neap tides.","exam":"Spring tides happen about every two weeks, around new and full moon, when the Earth, Sun and Moon are nearly in line, so the pulls of the Sun and Moon on the ocean add together and high tides reach higher. Neap tides happen near first and last quarter, when the Sun and Moon pull at right angles, so high tides are lower.","trap":"Thinking spring tides only happen in spring. They happen twice every lunar month, all year round.","big":"The big idea: the Moon sets the tides. Spring tides come about every two weeks, a day or so after new and full moon, in every season.","kupu":["marama","tai nunui","tai ririki"]},{"k":"spawn","lab":"Spawning","cap":"In late summer and autumn (mainly February to May), adults swim down to the estuary. On the top of a spring tide they swim into the flooded grass at the very edge of the water, lay their eggs, and leave as the tide drops. Set the dial to a spring tide and press Spawn.","exam":"Īnanga spawn on high spring tides, around new and full moon, mostly in late summer and autumn. They lay their eggs among dense grass and other vegetation at the upper edge of the tide, close to where the salt water reaches furthest up the river. When the tide falls, the eggs are left out of the water in the damp vegetation.","trap":"Saying īnanga lay their eggs in the water. The eggs are laid in vegetation on the bank, and they develop out of the water.","big":"The big idea: the timing puts the eggs as high up the bank as the water ever goes, so ordinary tides leave them alone until the next spring tide.","kupu":["toene","tai nunui"]},{"k":"eggs","lab":"Eggs in the grass","cap":"The eggs stay in the damp grass, out of the water, for two to four weeks. This machine's eggs need 13 nights. Press Play: they hatch when a spring tide reaches them after they're ready, and the larvae are washed out towards the sea.","exam":"The eggs develop out of the water for about 2 to 4 weeks, kept damp and hidden by long grass. When the next spring tide floods the vegetation, the developed eggs hatch and the larvae are carried out to sea. So the Moon, through the spring-tide cycle, sets the night they hatch.","trap":"Thinking the eggs hatch after a set number of days, whatever happens. They hatch when a spring tide reaches them after they've developed.","big":"The big idea: the tide is the trigger. Eggs laid on the top of one spring tide are reached again by the next spring tide, about two weeks later, when they're ready to hatch.","kupu":["paopao","toene"]},{"k":"sea","lab":"Out to sea and back","cap":"The larvae grow at sea for about six months. In spring the young fish, now whitebait, swim back up the rivers to live in streams, if the culvert lets them. Most adults live about a year, and in autumn they head down to the estuary to spawn. Press Play to run the year.","exam":"After hatching, īnanga larvae are carried to the sea, where they feed and grow for about six months. They come back into fresh water in spring as juvenile whitebait, move upstream to adult habitat in lowland streams and mature. Most live for about one year, spawning the following autumn.","trap":"Thinking whitebait and adult īnanga are different fish. They're the same species at different life stages.","big":"The big idea: one life cycle, three habitats. The sea grows the larvae, the streams grow the adults and the estuary bank hatches the eggs. Break any link and the cycle fails.","kupu":["ngutuawa","karohe","mataora"]},{"k":"harm","lab":"Human impacts","cap":"Many spawning banks are on farms or in towns. Stock eat the grass and trample eggs. Mowing leaves eggs in short grass to dry out. Clearing drains, silt from floods, and culverts or floodgates that fish can't get through add more losses. Flip the switches to see what each one does.","exam":"Grazing stock eat bankside vegetation and trample eggs, and mowing removes the long grass that keeps eggs damp, so more eggs dry out or are destroyed. Silt from floods can smother eggs, and clearing drains in the spawning season damages sites. Badly placed culverts, weirs and floodgates stop whitebait swimming upstream to adult habitat.","trap":"Blaming just one thing. Īnanga are harmed at several life-cycle stages. A strong answer links each human impact to the stage it harms.","big":"The big idea: the spawning strip is tiny. DOC estimates there's now less than 100 hectares of it in the whole country, so damage to a thin strip of bank hits the whole population.","kupu":["īnanga","toene"]},{"k":"restore","lab":"Restore the bank","cap":"Kaitiakitanga means guardianship. The whitebait run is an important mahinga kai (food-gathering resource) for Māori around Aotearoa, and caring for it starts at the bank. Fence out the stock, stop mowing, plant natives with thick roots and stems, and fix the culvert. Then lay eggs on a spring tide and play it through.","exam":"Fencing keeps stock from trampling eggs and grazing the bankside grass, leaving the grass long keeps eggs damp and hidden, and planting natives with thick roots and stems gives lasting cover. Fixing culverts lets whitebait reach adult habitat upstream. Each action protects a stage of the life cycle. Caring for the river this way is an example of kaitiakitanga, or guardianship.","trap":"Using kaitiakitanga as just a fancy word for helping nature. It's a Māori concept of guardianship that comes with responsibilities. Explain what it means, and learn from your teacher, or local hapū and iwi, how it's practised where you live.","big":"The big idea: restoration works on the stage that limits the cycle. One study found more spawning on banks fenced from stock for 18 months, where vegetation was 1.5 m tall, than on grazed banks under 0.3 m (Mitchell 1991, in NIWA's guide).","kupu":["kaitiakitanga","īnanga"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Four challenges. The bank starts grazed and mown, with a perched culvert. Use the controls, then press Check. The machine marks you with the same model it runs.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: link the chain. The Moon sets the spring tides, the spring tides set where the eggs are laid and when they hatch, and people decide whether the bank and the river let each stage survive.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Lay eggs on the top of a spring tide, then set Your hatch prediction to the night they'll hatch.","check":"()=>{sync();const e=s.egg;return !!(e&&e.kind===\"hatch\"&&s.guessSet&&s.guess===e.end)}","ok":"()=>`Yes: laid on night ${s.egg.n}, they're ready after ${DEV} nights, and the next spring tide climbs back up to them on night ${s.egg.end} (${phaseName(s.egg.end).toLowerCase()}).`","no":"()=>{const e=s.egg;if(!e)return \"Spawn first: set the moon dial to a spring tide (new or full moon) and press Spawn.\";if(e.kind!==\"hatch\")return \"These eggs won't hatch: an earlier tide floods them before they're ready. Spawn on the biggest tide of a spring series.\";if(!s.guessSet)return \"Move Your hatch prediction to a night, then check.\";return `Not night ${s.guess}. On the tide chart, find the next night the tide climbs back up to the egg line, at least ${DEV} nights after laying.`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Restore the bank so that at least 750 of the 1,000 eggs hatch. Spawn on a spring tide and play it through to hatching.","check":"()=>{sync();return s.lastHatch!=null&&s.lastHatch>=750}","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${nf(s.lastHatch)} of ${nf(EGGS)} hatched. Long grass behind a fence keeps the eggs damp and away from hooves.`","no":"()=>{const e=s.egg,b=bank();if(!e)return \"Lay eggs on the top of a spring tide first.\";if(e.kind!==\"hatch\")return \"Those eggs get flooded before they're ready. Spawn on the top of a spring tide.\";if(s.t<e.end)return \"Press Play and let the eggs reach the next spring tide.\";\n   return `Only ${nf(s.lastHatch||0)} hatched. ${b.grazed?\"Stock are still grazing and trampling the bank.\":b.mown?\"The bank is still mown short, so eggs dry out.\":\"The bank is restored now: lay a new batch and play it through.\"}`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Spawn on a neap tide and play on until those eggs fail. Then choose why they failed.","check":"()=>{sync();return s.neapSeen&&s.why===\"flood\"}","ok":"Yes: a neap tide only reaches low on the bank, below the grass. The tides on the next nights climb higher and flood the eggs before they're ready, so in this machine they're washed out. That's why spawning is timed to the top of a spring tide.","no":"()=>!s.neapSeen?\"First set the dial to a neap tide (around first or last quarter), press Spawn, then Play until the eggs fail.\":\"Not that one. Watch the tide chart: what do the next nights' tides do to the egg line after a neap tide?\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Close the loop: make next autumn's run at least as big as this year's (100% or more).","check":"()=>{sync();return s.lastHatch!=null&&runIdx(s.lastHatch)>=100}","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${nf(s.lastHatch)} eggs hatched and ${Math.round(passage()*100)}% of the returning whitebait get past the culvert, so the run is ${runIdx(s.lastHatch)}% of this year's.`","no":"()=>s.lastHatch==null?\"Get a batch of eggs to hatch first.\":!s.culvert?`The run is only ${runIdx(s.lastHatch)}%: most whitebait can't get up the perched culvert. Fix it.`:`The run is ${runIdx(s.lastHatch)}%. More eggs need to hatch: restore the bank, then spawn and hatch a new batch.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Back to night 0"},{"type":"range","id":"night","label":"Moon dial"},{"type":"button","id":"spawn","label":"Spawn on tonight's tide"},{"type":"button","id":"relay","label":"Lay again on night 1"},{"type":"range","id":"guess","label":"Your hatch prediction"},{"type":"toggle","id":"fence","label":"Fence out stock"},{"type":"toggle","id":"mow","label":"Stop mowing"},{"type":"toggle","id":"plant","label":"Plant natives"},{"type":"toggle","id":"culvert","label":"Fix the culvert"},{"type":"select","id":"why","label":"Why did the neap-tide eggs fail?"},{"type":"range","id":"month","label":"Month"},{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"Start again"}],"shell":"kit"},"reactions":{"id":"reactions","title":"The reaction machine","href":"m/reactions.html","desc":"A rates-of-reaction lab bench: marble chips fizz CO₂ into a gas syringe, a second flask loses mass on a balance, a water bath sets the temperature and a particle window shows the collisions. Concentration, temperature, surface area, a catalyst's energy profile, and an NO₂ equilibrium tube.","blurb":"Collision theory, rate graphs and a catalyst on a real lab bench.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science","NCEA L2 Chemistry 91166"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Chemistry · Rates of reaction","crumb":"Year 9–10 science · NCEA Level 2 Chemistry AS91166 Demonstrate understanding of chemical reactivity","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 Chemistry 91166","exam":"2026-11-16T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Mon 16 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Chemistry past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/chemistry/"},{"label":"2025 paper, AS91166","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nqfdocs/ncea-resource/exams/2025/91166-exm-2025.pdf"},{"label":"2025 marking schedule","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nqfdocs/ncea-resource/schedules/2025/91166-ass-2025.pdf"},{"label":"2024 paper (rate graph question)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nqfdocs/ncea-resource/exams/2024/91166-exm-2024.pdf"}],"truth":"The amounts and equations are real: 0.40 g of marble (CaCO₃, 100.1 g mol⁻¹) with 50 mL of hydrochloric acid in excess, and 25 mL of 0.32 mol L⁻¹ hydrogen peroxide with 0.50 g of manganese(IV) oxide (the amount in RSC Education's practical). Gas volumes are calculated for 20 °C and 101.3 kPa (24.1 L per mole); the balance readings are illustrative. How fast each run goes is illustrative: the rate constants, the activation energies (50 kJ mol⁻¹ for marble; 75 and 50 kJ mol⁻¹ for peroxide without and with the catalyst) and the surface factors for chips and powder were chosen to show the trends, not measured. The model holds each flask at the bath temperature, treats the rate as proportional to acid concentration, and ignores CO₂ that dissolves in the acid. The particle window is a slowed-down sketch that exaggerates the differences so you can see them. For the NO₂ tube, how Kc changes with temperature uses ΔH = −57 kJ mol⁻¹ (Chemguide); the share of NO₂ in the window is illustrative.","footer":"Covers AS91166 rates of reaction: collision theory and activation energy; the effect of concentration, temperature, surface area and a catalyst; energy profiles; reading rate from a graph. Introduces dynamic equilibrium and the effect of temperature on it. Also Year 9–10 science: chemical reactions and reaction rates.","mainLabel":"A lab bench: a conical flask of marble chips and acid in a water bath on a hot plate, a gas syringe on a clamp stand, a second flask on a balance, and a particle window magnifying the chips","kupu":[{"mi":"tauhohe matū","en":"chemical reaction","ta":40051,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"pūmatū hohe","en":"reactant","ta":40182,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"matūriki","en":"particle","ta":50542,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"waikawa pūhaumāota","en":"hydrochloric acid","ta":40475,"note":"","chip":["acid"],"evidence":"acid","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"hauhā","en":"carbon dioxide","ta":38806,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"pāpātanga","en":"rate","ta":45822,"note":"A maths term: a rate compares two quantities, here gas made and time.","chip":["r0","rnow"],"evidence":"r0, rnow","stepsNote":"A maths term: a rate compares two quantities, here gas made and time."},{"mi":"rōnaki","en":"gradient, slope","ta":13240,"note":"Also means to glide or slope.","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":"Also means to glide or slope."},{"mi":"paemahana","en":"temperature","ta":39927,"note":"Te Aka also lists pāmahana.","chip":["temp"],"evidence":"temp","stepsNote":"Te Aka also lists pāmahana."},{"mi":"horahanga mata","en":"surface area","ta":45668,"note":"","chip":["surf"],"evidence":"surf","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"whākōkī","en":"catalyst","ta":40520,"note":"Te Aka also lists whakakōkī.","chip":["cat"],"evidence":"cat","stepsNote":"Te Aka also lists whakakōkī."}],"steps":[{"k":"collide","lab":"Collisions","cap":"Zoom in on a marble chip in acid. Acid particles hit the chip all the time, but only some hits react. A collision needs enough energy and the right way round. Watch the window: a green flash is a successful collision.","exam":"For a reaction to happen, the reactant particles must collide with enough energy (at least the activation energy) and in the correct orientation. This is called a successful collision. The rate of reaction is the number of successful collisions per unit of time.","trap":"Saying particles react whenever they touch. Most collisions bounce off, because they don't have enough energy or they hit the wrong way round.","big":"The big idea: collision theory. Particles must collide to react, and a collision only works if it has at least the activation energy (Ea) and the particles are lined up the right way. The rate of reaction is the number of successful collisions per second.","kupu":["matūriki","tauhohe matū","pūmatū hohe"]},{"k":"measure","lab":"Measuring rate","cap":"Press Play. The chips tip into the acid and CO₂ pushes the syringe out. A second flask sits on a balance with a cotton wool plug: the gas escapes, so the mass drops. Both give a graph that is steep at first, then flattens.","exam":"The gradient of the graph is the rate of reaction. It is steepest at the start because the reactant particles are most concentrated, so there are the most successful collisions per second. As the marble and acid are used up there are fewer reactant particles to collide, so the rate decreases and the curve flattens. It is flat when all the marble has reacted.","trap":"Saying the reaction slows because the gas 'fills up'. It slows because there are fewer reactant particles left to collide, and stops when the limiting reactant (here, the marble) is used up.","big":"The big idea: rate is how fast a product forms or a reactant is used up. On the graph it is the gradient: steepest at the start, flatter as the reactants are used up, and flat once the marble has gone.","kupu":["hauhā","pāpātanga","rōnaki"]},{"k":"conc","lab":"Concentration","cap":"Change the acid concentration. More acid particles in the same volume means more collisions every second. Run one line, change the concentration, and the first run stays on the graph as a dashed line.","exam":"Increasing the concentration of the acid increases the number of acid particles per unit volume. Particles collide more frequently, so there are more successful collisions per second and the rate of reaction increases. The same mass of marble is used, so the same total volume of CO₂ forms; it just forms sooner.","trap":"Saying the particles move faster at a higher concentration. They don't: there are more of them, so collisions happen more often.","big":"The big idea: higher concentration means more particles per unit volume, so collisions happen more often and there are more successful collisions per second. The share of collisions that succeed doesn't change.","kupu":["waikawa pūhaumāota","matūriki"]},{"k":"temp","lab":"Temperature","cap":"Turn the hot plate up, or drop the bath to 10 °C with ice. Hotter particles move faster, so they collide more often, and more of the collisions have enough energy. The energy screen shows the spread of collision energies.","exam":"At a higher temperature the particles have more kinetic energy and move faster. They collide more frequently, and a greater proportion of the collisions have energy equal to or greater than the activation energy. So there are more successful collisions per second and the rate of reaction increases.","trap":"Saying the activation energy gets lower when it's hotter. The activation energy stays the same; more of the particles have enough energy to get over it.","big":"The big idea: temperature works in two ways: more frequent collisions, and a bigger share of collisions with at least the activation energy. The second effect is the bigger one. In this model the rate roughly doubles for every 10 °C rise.","kupu":["paemahana","matūriki"]},{"k":"surface","lab":"Surface area","cap":"The same 0.40 g of marble as large chips, small chips or powder. Tap a dish or use the menu. Only particles on the outside of a piece can be hit by acid, so smaller pieces give the acid more to collide with.","exam":"Powdered marble has a much greater surface area than the same mass of large chips. More calcium carbonate particles are exposed to the acid, so more collisions happen per second, giving more successful collisions per second and a faster rate of reaction. The total volume of CO₂ is the same because the mass of marble is the same.","trap":"Saying powder has 'more particles'. The same mass has the same number of particles; powder has more of them on the surface, where the acid can reach them.","big":"The big idea: cutting a solid into smaller pieces puts more of its particles on the surface. More particles can collide with the acid at once, so there are more successful collisions per second.","kupu":["horahanga mata"]},{"k":"catalyst","lab":"Catalyst","cap":"Now hydrogen peroxide: 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂. On its own it breaks down so slowly you'd wait days for this much oxygen. Press the MnO₂ button (or tap the brown bottle) to add 0.50 g of manganese(IV) oxide, and oxygen pours out. The MnO₂ is still there at the end.","exam":"Manganese(IV) oxide is a catalyst. It provides an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy. A greater proportion of collisions now have energy equal to or greater than the activation energy, so there are more successful collisions per second and the rate of reaction increases. The catalyst is not used up, so 0.50 g of MnO₂ is left at the end.","trap":"Saying a catalyst gives the particles more energy or makes them move faster. It lowers the energy they need, by giving the reaction a different route.","big":"The big idea: a catalyst provides an alternative pathway with a lower activation energy. A greater share of collisions now have enough energy, so the rate increases. The catalyst is not used up.","kupu":["whākōkī","tauhohe matū"]},{"k":"equil","lab":"Equilibrium","cap":"A sealed tube of brown NO₂ dips into the bath. NO₂ molecules pair up to make colourless N₂O₄, and N₂O₄ splits back, both at the same time. Heat the bath and the tube goes darker; cool it and it goes paler.","exam":"2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g) is exothermic in the forward direction. At equilibrium the forward and reverse reactions happen at the same rate, so the concentrations stay constant. Increasing the temperature favours the endothermic reverse reaction, so the position of equilibrium shifts left: more brown NO₂ forms and the colour darkens. Kc gets smaller, because only a change in temperature changes the value of Kc.","trap":"Saying the reactions stop at equilibrium. Both keep going; they just go at the same rate.","big":"The big idea: dynamic equilibrium. In a closed system the forward and reverse reactions keep going at the same rate, so the amounts stay the same. Change the temperature and the equilibrium shifts to oppose the change.","kupu":["tauhohe matū","paemahana"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Four challenges. Set up a run, press Play (or Skip to the end), then press Check. The machine marks you from the same model that runs the bench.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: for every factor, say what changes about the particles, link it to the frequency of successful collisions, then to the rate. When you talk about the total amount of product, name the limiting reactant.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Make the marble chips finish reacting (99%) in under 60 s. You can't use powder.","check":"()=>either(r=>r.rx===\"marble\"&&r.size!==\"powder\"&&r.tEnd!=null&&r.tEnd<60)","ok":"()=>{const r=[s.run,s.ghost].find(r=>r&&r.rx===\"marble\"&&r.size!==\"powder\"&&r.tEnd!=null&&r.tEnd<60);return `Yes: ${runTxt(r)} finished in ${secs(r.tEnd)}. Hotter and more concentrated acid both mean more successful collisions per second.`}","no":"()=>{const r=s.run;return r.rx!==\"marble\"?\"Switch the reaction to marble chips first.\":r.size===\"powder\"?\"No powder for this one: use chips, then change the temperature or the acid.\":r.tEnd!=null?`Not yet: that run took ${secs(r.tEnd)}. Turn up the temperature or the acid concentration, then run it again.`:\"Run it to the end first (Play, or Skip to the end), then Check.\"}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Draw a second line that starts twice as steep as the first, by changing only the acid concentration.","check":"()=>{const a=s.ghost,b=s.run;return !!(a&&b&&b.started&&a.rx===\"marble\"&&b.rx===\"marble\"&&a.T===b.T&&a.size===b.size&&a.c!==b.c&&Math.abs(b.r0/a.r0-2)<.1)}","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${s.ghost.c.toFixed(2)} → ${s.run.c.toFixed(2)} mol L⁻¹ doubles the initial rate, ${rateTxt(s.ghost.r0)} → ${rateTxt(s.run.r0)} mL s⁻¹. Twice the acid particles per unit volume, twice as many collisions per second (in this model).`","no":"()=>{const a=s.ghost,b=s.run;if(!a)return \"Run the reaction for a few seconds first: that becomes the first line.\";if(a.rx!==\"marble\"||b.rx!==\"marble\")return \"Use the marble reaction for both runs.\";if(a.T!==b.T||a.size!==b.size)return \"Change only the concentration: keep the temperature and marble size the same as the first run.\";if(!b.started)return \"Press Play to draw the second line.\";return `Not yet: the new line starts ${n2(b.r0/a.r0)}× as steep. What concentration is double ${a.c.toFixed(2)} mol L⁻¹?`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Prove a factor changes the rate but not the total gas: run to the end twice, changing one factor, so the lines start at different steepness but finish at the same volume.","check":"()=>{const a=s.ghost,b=s.run;if(!(a&&b&&a.complete&&b.complete&&!a.capped&&!b.capped&&a.rx===b.rx))return false;const d=diffs(a,b);return d.length===1&&Math.abs(a.x*VMAX(a.rx)-b.x*VMAX(b.rx))<.5&&Math.abs(Math.log(b.r0/a.r0))>Math.log(1.2)}","ok":"()=>{const d=diffs(s.ghost,s.run)[0];return `Yes: changing the ${FACT_N[d]} changed how fast (${rateTxt(s.ghost.r0)} → ${rateTxt(s.run.r0)} mL s⁻¹ at the start) but both made ${n1(s.run.x*VMAX(s.run.rx))} mL. The amount of ${s.run.rx===\"marble\"?\"marble (the limiting reactant)\":\"hydrogen peroxide\"} sets the total.`}","no":"()=>{const a=s.ghost,b=s.run;if(!a||!a.complete||a.capped)return \"First run the reaction all the way to the end (Skip to the end works).\";if(!b.complete||b.capped)return \"Now change one factor and run the second line all the way to the end.\";if(a.rx!==b.rx)return \"Keep the same reaction for both runs.\";const d=diffs(a,b);if(d.length===0)return \"The last two runs had the same settings. Change exactly one factor, then run again.\";if(d.length>1)return `You changed ${d.map(k=>FACT_N[k]).join(\" and \")}. Change only one, so it's a fair test.`;return \"That change barely altered the rate. Make a bigger change.\"}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Hydrogen peroxide at 20 °C: collect 50 mL of oxygen in under 20 s.","check":"()=>either(r=>r.rx===\"perox\"&&r.T===20&&r.t50!=null&&r.t50<20)","ok":"()=>{const r=[s.run,s.ghost].find(r=>r&&r.rx===\"perox\"&&r.T===20&&r.t50!=null&&r.t50<20);return `Yes: 50 mL in ${secs(r.t50)}. The MnO₂ gives a pathway with a lower activation energy, so far more collisions succeed. It isn't used up.`}","no":"()=>{const r=s.run;return r.rx!==\"perox\"?\"Switch the reaction to hydrogen peroxide.\":r.T!==20?\"Keep the bath at 20 °C for this one.\":!r.cat?\"At 20 °C without help it would take days. What can lower the activation energy?\":\"Run it until 50 mL is collected, then Check.\"}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"New run"},{"type":"button","id":"skip","label":"Skip to the end"},{"type":"select","id":"rx","label":"Reaction"},{"type":"range","id":"T","label":"Temperature"},{"type":"range","id":"c","label":"Acid"},{"type":"select","id":"size","label":"Marble"},{"type":"toggle","id":"cat","label":"MnO₂ catalyst"},{"type":"select","id":"meas","label":"Graph"}],"shell":"kit"},"slushie":{"id":"slushie","title":"The slushie machine","href":"m/slushie.html","desc":"An original countertop slushie machine, cut open on a lab bench: a compressor and refrigerant loop move heat from a sugary drink to the room, a cooling curve stalls while ice forms, sugar lowers the freezing point so you get slush not a block, an auger keeps the crystals small, and an energy-flow diagram balances the books.","blurb":"Freezing, latent heat, sugar and energy flow inside a slushie machine.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science","Energy","NCEA L2 Chemistry 91164"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Chemistry · Changes of state","crumb":"Year 10 science · NCEA Level 2 Chemistry AS91164 Demonstrate understanding of bonding, structure, properties and energy changes","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 Chemistry 91164","exam":"2026-11-16T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Mon 16 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Chemistry past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/chemistry/"},{"label":"2025 paper, AS91164","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nqfdocs/ncea-resource/exams/2025/91164-exm-2025.pdf"},{"label":"2025 marking schedule","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nqfdocs/ncea-resource/schedules/2025/91164-ass-2025.pdf"}],"truth":"The machine is assembly's own design, not a model of any product. Real numbers: freezing (or melting) 1 g of water takes 334 J, and warming 1 g of liquid water by 1 °C takes 4.18 J (Case Western Reserve University); ice takes about 2 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹ (Chemistry LibreTexts). The freezing points of sugar syrup come from a University of Guelph Food Science table (Leighton 1927, after Pickering 1891). Ice is about 9% less dense than liquid water (USGS), because hydrogen bonds hold its molecules in an open structure with hexagonal channels (Chemistry LibreTexts). Heat out = heat in + work for any refrigerator (OpenStax College Physics). The model's own choices: 1 kg of drink starting at 20 °C in a 20 °C room; the syrup is given water's heat capacity; the thermostat holds −3 °C; the powers (120 W compressor, 8 W fan, 12 W auger, about 200 W taken from the drink), the 85% motor efficiency, how fast ice builds up on a still drum, the crystal sizes, and the slush bands (25–55% ice is slush; 70% jams the auger). Real drinks can cool a little below their freezing point before ice first forms; the model leaves this out. The particle window is a flat, slowed-down sketch: real ice is 3D, the sugar is exaggerated, and molecules are not to scale.","footer":"Covers AS91164 energy changes: changes of state, attractions between particles (hydrogen bonding in ice), exothermic and endothermic changes and energy calculations with E = mL. Also Year 10 science: energy transfer and transformation, conservation of energy, energy flow diagrams and percentage efficiency.","mainLabel":"A countertop slushie machine on a lab bench, cut open: compressor, condenser and fan, expansion valve and a cold drum in a clear bowl with an auger, a thermometer probe, lab clock, cooling and energy diagrams, and a particle window","kupu":[{"mi":"tio","en":"ice; freezing","ta":8127,"note":"Te Aka gives ice, freezing cold as its third sense and freezing as its second.","chip":["ice"],"evidence":"ice","stepsNote":"Te Aka gives ice, freezing cold as its third sense and freezing as its second."},{"mi":"whakatio","en":"to freeze","ta":12572,"note":"Te Aka also lists whakatoka, to solidify.","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":"Te Aka also lists whakatoka, to solidify."},{"mi":"rewa","en":"to melt","ta":6746,"note":"Also means to float, or to set off.","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":"Also means to float, or to set off."},{"mi":"tioata","en":"crystal","ta":40349,"note":"","chip":["crystals"],"evidence":"crystals","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"huka","en":"sugar, sucrose","ta":1547,"note":"A loanword.","chip":["sugar"],"evidence":"sugar","stepsNote":"A loanword."},{"mi":"rāpoi ngota","en":"molecule","ta":40165,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"totoka","en":"solid; to become solid","ta":8538,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"paemahana","en":"temperature","ta":39927,"note":"","chip":["temp"],"evidence":"temp","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"pūngao","en":"energy","ta":40075,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"wera","en":"heat","ta":9229,"note":"Also means hot, or to burn.","chip":["qout"],"evidence":"qout","stepsNote":"Also means hot, or to burn."}],"steps":[{"k":"machine","lab":"The machine","cap":"A countertop slushie machine, cut open. Electricity drives a compressor that pumps refrigerant round a sealed loop. In the steel drum inside the bowl, the refrigerant takes heat from the drink. At the back, the condenser and fan give that heat to the room. Tap a part to name it.","exam":"The refrigerant evaporates inside the drum, absorbing heat from the drink, so the drink cools. The compressor squeezes the gas, which raises its temperature. In the condenser the refrigerant condenses and releases heat to the room. The machine transfers heat from the cold drink to the warmer room, using electrical energy to do it.","trap":"Saying the machine puts 'cold' into the drink. Cold isn't something that moves: heat moves out of the drink and into the refrigerant.","big":"The big idea: a slushie machine doesn't add cold. It moves heat. The refrigerant soaks up heat from the drink at the cold drum, the compressor pushes it round to the warm condenser at the back, and the fan blows that heat into the room. Electrical energy keeps the loop going.","kupu":["pūngao","wera"]},{"k":"curve","lab":"Cooling curve","cap":"Pure water this time (0% sugar). Press Play and watch the probe. The temperature falls, then stays flat at 0 °C while ice forms, even though the machine keeps taking heat out. The second screen splits the energy into cooling and freezing.","exam":"While the water freezes, its temperature stays at 0 °C. Heat is still being removed, but this energy is released as attractions (intermolecular forces) form between the water molecules as they join the solid, so the particles don't slow down and the temperature stays the same. Energy for a temperature change is E = mcΔT; energy for a change of state is E = mL, with L = 334 J g⁻¹ for water.","trap":"Thinking the machine has stopped working on the flat part. It is taking heat out just as fast; that energy is released as the ice forms.","big":"The big idea: on a cooling curve, a sloping line means the particles are slowing down, so the temperature falls. The flat part is the change of state. The energy removed there comes from attractions forming as the water freezes, not from the particles slowing, so the temperature stays the same.","kupu":["paemahana","whakatio","pūngao"]},{"k":"bonds","lab":"Particles and bonds","cap":"Look through the particle window at the ice front on the drum. Water molecules in the liquid tumble past each other. As heat is taken away, some lock into the ice lattice, and each one gives out a little energy (the orange flash). Switch the cooling off and watch the ice melt.","exam":"When water freezes, attractions (intermolecular forces) form between the molecules as they settle into a fixed lattice, and energy is released to the surroundings, so freezing is exothermic. Melting is endothermic, because energy must be absorbed to break the attractions between the molecules. In ice these attractions are hydrogen bonds, which hold the molecules in an open lattice, so ice is less dense than liquid water and floats.","trap":"Saying the covalent bonds inside each water molecule break when ice melts. Only the attractions between molecules break; every H₂O stays whole.","big":"The big idea: freezing forms attractions between water molecules, so it is exothermic: energy is released, and the machine carries it away. Melting breaks those attractions, so it is endothermic: energy has to be taken in from the surroundings. In water these attractions are hydrogen bonds. For AS91164 you can call them intermolecular forces; you don't have to name the type.","kupu":["rāpoi ngota","totoka","rewa","tio"]},{"k":"sugar","lab":"Why sugar makes slush","cap":"Sugar gets in the way. Its molecules make it harder for water to join the ice, so a sugary drink starts to freeze below 0 °C. As ice forms, the syrup left behind gets more concentrated and freezes at an even lower temperature. Change the sugar and see what the machine makes at −3 °C.","exam":"Dissolved sugar lowers the freezing point of water, so the drink starts to freeze below 0 °C. Only the water freezes, so as ice forms the remaining solution becomes more concentrated and its freezing point falls further. At a fixed temperature, freezing stops when the syrup's freezing point reaches that temperature, leaving a mixture of ice crystals and syrup.","trap":"Saying the sugar freezes too. The ice crystals are pure water; the sugar stays in the syrup between them.","big":"The big idea: pure water freezes solid at one temperature. Syrup doesn't: as it freezes, the sugar is left in less and less water, so its freezing point keeps dropping. At the machine's −3 °C the drink settles as ice crystals in syrup: slush. Too little sugar and it sets hard; too much and it barely freezes.","kupu":["huka","whakatio","paemahana"]},{"k":"auger","lab":"The auger","cap":"The auger turns round the cold drum and scrapes ice off as soon as it forms, so the crystals stay tiny and the slush stays smooth. Stop the auger and watch: ice builds up on the drum, the crystals grow big, and less heat comes out of the drink.","exam":"Scraping and stirring remove ice from the cold surface as it forms, so many small crystals form instead of a few large ones, which gives a smooth texture. If ice is left to build up on the drum it insulates it, so heat is removed from the drink more slowly.","trap":"Thinking the auger makes the drink colder. Stirring actually adds a little heat; it helps by keeping the cold drum clear and the crystals small.","big":"The big idea: the drum is the coldest place, so ice forms there first. Scraping it off often makes lots of small crystals spread through the syrup. Without scraping, ice coats the drum. Ice conducts heat poorly, so the layer slows the cooling, and the crystals that form grow large. How fast is the model's own.","kupu":["tioata","tio"]},{"k":"energy","lab":"Energy and efficiency","cap":"Follow the energy. Electrical energy comes in at the plug. Heat is taken out of the drink. Both leave the back of the machine as heat. Block the back vents and the condenser can't get rid of its heat, so the cooling slows.","exam":"Energy is transferred and transformed, never used up. The motors transform electrical energy into kinetic energy, and the machine transfers heat from the drink to the room. The heat given out at the back equals the heat taken from the drink plus the electrical energy put in, so the back is warmer than the room. Percentage efficiency = useful energy out ÷ total energy in × 100.","trap":"Thinking the heat at the back comes only from the motor. Most of it is heat that was taken out of the drink.","big":"The big idea: energy is conserved. Heat out the back = heat taken from the drink + the electrical energy used by the compressor and fan. That's why the back is warm. No machine is 100% efficient: some electrical energy is wasted as heat and sound in the motors.","kupu":["pūngao","wera"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Four challenges. Set up a drink, press Play (or Skip 5 min), then press Check. The machine marks you from the same model that runs the bench.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: for a change of state, say what happens to the particles (attractions form or break), whether energy is released or absorbed, and why the temperature stays steady. Give units for every energy.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Find the least sugar that still makes slush. Run a drink until the machine holds it at −3 °C as slush (not too stiff, not jammed), using as little sugar as you can.","check":"()=>{const r=s.run;return r.reached&&!r.jam&&s.auger&&r.shell<30&&outcome(phiSet(r.w))===\"slush\"&&r.w===MIN_SLUSH}","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${MIN_SLUSH}% sugar is the least that makes slush in this model. With less, more of the water freezes at −3 °C, so it gets too stiff (and below ${MIN_NOJAM}% it sets solid). Above about ${MAX_SLUSH}% it stays runny.`","no":"()=>{const r=s.run,o=outcome(phiSet(r.w));if(!s.auger||r.shell>=30)return \"Keep the auger turning: without it you get ice on the drum, not slush.\";if(!r.reached&&!r.jam)return \"Run the drink until the machine reaches −3 °C (Play, or Skip 5 min), then Check.\";\n    if(r.jam||o===\"block\")return `${r.w}% sugar sets solid and jams the auger. Add more sugar.`;if(o===\"stiff\")return `${r.w}% sugar is too stiff: too much of the water freezes. Add a little more sugar.`;\n    if(o===\"slush\")return \"That's slush. Can you make slush with less sugar? Try one step lower.\";return `${r.w}% sugar stays runny at −3 °C. Use less sugar.`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Run a drink with any sugar from 5% to 30% until ice starts to form. Read the temperature where its cooling curve flattens, and set Your reading to it.","check":"()=>{const r=s.run;return r.w>=5&&r.w<=30&&r.tIce!=null&&Math.abs(s.reading-tfOf(r.w))<=.2}","ok":"()=>`Yes: a ${s.run.w}% drink starts to freeze at ${fmtT(tfOf(s.run.w))}. Sugar lowers the freezing point below 0 °C.`","no":"()=>{const r=s.run;if(r.w<5||r.w>30)return \"Use a drink with 5% to 30% sugar.\";if(r.tIce==null)return \"Run it until ice starts to form (the curve turns blue and flattens), then read off the temperature.\";\n    return `Not quite: ${fmtT(s.reading)} isn't where this curve flattens. Find the bend where the line goes from steep to nearly flat.`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Pause when the bowl holds at least 200 g of ice. Use E = mL (L = 334 J g⁻¹) to work out how much energy was removed to freeze that ice, and set Your answer in kJ.","check":"()=>{const r=s.run;return r.mi>=200&&!s.playing&&Math.abs(s.ansE-r.mi*LF/1000)<=1.5}","ok":"()=>{const r=s.run;return `Yes: ${Math.round(r.mi)} g × 334 J g⁻¹ = ${fmtK(r.mi*LF)}. That energy was released as hydrogen bonds formed, and the refrigerant carried it away.`}","no":"()=>{const r=s.run;if(r.mi<200)return `Only ${Math.round(r.mi)} g of ice so far. Keep it running.`;if(s.playing)return \"Pause first, so the ice reading stays still.\";\n    return \"Not yet. E = m × L: grams × J g⁻¹ gives joules; divide by 1000 for kJ.\"}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"The back of the machine is warm. While the compressor is running, pause and set Heat out the back to the power the back gives to the room, in watts.","check":"()=>{const p=powers(s.run);return p.run&&!s.playing&&Math.abs(s.ansW-p.back)<=4}","ok":"()=>{const p=powers(s.run);return `Yes: ${Math.round(p.qc)} W from the drink + ${p.pc+p.pf} W for the compressor and fan = ${Math.round(p.back)} W. Energy is conserved, so the back gives out more heat than the drink loses.`}","no":"()=>{const p=powers(s.run);if(!p.run)return \"The compressor is resting (or switched off). Start a fresh drink, or wait for it to switch back on, then pause.\";if(s.playing)return \"Pause first, so the readings stay still.\";\n    return \"Not yet. Energy in = energy out: add the heat taken from the drink to the electrical power of the compressor and fan.\"}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"fresh","label":"Fresh drink"},{"type":"button","id":"skip","label":"Skip 5 min"},{"type":"range","id":"w","label":"Sugar"},{"type":"toggle","id":"auger","label":"Auger"},{"type":"toggle","id":"comp","label":"Cooling"},{"type":"toggle","id":"vents","label":"Back vents"},{"type":"range","id":"reading","label":"Your reading"},{"type":"range","id":"ansE","label":"Your answer"},{"type":"range","id":"ansW","label":"Heat out the back"}],"shell":"kit"},"climate":{"id":"climate","title":"The climate machine","href":"m/climate.html","desc":"Shine a lamp on a slice of planet, swap ice for ocean, thicken the greenhouse-gas blanket and run four linked carbon tanks. An illustrative model of Earth's energy balance and carbon cycle, with real CO₂ data and Aotearoa's emissions profile.","blurb":"Sunlight, albedo, infrared, greenhouse gases and the carbon cycle on one bench.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science","Climate","NCEA L2 Earth and Space Science 91193"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science · Earth systems and climate","crumb":"Year 9–10 science · Earth systems, the carbon cycle and climate change","badge":{"label":"Years 9–10 · NCEA L2 ESS 91193"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Earth and Space Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/earth-and-space-science/"},{"label":"MfE: Greenhouse Gas Inventory 1990–2024","url":"https://environment.govt.nz/publications/new-zealands-greenhouse-gas-inventory-19902024-snapshot/"},{"label":"NOAA: CO₂ trends","url":"https://gml.noaa.gov/ccgg/trends/"}],"truth":"An illustrative model, not a forecast. The slice is one well-mixed surface under one greenhouse 'blanket': it leaves out convection, evaporation, the deep ocean and clouds trapping infrared, and it is tuned so today's slice settles at 15 °C. The ice tile's feedback is stronger than the real global one (IPCC AR6 puts surface albedo at about 0.35 W/m² per °C), so you can see it. The carbon sinks simply grow with the extra CO₂. Tiles, clouds, blanket thickness, tank sizes and particle numbers are drawn for clarity, not to scale. Sources: sunlight (340 W/m²), about 29% reflected, about 15 °C average and 'more than 30 degrees' of natural greenhouse warming from NASA Earth Observatory; albedo values from Penn State EARTH 103 and CU Boulder MOSAiC, cities vs cropland from Ouyang et al. (2022, Nature Communications); CO₂ forcing (3.93 W/m² for doubling) and the water-vapour feedback (1.30 W/m² per °C) from IPCC AR6 WG1 chapter 7; carbon stores and natural flows from IPCC AR6 WG1 Figure 5.12; fossil and land-use emissions, ocean and land sinks, shares 50/29/21%, sink weakening and CO₂ in 2025 (425.6 ppm; 1 ppm = 2.124 GtC) from the Global Carbon Budget 2025 (Friedlingstein et al., ESSD); Mauna Loa yearly means from NOAA GML and Scripps; New Zealand figures from the Ministry for the Environment's Greenhouse Gas Inventory 1990–2024 Snapshot (April 2026); gas lifetimes and methane sources from He Pou a Rangi Climate Change Commission.","footer":"Covers Years 9–10 science: Earth systems (energy transfers through the atmosphere, hydrosphere, biosphere and lithosphere, and the carbon cycle) and climate change (CO₂ data trends, human sources, mitigation such as reforestation and cutting fossil fuel use). The radiation steps also support NCEA Level 2 Earth and Space Science AS 91193 (reflection, absorption and transmission of visible and infrared radiation, and links to climate change).","mainLabel":"A lamp shining on a slice of planet with eight surface tiles, clouds, a greenhouse-gas blanket and a thermometer, beside four linked carbon tanks","kupu":[{"mi":"āhuarangi","en":"climate","ta":11736,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"kōhauhau","en":"atmosphere","ta":39948,"note":"","chip":["atm"],"evidence":"atm","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"hihi pōkākā","en":"infrared radiation","ta":47296,"note":"","chip":["ir"],"evidence":"ir","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"paemahana","en":"temperature","ta":39927,"note":"Te Aka also lists pāmahana.","chip":["temp"],"evidence":"temp","stepsNote":"Te Aka also lists pāmahana."},{"mi":"tio","en":"ice","ta":8127,"note":"This is the third sense in the Te Aka entry: ice, freezing cold.","chip":["ice"],"evidence":"ice","stepsNote":"This is the third sense in the Te Aka entry: ice, freezing cold."},{"mi":"hauhā","en":"carbon dioxide","ta":38806,"note":"","chip":["co2"],"evidence":"co2","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"mahana haere o te ao","en":"global warming","ta":39815,"note":"The entry's example sentence (from Te Reo Pūtaiao) calls the greenhouse effect te kati mahana.","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":"The entry's example sentence (from Te Reo Pūtaiao) calls the greenhouse effect te kati mahana."},{"mi":"koranehe","en":"fossil fuel","ta":40187,"note":"","chip":["fos"],"evidence":"fos","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"ahotakakame","en":"photosynthesis","ta":38805,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"mewaro","en":"methane","ta":40472,"note":"A loanword.","chip":["meth"],"evidence":"meth","stepsNote":"A loanword."}],"steps":[{"k":"sun","lab":"Sunlight in","cap":"The lamp is the Sun. Averaged over the whole planet, about 340 watts of sunlight arrive on every square metre. Some bounces straight back to space. The rest is absorbed and warms the surface.","exam":"Energy from the Sun arrives mainly as visible light. About 30% of it is reflected back to space by clouds, ice and other bright surfaces. The rest is absorbed by the land, ocean and air, which raises their temperature.","trap":"Saying all the sunlight that reaches Earth warms it. About 30% is reflected straight back to space and never heats anything.","big":"The big idea: energy arrives as sunlight, mostly visible light. Whatever isn't reflected is absorbed, and that absorbed energy is what warms the land, the ocean and the air.","kupu":["āhuarangi","paemahana"]},{"k":"albedo","lab":"Albedo","cap":"Albedo is the share of sunlight a surface reflects. Pick a tile (or tap one on the bench) and change what it's made of. Ice reflects most of the light. Dark ocean absorbs almost all of it.","exam":"Albedo is the fraction of incoming sunlight that a surface reflects. Ice and snow have a high albedo (about 0.7 to 0.9), so they reflect most sunlight, while open ocean has a low albedo (about 0.06) and absorbs most of it. If ice is replaced by ocean, more sunlight is absorbed, so the surface warms.","trap":"Mixing up reflect and absorb. A high albedo means more is reflected and less is absorbed, so there is less warming.","big":"The big idea: light, bright surfaces have a high albedo and absorb less energy; dark surfaces have a low albedo and absorb more. Albedo runs from 0 (absorbs everything) to 1 (reflects everything). Earth as a whole is about 0.3.","kupu":["tio","āhuarangi"]},{"k":"infrared","lab":"Infrared out","cap":"A warm surface gives off energy as infrared, which we can't see. The hotter it is, the more it gives off. Here the greenhouse gases are taken away as a thought experiment. Press Play: the infrared escapes straight to space, and the surface cools until energy out matches energy in.","exam":"The surface absorbs sunlight and radiates energy as infrared. A warmer surface radiates more infrared, so the temperature rises or falls until the energy radiated away equals the energy absorbed. Without greenhouse gases, the infrared would escape straight to space and Earth's surface would be more than 30 °C colder than it is.","trap":"Thinking Earth sends the sunlight itself back out. The surface absorbs mostly visible light and gives off a different kind of radiation, infrared.","big":"The big idea: everything gives off infrared, and the amount rises steeply with temperature (with the fourth power of the absolute temperature). That's why a planet doesn't heat up forever: it settles where energy out equals energy in.","kupu":["hihi pōkākā","paemahana"]},{"k":"blanket","lab":"Greenhouse gases","cap":"Greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide, water vapour and methane let sunlight through but absorb infrared. They give that energy off again in every direction, so some comes back down. Add CO₂ and watch the surface warm until energy out matches energy in again.","exam":"Greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide absorb infrared radiated by the surface and re-emit it in all directions, including back towards the surface, which warms it. Adding more carbon dioxide increases this effect, so the surface warms until it radiates enough infrared to balance the energy it absorbs again.","trap":"Saying greenhouse gases reflect heat like a mirror, or that the greenhouse effect is caused by the ozone hole. The gases absorb infrared and re-emit it.","big":"The big idea: greenhouse gases don't trap heat for good; they slow down how fast infrared escapes to space. Adding more (the enhanced greenhouse effect) means the surface must get warmer before it sends out as much energy as it absorbs.","kupu":["kōhauhau","hauhā","mahana haere o te ao"]},{"k":"carbon","lab":"Carbon stores","cap":"Carbon moves between four stores. Every year plants and the ocean take up some of the carbon dioxide we add. Burning fossil fuels and clearing forest add it faster than those sinks remove it, so the air's store keeps growing. The graph below is real: CO₂ measured at Mauna Loa since 1959.","exam":"Burning fossil fuels releases carbon that was stored underground for millions of years into the atmosphere as carbon dioxide, and clearing forest releases carbon stored in plants and soil. Plants (through photosynthesis) and the ocean take up about half of the extra carbon dioxide, but the rest stays in the air, so the concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere keeps rising.","trap":"Blaming the natural flows because they're bigger. They nearly balance each other; the extra human flow is what tips the balance.","big":"The big idea: the natural flows are huge, about 140 GtC a year into plants and back out and about 80 GtC a year each way between air and ocean, and they nearly balance. The extra flow from fossil fuels and clearing doesn't. From 2015 to 2024 about half of it stayed in the air, 29% went into the ocean and 21% into plants and soil.","kupu":["hauhā","koranehe","ahotakakame","kōhauhau"]},{"k":"feedback","lab":"Feedbacks","cap":"A feedback is when a change causes more change. With the ice–albedo feedback on, add CO₂ and press Play. As the slice warms, the ice tile shrinks, dark ocean shows through, more sunlight is absorbed and it warms even more.","exam":"The ice–albedo feedback is a reinforcing (positive) feedback. Warming melts ice and snow, exposing darker ocean or land with a lower albedo. The darker surface absorbs more sunlight, which causes more warming and more melting.","trap":"Thinking a 'positive' feedback is a good thing. In science it means the change is reinforced, which here means more warming.","big":"The big idea: reinforcing (positive) feedbacks amplify a change. Ice–albedo is one; warmer air holding more water vapour is another (always on in this model). The extra infrared a warmer surface gives off is a balancing feedback. Climate change and natural swings also weakened the carbon sinks, by about 25% on land and 7% in the ocean over 2015 to 2024 (not in this model).","kupu":["tio","mahana haere o te ao","paemahana"]},{"k":"nz","lab":"Aotearoa","cap":"In 2024 New Zealand's gross emissions were 75.8 million tonnes of CO₂-equivalent. About 53% came from agriculture, mostly methane and nitrous oxide from farming. Energy, including transport and electricity, made about 38%.","exam":"In 2024 about 53% of New Zealand's gross greenhouse gas emissions came from agriculture, mainly methane from ruminant animals such as cattle and sheep, and nitrous oxide. Methane is short-lived and breaks down within a couple of decades, while carbon dioxide and nitrous oxide stay in the atmosphere for hundreds of years.","trap":"Saying transport or electricity is New Zealand's biggest source. Agriculture is the largest share of gross emissions; energy, including transport, is second.","big":"The big idea: not every greenhouse gas lasts as long. Methane stays in the air for a couple of decades; carbon dioxide and nitrous oxide stay for hundreds of years. So the mix of gases a country emits changes how its emissions affect warming over time.","kupu":["mewaro","āhuarangi"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Use the controls to answer each one, then press Check. The machine marks you with the same model it runs.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: follow the energy. Say where it comes in (sunlight), what is reflected (albedo), what goes out (infrared) and what greenhouse gases do to it. For carbon, name the stores and the flows between them.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Make the slice reflect at least 40% of the sunlight using only the tiles (keep cloud cover at 40%).","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.cloud-DEF.cloud)<1e-6&&alb()>=0.40","ok":"Yes: ice reflects about 70% of the light that hits it, ocean only about 6%. A brighter surface absorbs less sunlight.","no":"()=>Math.abs(s.cloud-DEF.cloud)>1e-6?\"Put cloud cover back to 40% and use the tiles.\":`Reflecting ${pct(alb())} now. Swap dark tiles for bright ones: which surface has the highest albedo?`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Balance the energy books at a warmer surface: energy in and out within 0.5 W/m², with the surface at 20 °C or more.","check":"()=>s.T>=20&&Math.abs(flows().N)<=0.5","ok":"Balanced: the warmer surface gives off more infrared, until energy out matches energy in again.","no":"()=>{const N=flows().N;return s.T<20?`The surface is ${fmt(s.T,1)} °C. Make the slice absorb more (fewer clouds, darker tiles), then press Play and wait.`:`Warm enough, but in − out is ${sg(N)} W/m². Press Play and let it settle.`}","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Show the ice–albedo feedback: with the feedback on, warm the slice until at least a quarter of the ice has melted.","check":"()=>{const il=iceLeft();return s.fb&&il!=null&&il<=0.75}","ok":"Yes: melting ice uncovered dark ocean, which absorbs more sunlight, which warmed the slice more. A reinforcing feedback.","no":"()=>{const il=iceLeft();return il==null?\"There's no ice left to melt. Set a tile to ice first.\":!s.fb?\"Turn the ice–albedo feedback on first.\":`Ice left: ${pct(il)}. Warm the slice more and press Play.`}","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make the air's carbon go down without changing fossil emissions (keep burning 10 GtC a year).","check":"()=>s.f===DEF.f&&netA()<0&&s.A<=A0+1","ok":"Yes: with no clearing, plenty of planting and capture, the sinks beat the fossil flow. It took the land and capture valves near full. Real forests and capture have limits, which is why cutting fossil emissions matters most.","no":"()=>s.f!==DEF.f?\"Put the fossil fuel valve back to 10 GtC a year.\":s.A>A0+1?`The air holds ${fmt(s.A)} GtC, more than today's ${fmt(A0)}. Press Reset to today and try again.`:`Carbon is still going into the air (${sg(netA())} GtC a year). Use the other valves.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"Reset to today"},{"type":"select","id":"tile","label":"Tile"},{"type":"select","id":"surf","label":"Make it"},{"type":"range","id":"cloud","label":"Cloud cover"},{"type":"toggle","id":"gh","label":"Greenhouse gases"},{"type":"range","id":"co2","label":"CO₂ in the air"},{"type":"toggle","id":"fb","label":"Ice–albedo feedback"},{"type":"range","id":"fossil","label":"Burn fossil fuels"},{"type":"range","id":"clear","label":"Clear forest"},{"type":"range","id":"plant","label":"Plant forest"},{"type":"range","id":"capture","label":"Capture and store"},{"type":"select","id":"nzv","label":"Sort by"}],"shell":"kit"},"shaky":{"id":"shaky","title":"Shaky Isles: a 3D book","href":"m/shaky/","desc":"A 3D pop-up book of extreme Earth events in Aotearoa: the plate boundary, where North Island magma comes from, the Alpine Fault's stick and slip, why Christchurch liquefied, and the Hikurangi tsunami. Every lesson runs predict, test, prove, with an exam ladder.","blurb":"Plates, magma, the Alpine Fault, liquefaction and tsunami in a pop-up book you can play with.","tags":["NCEA L2 ESS 91191","Year 12","Science"],"built":true,"shell":"own","kupu":[],"steps":[],"challenges":[],"papers":[]},"fish":{"id":"fish","title":"The fishing machine","href":"m/fish.html","desc":"A rock in the Hauraki Gulf: speed from a cast, the rod as a lever (moments), why a float floats (density), why the fish isn't where it looks (refraction), and mean vs median on the day's catch.","blurb":"Casts, levers, floating, refraction and mean vs median off a rock in the Hauraki Gulf.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science","Maths"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science and maths · Forces, density, light and statistics","crumb":"Year 9 and 10 Science (forces, density, light) and Maths (rates, statistics)","badge":{"label":"Forces, density, light and data · Years 9–11"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics, Earth and Space Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics-earth-and-space-science/"},{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"},{"label":"Fisheries NZ: fishing rules","url":"https://www.mpi.govt.nz/fishing-aquaculture/recreational-fishing/fishing-rules/"}],"truth":"The cast ignores air resistance and the height of the rod tip, and heights are squashed to fit the screen. The rod is held level and treated as weightless. Densities are typical values; sea water is taken as 1.025 g/cm³ and its refractive index as 1.33. Snapper lengths are random samples, not real catch data. Minimum sizes and daily limits vary by area and change: check the current rules with Fisheries New Zealand before you fish.","footer":"Covers Year 9–10 forces and motion (speed, gravity, moments), matter (density, floating), light (refraction) and statistics (mean, median, range, skew). Leads into NCEA Level 1 science and maths.","mainLabel":"A person fishing from a rock: a cast over the water, a rod as a lever, a float and sinker, a fish under the surface and a measuring board","kupu":[{"mi":"tere","en":"speed","ta":7953,"note":"","chip":["avg"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"cast"},{"mi":"tōpana","en":"force","ta":40375,"note":"","chip":["effort"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"lever"},{"mi":"taumaha","en":"weight","ta":7718,"note":"","chip":["weight"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"lever"},{"mi":"papatipu","en":"mass","ta":5179,"note":"","chip":["mass"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"float"},{"mi":"rōrahi","en":"volume","ta":40040,"note":"","chip":["vol"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"float"},{"mi":"kiato","en":"density","ta":45567,"note":"","chip":["rho"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"float"},{"mi":"mānu","en":"to float","ta":3595,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"float"},{"mi":"totohu","en":"to sink","ta":8537,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"float"}],"steps":[{"k":"cast","lab":"The cast","cap":"Cast the sinker out over the water. Change the angle and how fast it leaves the rod tip, then watch where it lands and how long it takes.","exam":"The sinker travelled 31 m across the water in 2.1 s, so its average horizontal speed was 31 ÷ 2.1 ≈ 15 m/s. Gravity pulls it down the whole time, which is why its path curves. Ignoring air, the longest cast is at 45°.","trap":"Using the launch speed as the average speed. The sinker slows going up and speeds up coming down, so divide the total distance by the total time.","big":"The big idea: average speed = distance ÷ time. Without air, a cast goes furthest at 45°: steeper wastes speed going up, flatter lands too soon.","kupu":["tere"]},{"k":"lever","lab":"The rod is a lever","cap":"Hold a fish on the end of the rod. Your back hand is the pivot, your front hand pushes up, and the fish pulls the tip down. Change the rod, your grip and the fish.","exam":"The fish's weight is W = mg = 2 × 9.8 = 19.6 N acting 2.4 m from the pivot, a moment of 47 N m. My front hand is 0.5 m from the pivot, so it must push with 47 ÷ 0.5 = 94 N to balance it. The rod trades force for distance: the tip moves 4.8 times further than my hand.","trap":"Using the mass (kg) instead of the weight (N). Moments use forces, so multiply the mass by 9.8 first.","big":"The big idea: a moment is a turning effect: force × distance from the pivot. When it balances, clockwise moments = anticlockwise moments.","kupu":["tōpana","taumaha"]},{"k":"float","lab":"Float or sink","cap":"A foam float holds up a lead sinker. Add sinker weight and watch the float ride lower, until the whole rig is denser than sea water and sinks.","exam":"Density = mass ÷ volume. Lead is 11.3 g/cm³, much denser than sea water (1.025 g/cm³), so a sinker alone sinks. The foam float is only 0.05 g/cm³. Together, the float and sinker have an average density below 1.025 g/cm³, so they float. Add too much lead and the average density goes above the water's, and the rig sinks.","trap":"Thinking heavy things always sink. A ship is very heavy but floats, because its average density (including the air inside) is less than water.","big":"The big idea: density = mass ÷ volume. An object floats when its average density is less than the water's. Upthrust equals the weight of the water pushed aside.","kupu":["kiato","papatipu","rōrahi","mānu","totohu"]},{"k":"light","lab":"Where's the fish?","cap":"Look into the water and the fish seems closer to the surface than it really is. Move your eye and change the depth to see why.","exam":"Light from the fish travels from water into air. It speeds up and bends away from the normal at the surface. My brain traces the light back in a straight line, so the fish appears higher than it really is. Looking straight down, a fish 2.0 m deep looks about 2.0 ÷ 1.33 = 1.5 m deep.","trap":"Saying light bends towards the normal as it leaves the water. Into a faster material (air) it bends away; into a slower one (water, glass) it bends towards.","big":"The big idea: refraction. Light changes speed when it crosses from water into air, so it changes direction. Your brain assumes light travels in straight lines.","kupu":[]},{"k":"stats","lab":"The day's catch","cap":"Twenty snapper on the measuring board. The pink line is 30 cm, the minimum size in much of the upper North Island. Catch more samples and compare the mean and the median.","exam":"To find the median of 20 lengths, put them in order and take the mean of the 10th and 11th values. To find the mean, add all 20 lengths and divide by 20. If a few large fish pull the mean above the median, the data is skewed to the right, so the median is the better 'typical' length.","trap":"Finding the middle of the list without ordering it first. Always sort the values, smallest to largest.","big":"The big idea: the mean adds up and shares out; the median is the middle value. A few very big or very small fish pull the mean but barely move the median.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Four challenges. Use the controls, then press Check. The picture follows the last control you used.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: write the formula, put the numbers in with units, then say what it means: \"so the rig floats\".","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Land a cast exactly 40 m out (within 1 m).","check":"()=>Math.abs(range()-40)<1","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(range(),1)} m at ${s.ang}° and ${fmt(s.v,1)} m/s, in ${fmt(flight(),2)} s.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(range(),1)} m. Try 45° first, then change the speed.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Find the heaviest whole-gram sinker that still lets the float float.","check":"()=>floats()&&s.sink===Math.floor((FLOAT_V*RHO_SEA-FLOAT_M)/(1-RHO_SEA/RHO_LEAD))","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${s.sink} g. The rig's average density is ${fmt(avgRho(),3)} g/cm³, just under sea water's 1.025.`","no":"()=>floats()?`It floats with ${s.sink} g, but could you add more?`:`At ${s.sink} g the average density is ${fmt(avgRho(),3)} g/cm³, more than 1.025, so it sinks.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Looking straight down, set the real depth so the fish looks 1.5 m deep.","check":"()=>Math.abs(appStraight()-1.5)<0.03","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.depth,2)} ÷ 1.33 = ${fmt(appStraight(),2)} m.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(s.depth,2)} ÷ 1.33 = ${fmt(appStraight(),2)} m. Real depth = apparent depth × 1.33.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Catch samples until you get one where the median is 30 cm or more but the mean is under 30 cm.","check":"()=>{const S=stats();return S.med>=30&&S.mean<30}","ok":"()=>{const S=stats();return `Yes: median ${fmt(S.med,1)}, mean ${fmt(S.mean,1)}. A few small fish drag the mean down but not the median.`}","no":"()=>{const S=stats();return `This sample: median ${fmt(S.med,1)}, mean ${fmt(S.mean,1)}. Catch another and look for small fish pulling the mean down.`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"range","id":"ang","label":"Cast angle"},{"type":"range","id":"v","label":"Launch speed"},{"type":"range","id":"L","label":"Rod length"},{"type":"range","id":"d","label":"Hands apart"},{"type":"range","id":"m","label":"Fish"},{"type":"range","id":"sink","label":"Sinker"},{"type":"range","id":"depth","label":"Real depth"},{"type":"range","id":"eye","label":"Your eye, sideways from the fish"},{"type":"button","id":"catch","label":"🎣 Catch another 20"}],"shell":"kit"},"read":{"id":"read","title":"The reading machine","href":"m/read.html","desc":"Adaptive silent reading for Years 7–13: pick your worlds, read with a guided line-by-line window, a free timer or one-sentence focus, then answer explained questions with \"Prove it\" evidence while speed and level adapt to you.","blurb":"Adaptive silent reading with “Prove it” evidence questions that level up with you.","tags":["Year 7","Year 8","Year 9","Year 10","NCEA L1–L3","English","Reading","NCEA L1 English 91927","NCEA L3 English 91474","NCEA L2 English 91100"],"built":true,"shell":"own","kupu":[{"mi":"pānui","en":"to read","ta":5085,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"tuhinga","en":"text, writing","ta":8648,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"kupu","en":"word","ta":3275,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"kupu matua","en":"keyword","ta":11923,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"whakarāpopoto","en":"to summarise; summary","ta":9800,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"kaituhi","en":"writer, author","ta":2031,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"}],"steps":[],"challenges":[],"papers":[]},"words":{"id":"words","title":"The language features machine","href":"m/words.html","desc":"A word mixing desk: hear alliteration, assonance and onomatopoeia, overlap the two sides of a comparison, turn up intensity with faders, recolour a scene by tone, then build the exam sentence on a slot machine.","blurb":"A mixing desk for alliteration, assonance, comparison and tone.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","English"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"English · Language features","crumb":"Year 9 and 10 English: reading unfamiliar texts and poems","badge":{"label":"English · Year 9","examText":"Tue 3 Nov","exam":"2026-11-03T09:00:00+13:00"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: English past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"}],"truth":"Every line, passage and poem here is original, written for this machine, and simplified so one feature stands out at a time. Real texts mix features together, and a line can use more than one. The sounds are simple synthesised effects. Check your school's exam guidance for exactly what your teacher expects.","footer":"Covers Year 9–10 English: identifying and explaining language features (simile, metaphor, personification, alliteration, assonance, onomatopoeia, hyperbole, repetition, rhetorical questions, sensory imagery, emotive language and tone) in unfamiliar texts and poems. Leads into NCEA Level 1 English.","mainLabel":"A word mixing desk: words as pads, comparisons as overlapping circles, intensity faders, a scene that changes with tone, and an exam-sentence slot machine","kupu":[{"mi":"kupu","en":"word","ta":3275,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"tone"},{"mi":"kupu whakarite","en":"metaphor, simile, figurative language","ta":3286,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"compare"},{"mi":"orokati tārua","en":"alliteration","ta":46400,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"sound"},{"mi":"kupu ororite","en":"onomatopoeia","ta":46388,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"sound"},{"mi":"huarite","en":"rhyme","ta":46329,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"sound"},{"mi":"tukurua","en":"repetition","ta":8681,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"amp"},{"mi":"kīanga","en":"phrase","ta":2607,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"explain"},{"mi":"rerenga kōrero","en":"sentence","ta":6727,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"explain"}],"steps":[{"k":"sound","lab":"Hear the words","cap":"Each word is a pad on the desk. Pick a channel, press ▶ Play the line and listen for the pattern. Tap any pad to hear one word. Sound only plays when you tap.","exam":"The writer uses alliteration ('Big berms bounced my bike back') to copy the bumpy, thudding rhythm of the wheels slamming into each berm, which makes the reader feel every jolt through the handlebars.","trap":"Calling any repeated letter alliteration. It's about the sound, not the spelling: 'kind cats' is alliteration, but 'cheap cars' isn't. Assonance is vowel sounds, wherever they are in the word.","big":"The big idea: sound features make you hear the writing. Alliteration repeats first consonant sounds, assonance repeats vowel sounds, and onomatopoeia sounds like the noise it names. Always ask: what does the sound copy, or what mood does it make?","kupu":["orokati tārua","kupu ororite","huarite"]},{"k":"compare","lab":"Comparisons","cap":"A comparison puts two things side by side. The overlap is what they share, and that's where the effect comes from. Switch between simile, metaphor and personification.","exam":"The writer uses a simile ('like a stone down a well') to show how steep the drop is and how little control the rider has, which makes the reader feel the stomach-lurching fall and sense there is no turning back.","trap":"Just saying 'it compares the track to a stone'. That only names the feature. Say what they share (straight down, fast, no way back) and why that matters.","big":"The big idea: a simile says one thing is like another; a metaphor says it is another; personification gives something non-human a human action. To explain the effect, name what the two things share.","kupu":["kupu whakarite"]},{"k":"amp","lab":"Turn it up","cap":"Start with a plain sentence. Slide up a channel to add hyperbole, repetition, a rhetorical question or emotive language, and watch the intensity climb.","exam":"The writer uses hyperbole ('practically upside down') to exaggerate the steepness and show how terrifying it looks from the top, which makes the reader understand how huge the drop feels to the rider.","trap":"Calling every question a rhetorical question. If the writer answers it straight away, or expects an answer, it's just a question. A rhetorical question is asked for effect.","big":"The big idea: these features turn up the feeling. Hyperbole exaggerates, repetition hammers an idea home, a rhetorical question pulls the reader in, and emotive language makes them feel. Explain what feeling is being turned up, and why.","kupu":["tukurua"]},{"k":"tone","lab":"Picture and tone","cap":"The same place, three tones. Change the tone and watch the scene and the word choices change. Sensory imagery is underlined by sense; tone words are in bold.","exam":"The writer creates an excited tone through word choices like 'flew' and 'whooped'. Sensory imagery such as 'cold air whipping my face' (touch) helps the reader share the rush and energy of the moment.","trap":"Saying 'the tone is happy' or 'sad' with no proof. Pick a precise tone word (thrilled, uneasy, peaceful) and quote the words that create it.","big":"The big idea: imagery paints the picture; tone colours it. Tone is the writer's attitude, and you prove it with word choices. Name the tone with a precise word (tense, not just 'bad'), then quote the words that create it.","kupu":["kupu"]},{"k":"explain","lab":"Build the sentence","cap":"The exam sentence has four parts. Tap a reel to change it, and push the strength meter as high as it goes. Then try Next line.","exam":"","trap":"Ending with 'which makes the reader want to keep reading'. It could be said about any text, so it shows nothing. Say what this line makes the reader feel, picture or understand.","big":"The big idea: The writer uses [feature] ('quote') to [effect], which makes the reader [feel or understand something]. The feature name gets you started; the effect and the reader's response are where the marks are.","kupu":["rerenga kōrero","kīanga"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"An original poem from your world. Answer each challenge, then press Check. Solved lines light up on the poem.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: in an unfamiliar text, find two or three features, then write one strong sentence for each: feature, short quote, effect, reader. Three precise sentences beat a page of feature-spotting.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Line 1: “At the top, the redwoods hold their breath.” Which feature stands out? Choose…hyperbolemetaphorsimilepersonification","check":"()=>solve(\"s0\",val(\"wc0\")===P_().spot[0][1])","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${FEAT[P_().spot[0][1]].n}, ${FEAT[P_().spot[0][1]].def}.`","no":"()=>val(\"wc0\")?`Not quite. Check the definition: ${FEAT[val(\"wc0\")].n} is ${FEAT[val(\"wc0\")].def}. Does that fit?`:\"Choose a feature first.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Line 4: “Roots rattle, rocks rip at the rubber,” Which feature stands out? Choose…hyperboleassonanceonomatopoeiaalliteration","check":"()=>solve(\"s1\",val(\"wc1\")===P_().spot[1][1])","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${FEAT[P_().spot[1][1]].n}. Now you could explain its effect.`","no":"()=>val(\"wc1\")?`Not quite. Read the line aloud and ask what the words are doing.`:\"Choose a feature first.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Line 5 uses a simile: “blurs like a video on fast-forward”. Pick the best explanation. The writer uses a simile to make the poem more interesting.The writer uses a simile ('blurs like a video on fast-forward'), which is comparing two things using 'like' or 'as'.The writer uses a simile ('blurs like a video on fast-forward') to show how fast the rider is going, so the trees smear past, which makes the reader feel the dizzy, rushing speed of the ride.","check":"()=>solve(\"best\",rval(\"wc2\")===\"c\")","ok":"Yes: it names the feature, quotes briefly, says the effect in this line and how the reader responds.","no":"()=>rval(\"wc2\")?\"Not that one. One option is vague and one just defines the feature. Which one says what this line actually does?\":\"Pick an option first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"This explanation is weak: “The writer uses a metaphor ('My heart is a drum') to show it is good, which makes the reader want to read on.” Which ending fixes it? to make it sound good, which makes the reader enjoy itto show the rider's heart pounding loudly with nerves, which makes the reader hear and feel the rider's fear before the dropto describe it, which makes the reader read on","check":"()=>solve(\"fix\",rval(\"wc3\")===\"b\")","ok":"()=>`Yes. Now the whole sentence works: ${sentence(P_().fix)}`","no":"()=>rval(\"wc3\")?\"That ending is still vague. Look for the one that's specific to this line.\":\"Pick an option first.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"What is the tone of the whole poem? Choose…furiousthrilledboredgloomy","check":"()=>solve(\"tone\",val(\"wc4\")===P_().tone)","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${P_().tone}. In the exam, back it up with two quotes from the poem.`","no":"()=>val(\"wc4\")?\"Not quite. Look at the verbs and the last line: what attitude do they show?\":\"Choose a tone first.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"select","id":"ctx","label":"Your world"},{"type":"select","id":"sch","label":"Channel"},{"type":"button","id":"play","label":"▶ Play the line"},{"type":"toggle","id":"mute","label":"Sound off"},{"type":"select","id":"cch","label":"Feature"},{"type":"toggle","id":"hyper","label":"Hyperbole"},{"type":"toggle","id":"rep","label":"Repetition"},{"type":"toggle","id":"rq","label":"Rhetorical question"},{"type":"toggle","id":"emo","label":"Emotive language"},{"type":"select","id":"tone","label":"Tone"},{"type":"button","id":"next","label":"Next line →"}],"shell":"kit"},"essay":{"id":"essay","title":"The paragraph machine","href":"m/essay.html","desc":"Read an unfamiliar text for purpose, audience and techniques, answer retrieve, infer and explain-the-effect questions, build a TEEL paragraph, sharpen vague words, and plan an essay where every paragraph plugs into the thesis.","blurb":"Retrieve, infer and explain the effect, then build a TEEL paragraph and plan an essay.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","English"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"English · Reading and writing","crumb":"Year 9 and 10 English: unfamiliar texts, paragraphs and essays","badge":{"label":"English · Year 9","examText":"Tue 3 Nov","exam":"2026-11-03T09:00:00+13:00"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: English past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"}],"truth":"The letter and the story are original, written for this machine, and kept short and simple so the skills stand out. TEEL is one common way to structure an analysis paragraph; your teacher may call it PEEL or SEXY and want slightly different parts. Check your school's exam guidance for exactly what your teacher expects.","footer":"Covers Year 9–10 English: reading unfamiliar texts for purpose, audience and techniques; retrieve, infer and explain-the-effect questions; TEEL analysis paragraphs; precise vocabulary; and planning a short essay with a thesis. Leads into NCEA Level 1 English.","mainLabel":"A letter to the editor under three reading lenses, sentence blocks that build a TEEL paragraph, and an essay plan wired together with cables","kupu":[{"mi":"tuhinga","en":"text, writing","ta":8648,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"read, questions"},{"mi":"kaituhi","en":"writer, author","ta":2031,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"read"},{"mi":"kaupapa","en":"topic, theme, purpose","ta":2439,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"read, plan"},{"mi":"kōwae","en":"paragraph","ta":3169,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"teel, plan"},{"mi":"rerenga kōrero","en":"sentence","ta":6727,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"teel"},{"mi":"taunakitanga","en":"evidence","ta":14911,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"teel, sharpen"},{"mi":"whakamārama","en":"explanation","ta":9594,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"teel"},{"mi":"tohutoro","en":"reference (where a quote comes from)","ta":12623,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"sharpen"}],"steps":[{"k":"read","lab":"Read the text","cap":"An unfamiliar text, read three ways. Switch the lens to find the writer's purpose, the audience, and the techniques they use.","exam":"This letter to the editor is written to persuade the local council to build a pump track. Its audience is the council and adult readers of the local paper, which is why the writer stays polite and reasonable: 'We are not asking for a stadium.'","trap":"Saying the audience is 'everyone'. Look for clues: who is the writer asking, and who has the power to say yes?","big":"The big idea: before you answer anything, work out why the text was written, who it's for, and how the writer tries to get what they want. Every answer you write hangs off those three.","kupu":["tuhinga","kaituhi","kaupapa"]},{"k":"questions","lab":"Three kinds of question","cap":"Comprehension questions come in three kinds. Choose one, then tap the sentence in the letter where the answer lives.","exam":"Kids ride in the car park behind the library.","trap":"Writing an inference with no evidence. An infer answer needs your idea, then 'because', then a quote that proves it.","big":"The big idea: retrieve means find it, infer means work it out from clues, and explain the effect means say what the writing does to the reader. Each kind needs a different-shaped answer.","kupu":["tuhinga"]},{"k":"teel","lab":"Build a TEEL paragraph","cap":"Six sentences, but only four belong in an analysis paragraph. Tap them in order: Topic, Evidence, Explain, Link. Tap a numbered one again to take it out.","exam":"The writer persuades the council by making the problem feel urgent and the solution feel easy. The letter asks, 'Is that really the best our town can offer?' This rhetorical question makes adult readers feel a little embarrassed that young people are stuck dodging cars in a car park. Once readers feel responsible for the problem, a simple, affordable pump track seems like the obvious answer.","trap":"Retelling the text instead of analysing it. 'The letter has four paragraphs' describes it; it doesn't explain how the writer persuades.","big":"The big idea: Topic sentence makes your point. Evidence is a short quote. Explain how the quote proves the point. Link back to the question. Personal opinions and retelling don't count as analysis.","kupu":["kōwae","rerenga kōrero","taunakitanga","whakamārama"]},{"k":"sharpen","lab":"Sharpen it","cap":"This paragraph has the right shape but vague words. Tap each highlighted phrase to swap it for something more precise, and fill the meter.","exam":"The writer uses a list of three ('little kids on balance bikes, teenagers learning tricks, parents chatting') to emphasise that a pump track is good for the whole community, not just teenagers. This makes parents picture their own children there and makes the council more likely to say yes.","trap":"Using 'shows' for everything. Choose a verb that says how the writing works: suggests, emphasises, exaggerates, contrasts, challenges.","big":"The big idea: markers reward precise words. Name the exact technique, use a verb that says how (emphasises, suggests, contrasts), and say exactly who feels what.","kupu":["taunakitanga","tohutoro"]},{"k":"plan","lab":"Plan the essay","cap":"An essay is an intro with a thesis, two or three body paragraphs, and a conclusion. Pick a thesis and watch whether the body paragraphs can plug into it.","exam":"In the letter 'Give Us Somewhere to Ride', a Year 10 rider writes to the local paper asking for a pump track. The writer persuades the council by making the problem feel urgent and the solution feel easy and fair.","trap":"A thesis that only announces the topic ('This essay is about…'). A thesis makes a claim your body paragraphs can prove.","big":"The big idea: the thesis is your answer to the essay question in one sentence. Each body paragraph proves one part of it. If a paragraph can't plug into the thesis, either the paragraph or the thesis needs to change.","kupu":["kaupapa","kōwae"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"A new unseen text. Read it twice, then answer each challenge and press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: in the exam, read the questions first, then the text. Underline the evidence as you go, and match the shape of your answer to the kind of question.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Retrieve. What time did the lights come back on? Choose…7:14Ten o'clockMidnightThe next morning","check":"()=>solve(\"c0\",val(\"ec0\")===\"ten\")","ok":"Yes: 'When the lights snapped back on at ten'. Retrieve answers can be short.","no":"()=>val(\"ec0\")?\"Not quite. 7:14 is when the power went off. Look near the end.\":\"Choose an answer first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Infer. How does the family feel when the lights come back on, and what's the best evidence? Relieved: 'the lights snapped back on'Disappointed: 'we all groaned'Scared: 'long, wobbling shadows'","check":"()=>solve(\"c1\",rval(\"ec1\")===\"b\")","ok":"Yes. They groan because the cosy night is over, which the last line confirms: 'the best night in months'.","no":"()=>rval(\"ec1\")?\"Not that one. Find the moment the lights come back on: what do they all do?\":\"Pick an option first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Explain the effect. Which is the best explanation of “the wind shoved at the windows as if it wanted to come in”? The writer uses personification to make the story more interesting.Personification is when you give human qualities to something that isn't human.The writer uses personification ('shoved at the windows') to make the storm seem like an aggressive intruder, which makes the reader feel how safe and cosy the family is inside.","check":"()=>solve(\"c2\",rval(\"ec2\")===\"c\")","ok":"Yes: feature, short quote, effect, and the reader's response.","no":"()=>rval(\"ec2\")?\"Not that one. One is vague and one only defines the feature.\":\"Pick an option first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Topic sentence. For an essay on how the writer shows a power cut bringing a family together, which topic sentence is strongest? In this paragraph I will talk about the power cut.The power went out at 7:14 and they ate pizza.By taking away every screen, the writer leaves the family with nothing to do but enjoy each other's company.","check":"()=>solve(\"c3\",rval(\"ec3\")===\"c\")","ok":"Yes: it makes a point that answers the question, and the rest of the paragraph can prove it.","no":"()=>rval(\"ec3\")?\"Not that one. One announces, one retells. Which makes a point you could prove?\":\"Pick an option first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Precise verb. “The family's groan ___ that they did not want the night to end.” Choose…showssayssuggestsmakes","check":"()=>solve(\"c4\",val(\"ec4\")===\"suggests\")","ok":"Yes: 'suggests' fits an inference. Nobody says it out loud; the groan hints at it.","no":"()=>val(\"ec4\")===\"shows\"?\"'Shows' isn't wrong, but it's vague. Which verb fits something hinted at, not stated?\":val(\"ec4\")?\"That verb doesn't fit the sentence. Try again.\":\"Choose a verb first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Purpose. What is the main purpose of this text? Choose…To persuadeTo informTo entertain and share an experienceTo instruct","check":"()=>solve(\"c5\",val(\"ec5\")===\"ent\")","ok":"Yes: it's a story that entertains and reflects on an experience. Clue: 'It had been the best night in months.'","no":"()=>val(\"ec5\")?\"Not quite. Is the writer asking for something, teaching facts, or telling a story?\":\"Choose an answer first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"select","id":"lens","label":"Lens"},{"type":"select","id":"qt","label":"Question"},{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"↺ Start again"},{"type":"button","id":"showme","label":"Show me"},{"type":"button","id":"dreset","label":"↺ Back to vague"},{"type":"select","id":"thesis","label":"Thesis"},{"type":"select","id":"bodies","label":"Body paragraphs"}],"shell":"kit"},"maps":{"id":"maps","title":"The map machine","href":"m/maps.html","desc":"Read a made-up 1:25 000 map of Kōwhai Bay: compass points and bearings, grid references, scale along a winding bike trail, contours with a cross-section that draws itself, latitude and longitude across Aotearoa, and a climate graph.","blurb":"Bearings, grid references, scale, contours and climate graphs around Kōwhai Bay.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Social studies","Geography"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Social studies and geography · Map and graph skills","crumb":"Year 9–10 social studies: compass, grid references, scale, contours, latitude and longitude, climate graphs","badge":{"label":"Year 9 social studies","exam":"2026-11-02T08:30:00+13:00","examText":"Mon 2 Nov"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA past exams: Social Studies and Geography","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"},{"label":"LINZ: NZ topographic maps","url":"https://www.linz.govt.nz/products-services/maps/new-zealand-topographic-maps"}],"truth":"Kōwhai Bay, its marae, trail, heights and climate graph are made up for practice; the climate figures are example data, not real NIWA records. The Aotearoa coastline is simplified. City positions are rounded city-centre coordinates. Real NZ Topo50 maps are 1:50 000 with 20 m contours and use a different grid (NZTM); this map uses a simple 1:25 000 grid like many school exams. Check your school's exam topics.","footer":"Covers the map and graph skills used across Year 9–10 social studies and geography: direction and bearings, four- and six-figure grid references, scale and distance, contours and cross-sections, latitude and longitude, and climate graphs.","mainLabel":"A made-up topographic map of a coastal valley with a stream, a marae, a town, a mountain-bike trail and fishing rocks; a map of Aotearoa; and a climate graph","kupu":[{"mi":"raki","en":"north","ta":6446,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"compass"},{"mi":"tonga","en":"south","ta":8433,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"compass"},{"mi":"rāwhiti","en":"east","ta":6663,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"compass"},{"mi":"uru","en":"west","ta":8912,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"compass"},{"mi":"kāpehu","en":"compass","ta":2226,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"compass"},{"mi":"mahere","en":"map, plan, chart","ta":3350,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"grid, scale"},{"mi":"āwhata","en":"scale (of a map)","ta":15342,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"scale"},{"mi":"teitei","en":"height, altitude","ta":7895,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"contour"}],"steps":[{"k":"compass","lab":"Which way?","cap":"The compass sits on the place you start from. Tap anywhere on the map to drop a pin, or pick a place, and watch the needle swing round to it.","exam":"Lookout Hill summit is south-west of the marae, on a bearing of 237° and about 2.5 km away. I measured the bearing clockwise from north, starting at the marae.","trap":"Measuring from the wrong place. \"The summit from the marae\" means stand at the marae and look towards the summit. Swap them and the direction flips: north-east becomes south-west.","big":"The big idea: there are 8 compass points, 45° apart. A bearing is the angle measured clockwise from north, always written with three figures: north-east is 045°.","kupu":["raki","tonga","rāwhiti","uru","kāpehu"]},{"k":"grid","lab":"Grid references","cap":"Tap the map to drop a pin. The machine reads its four-figure reference (the square) and its six-figure reference (the exact spot, to the nearest 100 m).","exam":"The marae is in grid square 2342. Its six-figure grid reference is 238 429: easting 238 (along the corridor) then northing 429 (up the stairs).","trap":"Reading the northing first, or using the lines at the top or right of the square. Always use the easting line on the left and the northing line along the bottom of the square.","big":"The big idea: eastings first, then northings: \"along the corridor, then up the stairs\". A four-figure reference names a 1 km square; a six-figure one adds tenths to pin a point within 100 m.","kupu":["mahere"]},{"k":"scale","lab":"Scale and distance","cap":"Ride the mountain-bike trail from the car park (P) to the summit. The ruler only measures straight lines; a piece of string follows every bend.","exam":"The trail from the car park to the summit is about 4.4 km long, but the straight-line distance is only 3.2 km. I laid string along the trail (17.5 cm on the 1:25 000 map) and multiplied by 250 m per cm.","trap":"Measuring a winding road or river with a straight ruler. That gives the straight-line distance, which is always shorter. Follow the bends with string or the edge of a piece of paper.","big":"The big idea: scale links the map to real life. On a 1:25 000 map, 1 cm stands for 25 000 cm (250 m), so 4 cm is 1 km.","kupu":["āwhata","mahere"]},{"k":"contour","lab":"Contours and height","cap":"Contour lines join places at the same height. Drag A and B anywhere on the map, or pick a line, and the cross-section draws itself in the close-up.","exam":"Contour lines join points of equal height above sea level. On the slope above the stream they are close together, so the land is steep; on the valley floor they are far apart, so it is gentle. The cross-section shows a valley, with the stream at its lowest point.","trap":"Thinking close contours mean high land. Close means steep. To find the height, read the numbers on the lines and count up or down in 50 m steps.","big":"The big idea: close-together contours mean steep land; far-apart contours mean gentle land. A cross-section is the side view you'd see if you sliced the land along a line.","kupu":["teitei"]},{"k":"latlong","lab":"Where on Earth?","cap":"Pick a city or slide the crosshair. Latitude says how far south of the equator; longitude says how far east of Greenwich.","exam":"Tāmaki Makaurau Auckland is at about 36.85°S, 174.76°E (36°51′S, 174°46′E). It is 36.85° south of the equator and 174.76° east of the prime meridian at Greenwich. Ōtautahi Christchurch, at 43.53°S, is further from the equator.","trap":"Swapping them. Latitude lines run across the map (east–west), but they measure how far north or south you are. And don't forget the S and E.","big":"The big idea: latitude and longitude give any place on Earth an exact address. Say latitude first, then longitude, with N/S and E/W.","kupu":[]},{"k":"climate","lab":"Climate graph","cap":"A year of weather in Kōwhai Bay. Bars show rainfall (left axis), the line shows temperature (right axis). Tap a month or slide through the year.","exam":"Kōwhai Bay is warmest in February (20.0 °C) and coolest in July (11.2 °C), a temperature range of 8.8 °C. Rainfall is highest in winter, peaking in July at 140 mm, and lowest in January (70 mm). It gets about 1,215 mm of rain a year.","trap":"Reading temperature off the rainfall axis. Bars belong to the left axis (mm), the line to the right axis (°C). And in Aotearoa, July is the middle of winter.","big":"The big idea: a climate graph shows average temperature and rainfall for each month. Describe the pattern, quote numbers with units, and point out any exception.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Six challenges. Choose a map layer, use the controls or tap the map, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: always give units and the hemisphere letters, say where you measured from, and quote numbers from the map or graph to back up what you say.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Drop a pin on the marae (Grid references layer). What's its six-figure grid reference?","check":"()=>Math.hypot(s.pin.X-M.X,s.pin.Y-M.Y)<0.15","ok":"()=>`Yes: the marae is at ${grid6(s.pin)}, in square ${grid4(s.pin)}.`","no":"()=>`Your pin is at ${grid6(s.pin)}. The marae is the little house with the pink roof, north of the stream.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Drop a pin that is south-east of Lookout Hill summit and at least 1 km from it.","check":"()=>{const b=bearing(LK,s.pin);return b>=112.5&&b<157.5&&Math.hypot(s.pin.X-LK.X,s.pin.Y-LK.Y)>=1}","ok":"()=>`Yes: bearing ${b3(bearing(LK,s.pin))} from the summit, ${fmt(Math.hypot(s.pin.X-LK.X,s.pin.Y-LK.Y),1)} km away.`","no":"()=>{const b=bearing(LK,s.pin);return `From the summit your pin is ${dir8(b)} (${b3(b)}), ${fmt(Math.hypot(s.pin.X-LK.X,s.pin.Y-LK.Y),1)} km away. South-east is between 112.5° and 157.5°. Tip: set From to summit.`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Ride exactly 3.0 km along the trail (within 0.1 km).","check":"()=>Math.abs(Math.min(s.trail,TRL)-3)<=0.1","ok":"Yes: 3 km along the trail. On a 1:25 000 map that's 12 cm of string.","no":"()=>`You're at ${fmt(Math.min(s.trail,TRL),2)} km. Use the Along the trail slider.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Drag A or B so the cross-section goes over the top of Lookout Hill (above 400 m).","check":"()=>profile().P.some(p=>p.h>400)","ok":"()=>`Yes: the highest point on your line is ${Math.round(Math.max(...profile().P.map(p=>p.h)))} m. The summit is inside the 400 m contour.`","no":"()=>`The highest point on your line is ${Math.round(Math.max(...profile().P.map(p=>p.h)))} m. Find the ring of contours with the ▲ inside it.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the crosshair within 0.1° of 45.87°S, 170.50°E. Which city is it?","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.lat-45.87)<=0.1&&Math.abs(s.lon-170.5)<=0.1","ok":"Yes: that's Ōtepoti Dunedin, about 2.3° further south than Ōtautahi Christchurch.","no":"()=>`You're at ${fmt(s.lat,2)}°S, ${fmt(s.lon,2)}°E. Latitude first: slide down to 45.87°S.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the climate graph to Kōwhai Bay's coldest month.","check":"()=>s.month===TEMP.indexOf(TMIN)","ok":"Yes: July, 11.2 °C. It's midwinter in the southern hemisphere.","no":"()=>`${MONL[s.month]} averages ${fmt(TEMP[s.month],1)} °C. Look for the lowest point on the line.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"select","id":"from","label":"From"},{"type":"select","id":"to","label":"To"},{"type":"button","id":"pinat","label":"📍 Pin the marae"},{"type":"range","id":"trail","label":"Along the trail"},{"type":"select","id":"sec","label":"Cross-section"},{"type":"button","id":"redraw","label":"✏️ Draw it again"},{"type":"select","id":"city","label":"City"},{"type":"range","id":"lat","label":"Latitude"},{"type":"range","id":"lon","label":"Longitude"},{"type":"range","id":"month","label":"Month"},{"type":"select","id":"view","label":"Map layer"}],"shell":"kit"},"fly":{"id":"fly","title":"The fly-over machine","href":"m/fly.html","desc":"Fly a toy plane over Aotearoa on real LINZ terrain: Aoraki and the Alpine Fault, the Taupō caldera, Rotorua's geothermal field, the Wellington Fault, Auckland's volcanic cones, the Ōtautahi red zone and Waitangi. Each landmark card has a geography prompt on plate boundaries, volcanic landforms or urban change, and a Fly button tours them all.","blurb":"A tilt-shift fly-over of Aotearoa: plate boundary, volcanoes and city change, card by card.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Year 11","Year 12","Year 13","Social studies","Geography","NCEA L1 Geography 91934","NCEA L2 Geography 91240"],"built":true,"shell":"own","kupu":[],"steps":[],"challenges":[],"papers":[]},"market":{"id":"market","title":"The market machine","href":"m/market.html","desc":"Sort needs from wants in a vending machine, find your opportunity cost, run an ice-block stall at the school fair, shift supply and demand until the price settles, then weigh up perspectives on a bike park.","blurb":"Needs and wants, opportunity cost, and supply and demand at the school fair.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Social studies"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Social studies · Economics and social inquiry","crumb":"Year 9–10 social studies: needs and wants, scarcity, opportunity cost, producers and consumers, supply and demand, perspectives and values","badge":{"label":"Year 9 social studies","exam":"2026-11-02T08:30:00+13:00","examText":"Mon 2 Nov"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA past exams: Social Studies and Geography","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"}],"truth":"The vending machine, prices, the stall's demand, supply and costs, the people and the Kōwhai Reserve bike-park issue are all made up for practice; real markets are messier. The people are fictional and don't speak for any real group. Check your school's exam topics.","footer":"Covers Year 9–10 social studies economics (needs and wants, scarcity, choices and opportunity cost, producers and consumers, supply and demand) and social inquiry (focus questions, sources, perspectives and values). Non-partisan: no real parties or politicians.","mainLabel":"A pastel vending machine, an ice-block stall at a school fair, a supply and demand graph and a values continuum with seven people","kupu":[{"mi":"ōhanga","en":"economy","ta":4704,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"matea","en":"needs","ta":3846,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"needs"},{"mi":"hiahia","en":"want, desire","ta":1132,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"needs"},{"mi":"ngōuruuru","en":"scarcity, shortage","ta":4681,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"choice"},{"mi":"utu","en":"price, cost","ta":8937,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"choice, market"},{"mi":"kaiwhakaputa","en":"producer","ta":45702,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"stall"},{"mi":"kiritaki","en":"consumer, customer","ta":11034,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"stall"},{"mi":"pakihi","en":"business","ta":5016,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"stall"}],"steps":[{"k":"needs","lab":"Needs and wants","cap":"Tap each item in the vending machine to sort it: tap once for NEED, twice for WANT. Sort all eight and the machine marks your sort.","exam":"Needs are things people must have to survive and stay healthy, such as food, water, clothing, shelter and health care. Wants are things that make life more enjoyable but aren't essential, such as bubble tea or headphones. A rain jacket is a need because clothing protects us from the weather.","trap":"Calling something a need just because you really, really want it. Ask: could I survive and stay healthy without it? If yes, it's a want.","big":"The big idea: needs are what people must have to survive and stay well: food, water, clothing, shelter and health care. Wants make life more enjoyable but aren't essential.","kupu":["matea","hiahia"]},{"k":"choice","lab":"Scarcity and choice","cap":"You have limited money. Tap items in the order you want them: 1st, 2nd, 3rd… The machine buys down your list while the money lasts. The first thing you miss out on is your opportunity cost.","exam":"Scarcity means our wants are unlimited but our resources, like money and time, are limited, so we have to make choices. Opportunity cost is the next-best choice we give up. With $20 I bought bubble tea, game credit and lollies, so my opportunity cost was the sandwich, my next-best choice, which I couldn't afford.","trap":"Listing everything you didn't buy as the opportunity cost. It's only the single next-best thing you gave up.","big":"The big idea: scarcity means wants are unlimited but resources (money, time, materials) are limited, so we must choose. Every choice has an opportunity cost: the next-best thing you give up.","kupu":["ngōuruuru","utu"]},{"k":"stall","lab":"Producer, consumer","cap":"You run the ice-block stall at the school fair. You're the producer; the students buying are consumers. Change your price and watch the queue, the money and the profit.","exam":"A producer makes goods or provides services, and a consumer buys and uses them. At the stall I am the producer and the students are consumers: ice blocks go from me to them, and money comes back to me. When I buy fruit to make the ice blocks, I am a consumer too. If I raise my price too much, fewer consumers buy, so my profit falls.","trap":"Thinking a higher price always means more profit. Too high and customers stop buying; too low and you barely cover your costs.","big":"The big idea: producers make goods or provide services; consumers buy and use them. Goods and services flow one way, money flows the other. Most people are both.","kupu":["kaiwhakaputa","kiritaki","pakihi"]},{"k":"market","lab":"Supply and demand","cap":"Slide the price and watch for a shortage (a queue) or a surplus (melting leftovers). Then make something happen, a hot day or a fruit price rise, and let the price settle.","exam":"On a hot day the demand for ice blocks increases, so the demand curve shifts right. At the old price of $1.75 there is a shortage, because more people want ice blocks than the stall makes. The price rises until the quantity demanded equals the quantity supplied, at a new equilibrium of $2.00, with 60 sold an hour.","trap":"Mixing up a movement and a shift. A change in the ice block's own price moves you along a curve; something else changing (weather, costs) shifts the whole curve.","big":"The big idea: when the price rises, people want to buy less (demand) but sellers want to make more (supply). The equilibrium price is where they match: no shortage, no surplus.","kupu":["utu"]},{"k":"inquiry","lab":"Social inquiry","cap":"A made-up issue: should Kōwhai Reserve get a mountain-bike park? Tap each person to hear their perspective, then slide yourself onto the values continuum. The close-up has focus questions and sources.","exam":"Grace is against the bike park because she values protecting native species: she thinks new trails and noise would disturb the kererū and tūī nesting in the reserve. Sam supports it because he values young people's health and wellbeing, and says there is nowhere safe to ride nearby. They disagree because they hold different values.","trap":"Describing what someone thinks without the value behind it. Say who, what they think, and why: \"… because they value …\".","big":"The big idea: a perspective is a way of seeing an issue, shaped by values: what people believe is important. People can share the facts and still disagree because their values differ.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Five challenges. Choose a scene, use the controls or tap the picture, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: for cause and effect, chain it: \"because … this means … as a result …\". For perspectives, always add the value: \"… because they value …\".","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Sort all eight items correctly into needs and wants.","check":"()=>{const sc=sortScore();return sc.done===8&&sc.right===8}","ok":"Yes: food, water, clothing and health care are needs; the rest are wants.","no":"()=>{const sc=sortScore();return sc.done<8?`You've sorted ${sc.done} of 8. Choose the Needs and wants scene and tap each item.`:`${sc.right} of 8 right. Tap the ✗ items to move them.`}","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Rank at least three items, then set your wallet so your opportunity cost is your number 2 choice.","check":"()=>{const sh=shop();return s.rank.length>=3&&sh.oc===s.rank[1]}","ok":"()=>`Yes: you gave up the ${ITEMS[shop().oc].n.toLowerCase()}, your next-best choice after the ${ITEMS[s.rank[0]].n.toLowerCase()}.`","no":"()=>{const sh=shop();return s.rank.length<3?\"Rank at least three items first (Scarcity and choice scene).\":`Right now your opportunity cost is ${sh.oc>=0?\"the \"+ITEMS[sh.oc].n.toLowerCase():\"nothing\"}. You want enough money for your 1st choice but not your 2nd.`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the stall price that makes the most profit.","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.sp-1.75)<0.01","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${$$(s.sp)} gives ${$$(profit(s.sp))} an hour. Cheaper sells more but earns less on each; dearer scares buyers off.`","no":"()=>`At ${$$(s.sp)} you make ${$$(profit(s.sp))} an hour. Look for the top of the profit curve.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"It's a hot day AND fruit prices rise. Set the price where there's no shortage and no surplus.","check":"()=>s.dS===1&&s.sS===-1&&Math.abs(Qd(s.P)-Qs(s.P))<0.5","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${$$(s.P)}, ${fmt(Qd(s.P))} an hour. Both changes push the price up.`","no":"()=>s.dS!==1||s.sS!==-1?\"First set Demand to a hot day and Supply to fruit prices rise.\":`At ${$$(s.P)} there's a ${Qd(s.P)>Qs(s.P)?\"shortage\":\"surplus\"} of ${fmt(Math.abs(Qd(s.P)-Qs(s.P)))}. Try “Let the price settle”.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"In the social inquiry, tap a person who is against the bike park. What value is behind their view?","check":"()=>{const P=PEOPLE.find(p=>p.id===s.sel);return !!P&&P.st<-0.6}","ok":"()=>{const P=PEOPLE.find(p=>p.id===s.sel);return `Yes: ${P.n} is against it because ${P.because}.`}","no":"()=>{const P=PEOPLE.find(p=>p.id===s.sel);return P?`${P.n.split(\",\")[0]} is ${P.st>0.6?\"for it\":\"unsure\"}. Look at the left (against) end of the continuum.`:\"Tap a person first.\"}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"resetSort","label":"↺ Empty the bins"},{"type":"range","id":"budget","label":"Wallet"},{"type":"button","id":"clear","label":"↺ Clear my ranking"},{"type":"range","id":"sp","label":"Stall price"},{"type":"range","id":"P","label":"Market price"},{"type":"select","id":"dS","label":"Demand"},{"type":"select","id":"sS","label":"Supply"},{"type":"button","id":"settle","label":"⚖️ Let the price settle"},{"type":"range","id":"you","label":"Where you stand"},{"type":"select","id":"tool","label":"Close-up"},{"type":"select","id":"view","label":"Scene"}],"shell":"kit"},"claw":{"id":"claw","title":"The claw machine","href":"m/claw.html","desc":"A pastel arcade claw machine: relative frequency, the long run, two-step tree diagrams, expected value (is it worth $2?) and spotting a machine that pays out on a schedule.","blurb":"Relative frequency, tree diagrams and expected value: is it worth $2?","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Maths","Probability","Year 7","Year 8"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Maths · Probability and statistics","crumb":"Years 9 and 10 Maths (probability, relative frequency, expected value) and NCEA Level 1 ideas","badge":{"label":"Probability · Years 9–11"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"}],"truth":"A probability experiment with chances you set. Claw motion illustrates the sampled grab/hold outcome; it does not calculate contact forces, friction or physical skill. Scheduled mode is an idealised comparison, not a model of a specific commercial machine. Reset repeats a seeded sequence. Changing settings starts a fresh run. No real money is involved.","footer":"Covers Years 9–10 probability (experimental and theoretical probability, relative frequency, independent events, tree diagrams, expected value) leading into NCEA Level 1 statistics and probability.","mainLabel":"A detailed lilac claw machine with chrome linkage and pastel prize capsules","kupu":[{"mi":"tūponotanga","en":"probability, chance","ta":46069,"note":"","chip":["p"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"long, tree, value"},{"mi":"whakamātau tūponotanga","en":"probability experiment","ta":46107,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"play"},{"mi":"pāpono","en":"event (in probability)","ta":45828,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"tree"},{"mi":"putanga","en":"outcome","ta":6346,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"play, tree"},{"mi":"hoahoa rākau","en":"tree diagram","ta":45657,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"tree"},{"mi":"raraunga","en":"data","ta":6555,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"play, long, rigged"},{"mi":"tauanga","en":"statistics","ta":44587,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"rigged"},{"mi":"uara","en":"value (maths)","ta":8853,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"value"}],"steps":[{"k":"play","lab":"Have a go","cap":"Press Play once to drop the claw, or play 10 at once. Count your prizes. Is it as easy as it looks?","exam":"Relative frequency is an estimate of probability from an experiment: wins ÷ total plays. With a small number of trials it can be far from the true probability.","trap":"Treating a few lucky wins as proof the machine is generous. Small samples vary a lot.","big":"The big idea: relative frequency = number of wins ÷ number of plays. With only a few plays it jumps around a lot.","kupu":["whakamātau tūponotanga","putanga","raraunga"]},{"k":"long","lab":"The long run","cap":"Compare a short run with a longer one. The coral line is your relative frequency; the dashed line is the probability in this model.","exam":"As the number of trials increases, the relative frequency tends to get closer to the theoretical probability. After 1,000 plays the estimate is far more reliable than after 10.","trap":"Thinking a win is 'due' after lots of losses. Each play is independent.","big":"The big idea: over many independent trials, relative frequency tends towards the theoretical probability. It need not get closer after every trial.","kupu":["tūponotanga","raraunga"]},{"k":"tree","lab":"Two steps to win","cap":"To win, the claw has to grab the prize AND hold on all the way to the chute. Change each chance and see the tree diagram multiply them.","exam":"Winning needs a grab, followed by a successful hold. Multiply the grab probability by the conditional hold probability. At 0.60 and 0.25, P(win) = 0.15: 15 wins per 100 trials on average.","trap":"Adding the two chances. Follow the win branch and multiply the grab chance by the hold chance given a grab.","big":"The big idea: P(win) = P(grab) × P(hold given grab). Both stages must succeed.","kupu":["pāpono","putanga","hoahoa rākau","tūponotanga"]},{"k":"value","lab":"Is it worth it?","cap":"Each play costs money and each prize is worth something. The expected value tells you what you win or lose per play on average.","trap":"Comparing the prize value with one play's cost. Compare it with the cost of all the plays it takes to win.","big":"The big idea: expected value = P(win) × prize value − cost. If it's negative, you lose money on average the more you play.","kupu":["uara","tūponotanga"]},{"k":"rigged","lab":"Random or rigged?","cap":"Compare independent random trials with an idealised schedule: exactly one win every N plays. Watch the last 40 outcomes. This does not represent the controls of a particular real machine.","exam":"In a random process, wins are scattered unpredictably and each play is independent. If wins happen at regular intervals (every 12th play), the outcomes are not independent, and the probability changes depending on how many plays since the last win.","trap":"Assuming every game of chance is fair and random. Check the data for patterns.","big":"The big idea: a random process has no memory. A machine set to pay out on a schedule does: the pattern gives it away in the data.","kupu":["tauanga","raraunga"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Three challenges. Use the controls, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: show the calculation: P(A) × P(B) for 'and', wins ÷ plays for relative frequency, and P × value − cost for expected value.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Set the chances so P(win) is exactly 0.2.","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.pGrab*s.pHold-0.2)<0.001&&s.mode===\"random\"","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.pGrab,2)} × ${fmt(s.pHold,2)} = 0.2.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(s.pGrab,2)} × ${fmt(s.pHold,2)} = ${fmt(s.pGrab*s.pHold,3)}. Try 0.8 × 0.25 or 0.4 × 0.5.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"With $2 plays and P(win) = 0.15, what's the smallest whole-dollar prize value that makes the expected value zero or better? Set it.","check":"()=>s.cost===2&&Math.abs(pWin()-0.15)<0.001&&s.prize===14","ok":"Yes: 0.15 × $14 = $2.10, which beats the $2 cost. $13 gives $1.95.","no":"()=>s.cost!==2||Math.abs(pWin()-0.15)>0.001?\"Set $2 a play and P(win) = 0.15 (0.6 and 0.25) first.\":`Not yet: 0.15 × $${s.prize} = $${fmt(0.15*s.prize,2)}. You need at least $2.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Play at least 1,000 times and get a relative frequency within 0.02 of P(win).","check":"()=>s.plays>=1000&&Math.abs(s.wins/s.plays-pWin())<=0.02","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.wins/s.plays,3)} vs ${fmt(pWin(),3)} after ${fmt(s.plays)} plays.`","no":"()=>s.plays<1000?`Only ${fmt(s.plays)} plays so far.`:`Off by ${fmt(Math.abs(s.wins/s.plays-pWin()),3)}. Reset and try again; more plays help.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"button","id":"play1","label":"▶ Play once"},{"type":"button","id":"play10","label":"Play 10"},{"type":"button","id":"play100","label":"Play 100"},{"type":"button","id":"play1000","label":"Play 1,000"},{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"Reset"},{"type":"range","id":"pGrab","label":"P(claw grabs it)"},{"type":"range","id":"pHold","label":"P(hold given grab)"},{"type":"range","id":"cost","label":"Cost a play"},{"type":"range","id":"prize","label":"Prize worth"},{"type":"select","id":"mode","label":"Machine"},{"type":"range","id":"every","label":"Win every"}],"shell":"kit"},"ai":{"id":"ai","title":"Train your own AI","href":"m/ai.html","desc":"Draw two kinds of thing and teach a real machine-learning model to tell them apart. Test it fairly, then find and fix its bias. Runs on your device; nothing is sent anywhere.","blurb":"Teach a real machine-learning model, test it fairly, then fix its bias. Runs on your device.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Digital technologies","AI"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Digital technologies · Artificial intelligence","crumb":"Years 9–10 Technology and Digital Technologies: data, algorithms and AI ethics","badge":{"label":"AI and digital tech · Years 9–11"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Digital Technologies","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/subject/digital-technologies/"}],"truth":"A real machine-learning method (k-nearest neighbours, k = 3) running entirely on this page. Your drawings stay on your device and are gone when you leave. The built-in test set is generated drawings, not anyone's data.","footer":"Covers Years 9–10 Technology (computational thinking, data and algorithms, the ethics and bias of digital systems). Leads into NCEA Digital Technologies and the new Applied Intelligent Systems subject planned from 2029.","mainLabel":"A drawing pad on the left, your training examples on the right and the AI's guess","kupu":[{"mi":"rorohiko","en":"computer","ta":6926,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"guess"},{"mi":"raraunga","en":"data","ta":6555,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"draw, score"},{"mi":"hātepe","en":"algorithm","ta":917,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"guess"},{"mi":"whakangungu","en":"to train, training","ta":9686,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"draw"},{"mi":"tauira","en":"example, pattern, model","ta":7706,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"draw"},{"mi":"tīpako","en":"sample","ta":8150,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"score"},{"mi":"tīpako haukume","en":"biased sample","ta":46051,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"bias"},{"mi":"tīpako matapōkere","en":"random sample","ta":46052,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"bias"}],"steps":[{"k":"draw","lab":"Teach it","cap":"You're the teacher. Draw a fish, press Add as Fish. Draw a wheel, press Add as Wheel. Give it at least five of each, all a bit different.","exam":"Machine learning is when a computer learns patterns from training data instead of being given step-by-step rules. The quality and variety of the training data decides how well the model works on new examples.","trap":"Thinking the computer 'understands' what a fish is. It only compares numbers with the examples it was given.","big":"The big idea: an AI like this doesn't follow rules you write. It learns from examples, so the examples you give it decide what it can do.","kupu":["whakangungu","tauira","raraunga"]},{"k":"guess","lab":"Let it guess","cap":"Now draw something new and press Guess. The AI compares your drawing with every example and votes using the three closest.","exam":"To classify a new drawing, the model converts it into numbers and finds the training examples with the smallest distance. The most common label among those nearest neighbours becomes the prediction.","trap":"Treating confidence as certainty. 90% sure can still be wrong.","big":"The big idea: this AI is a nearest-neighbour classifier: it says 'this looks most like those', using distance between grids of numbers.","kupu":["hātepe","rorohiko"]},{"k":"score","lab":"Test it fairly","cap":"Don't just test with your own drawings. Press Run the test set: 40 drawings the AI has never seen, half fish and half wheels.","exam":"A model is evaluated on a separate test set that was not used in training, so its accuracy reflects how well it generalises to new data rather than how well it memorised the training data.","trap":"Testing on the same examples you trained with. That only shows it memorised them.","big":"The big idea: we judge an AI on test data it wasn't trained on. Doing well on the training data proves very little.","kupu":["tīpako","raraunga"]},{"k":"bias","lab":"Find the bias","cap":"Load a lopsided dataset: every training drawing is big and in the top-left corner. Then draw a small one in the bottom-right and ask for a guess. Watch it fail, then fix it.","exam":"Bias in an AI system can come from unrepresentative training data. If every training example is large and in one corner, the model has not learned what a fish looks like elsewhere. Adding varied examples, or centring and scaling each drawing first, reduces this bias.","trap":"Blaming the user for drawing it 'wrong'. The model should be trained on the variety of inputs real people will give it.","big":"The big idea: if the training data only covers some situations, the AI fails in the rest. That's bias, and the fix is better data or better features, not a cleverer excuse.","kupu":["tīpako haukume","tīpako matapōkere"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Three challenges. Use what you've learned.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Real AIs: image and language models learn the same way, from examples, just with billions of numbers instead of 256. Their training data decides what they're good at and where they're biased.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Get three right guesses in a row on your own new drawings.","check":"()=>s.streak>=3","ok":"Yes. More varied examples make it more reliable.","no":"()=>`Your streak is ${s.streak}. Add more varied examples, then keep testing.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Teach it well enough to score at least 80% on the test set (with centring off).","check":"()=>!s.center&&s.acc!=null&&s.acc>=0.8&&!s.biasLoaded","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.acc*100)}%. Your examples covered enough variety.`","no":"()=>s.biasLoaded?\"Clear the lopsided dataset first: it's loaded. Reload the page or teach it from scratch.\":s.acc==null?\"Run the test set first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(s.acc*100)}%. Add examples in different sizes and places.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Load the lopsided dataset, then get it above 75% on the test set without adding any examples.","check":"()=>s.biasLoaded&&s.ex[0].length===8&&s.ex[1].length===8&&s.acc>=0.75","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.acc*100)}%. Centring and scaling removed the position and size bias.`","no":"()=>!s.biasLoaded?\"Press Load a lopsided dataset first.\":`Not yet: ${s.acc==null?\"run the test set\":fmt(s.acc*100)+\"%\"}. What setting stops position and size mattering?`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"button","id":"addA","label":"Add as Fish"},{"type":"button","id":"addB","label":"Add as Wheel"},{"type":"button","id":"guess","label":"Guess"},{"type":"button","id":"right","label":"✓ It was right"},{"type":"button","id":"wrong","label":"✗ It was wrong"},{"type":"button","id":"clear","label":"Clear drawing"},{"type":"button","id":"score","label":"Run the test set"},{"type":"button","id":"bias","label":"Load a lopsided dataset"},{"type":"toggle","id":"center","label":"Centre and scale each drawing"},{"type":"select","id":"nameA","label":"First thing"},{"type":"select","id":"nameB","label":"Second thing"}],"shell":"kit"},"vinyl":{"id":"vinyl","title":"The vinyl machine","href":"m/vinyl.html","desc":"A turntable under a magnifier: circumference and rpm, the groove as a wave, why sped-up songs sound higher, sound waves in air, decibels and safe listening. With real tones to hear.","blurb":"Circumference, rpm, sound waves and safe decibels on a turntable, plus NCEA circular motion.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science","Maths","NCEA L2 Physics 91171","NCEA L3 Physics 91524","Waves","NCEA L2 Physics 91170","NCEA L3 Physics 91523"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science and maths · Waves, sound and circles","crumb":"Year 9 and 10 Science (waves and sound) and Maths (circles, rates and ratios), plus three NCEA Level 2–3 steps on circular motion (AS91171, AS91524)","badge":{"label":"Years 9–11 · NCEA L2 91171 · L3 91524"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics, Earth and Space Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics-earth-and-space-science/"},{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"},{"label":"NZQA: L2 Physics 91171 (mechanics)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91171"},{"label":"NZQA: L3 Physics 91524 (mechanical systems)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91524"}],"truth":"The NCEA steps use the practice pack's radii (0.146 m LP, 0.088 m single); the platter's 1.5 kg and 0.15 m for the Level 3 figures are estimates. A 12-inch LP with grooves from about 14.6 cm to 6.0 cm from the centre. Speed of sound 343 m/s (air at 20°C). Safe listening uses the WHO guide of about 80 dB for up to 40 hours a week for adults (lower for children), halving for every 3 dB louder. The groove drawing is exaggerated so you can see it, and the waves are slowed down. The Blender-built deck and speaker are generic educational apparatus. Belt motion uses a fixed visual pulley ratio, not a slip or torque simulation. The speaker cone is illustrative; device playback volume is not the decibel control. Browser materials differ from the offline Blender render.","footer":"Covers Year 9–10 waves and sound (frequency, wavelength, amplitude, v = fλ, longitudinal waves, decibels) and maths (circumference, rates, ratios). Waves ideas lead into NCEA physics. The three NCEA steps cover circular motion for L2 AS91171 (ω = 2πf, v = rω, a = v²/r) and the rotational ideas of L3 AS91524 (I, L = Iω, ½Iω²).","mainLabel":"A turntable with a record spinning, a magnifier showing the groove's wiggles, and a speaker pushing air","kupu":[{"mi":"oro","en":"sound","ta":4804,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"air, loud"},{"mi":"ngaru","en":"wave","ta":4584,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"groove"},{"mi":"ngaru pou","en":"longitudinal wave","ta":40248,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"air"},{"mi":"auau","en":"frequency","ta":523,"note":"","chip":["f","heard"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"groove, pitch"},{"mi":"hauoro","en":"pitch (of a sound)","ta":39275,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"pitch"},{"mi":"tōiriiri","en":"vibration","ta":15290,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"groove, air"},{"mi":"kahaoro","en":"volume, loudness","ta":45548,"note":"","chip":["db"],"evidence":"Te Reo o Ngā Toi Puoro (MoE 2015) cited in entry","stepsNote":"loud"},{"mi":"pūngao oro","en":"sound energy","ta":47724,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"air"}],"steps":[{"k":"spin","lab":"Spin it","cap":"A record turns at 33⅓ revolutions per minute. Every groove takes the same time to go round, but the outer groove has further to travel.","exam":"At 33⅓ rpm the record makes 33⅓ ÷ 60 = 0.556 turns a second. The outer groove (r = 0.146 m) has a circumference of 2π × 0.146 = 0.917 m, so it moves at 0.917 × 0.556 ≈ 0.51 m/s. The inner groove (r = 0.06 m) moves at only 0.21 m/s.","trap":"Using the diameter in C = 2πr. Use the radius, or use C = πd.","big":"The big idea: one turn covers one circumference, C = 2πr. Same turns a second, bigger circle, faster speed.","kupu":[]},{"k":"groove","lab":"The groove is a wave","cap":"Under the magnifier, the groove wiggles. One full wiggle is one wavelength. Higher notes squeeze more wiggles into each millimetre.","exam":"The groove passes the needle at about 0.51 m/s near the edge. For a 440 Hz note, the wavelength in the groove is λ = v ÷ f = 0.51 ÷ 440 ≈ 0.0012 m, about 1.2 mm. Near the centre the groove moves slower, so the same note is squeezed into a shorter wavelength.","trap":"Mixing up frequency and wavelength. Higher frequency means a shorter wavelength when the speed is the same.","big":"The big idea: wave speed = frequency × wavelength (v = fλ). Here the 'wave speed' is how fast the groove passes the needle.","kupu":["ngaru","auau","tōiriiri"]},{"k":"pitch","lab":"Sped-up songs","cap":"Play a 33⅓ record at 45 rpm and everything happens 1.35 times faster: every frequency goes up, so every note sounds higher. That's how a sped-up song works.","exam":"Playing a 33⅓ rpm record at 45 rpm multiplies every frequency by 45 ÷ 33⅓ = 1.35. A 440 Hz note (A) is heard at 440 × 1.35 = 594 Hz, about five semitones higher (near D). Higher frequency means higher pitch.","trap":"Thinking louder means higher. Loudness is amplitude; pitch is frequency.","big":"The big idea: pitch depends on frequency. Speed the record up by a ratio and every frequency is multiplied by the same ratio.","kupu":["auau","hauoro"]},{"k":"air","lab":"Sound in the air","cap":"The speaker pushes the air back and forth. Bunched-up air (compressions) and spread-out air (rarefactions) travel away at 343 m/s. Slide the frequency and watch the wavelength change.","exam":"Sound is a longitudinal wave: air particles vibrate parallel to the direction the wave travels, making compressions and rarefactions. The speed of sound in air is about 343 m/s, so a 440 Hz note has a wavelength of λ = v ÷ f = 343 ÷ 440 ≈ 0.78 m.","trap":"Thinking the air itself travels from the speaker to your ear. The particles vibrate in place; the energy travels.","big":"The big idea: sound is a longitudinal wave: air particles vibrate back and forth in the same direction the wave travels. v = fλ.","kupu":["oro","ngaru pou","tōiriiri","pūngao oro"]},{"k":"loud","lab":"Loud and safe","cap":"Bigger vibrations mean more energy and a louder sound, measured in decibels. Every 3 dB more doubles the energy, so your safe listening time halves.","exam":"A louder sound has a larger amplitude, so the particles vibrate further and carry more energy. On the decibel scale each increase of 10 dB is ten times the sound energy, and about +3 dB doubles it, so the safe listening time halves.","trap":"Thinking 100 dB is only a bit more than 80 dB. It is about 100 times the sound energy.","big":"The big idea: loudness depends on amplitude. Decibels are a scale where +10 dB is ten times the sound energy.","kupu":["kahaoro","oro"]},{"k":"omega","lab":"NCEA: angular velocity","cap":"Level 2 and 3. Physicists measure spin in radians a second: ω = 2πf. Show that 33⅓ rpm is about 3.5 rad/s. The pink arrow is the velocity of a dust speck on the groove; the mint arrow is its acceleration.","exam":"f = 33.33 ÷ 60 = 0.556 Hz, so ω = 2πf = 2π × 0.556 = 3.49 rad s⁻¹. The period is T = 1 ÷ f = 1.8 s. (Level 3: a 1.5 kg, 0.15 m platter has I = ½MR² = 0.017 kg m², so L = Iω = 0.059 kg m² s⁻¹.)","trap":"Putting rpm straight into ω. Divide by 60 for revolutions a second, then multiply by 2π for radians a second.","big":"The big idea: one turn is 2π radians, so ω = 2πf = 2π ÷ T. Level 3 adds the platter's rotational inertia I = ½MR², angular momentum L = Iω and rotational kinetic energy ½Iω².","kupu":["auau"]},{"k":"centripetal","lab":"NCEA: centripetal acceleration","cap":"A dust speck on the outer groove moves at v = rω and, because its direction keeps changing, it accelerates toward the centre at a = v²/r. Move the needle and watch both arrows.","exam":"v = rω = 0.146 × 3.49 = 0.51 m s⁻¹. a = v² ÷ r = 0.51² ÷ 0.146 ≈ 1.78 m s⁻², directed toward the centre of the record. The friction between the speck and the vinyl provides the centripetal force F = ma.","trap":"Drawing the acceleration along the velocity. In uniform circular motion the acceleration is perpendicular to the velocity, pointing at the centre.","big":"The big idea: uniform circular motion has constant speed but changing velocity, so there is an acceleration, toward the centre, of size v²/r = ω²r. Something must supply the force (friction, for the speck).","kupu":["auau"]},{"k":"formats","lab":"NCEA: LP vs single","cap":"A 7-inch single spins faster (45 rpm) but its outer groove is at only r = 0.088 m. Compare the track speed with the LP's, then use v = rω to explain why inner grooves carry less information per second.","exam":"Single: ω = 2π × 45 ÷ 60 = 4.71 rad s⁻¹, v = rω = 0.088 × 4.71 = 0.41 m s⁻¹, slower than the LP's outer groove (0.51 m s⁻¹) despite the faster spin, because v = rω scales with radius. On any record the inner grooves pass the stylus at lower v, so fewer metres of groove pass each second and less waveform detail fits: inner grooves carry less information per second.","trap":"Assuming faster rpm means a faster groove. Compare rω, not ω: a small record at 45 rpm can be slower at the edge than a big one at 33⅓.","big":"The big idea: v = rω: at the same ω, speed scales with radius. More groove passing the stylus each second means more room for waveform detail, which is why engineers keep the loudest tracks near the outer edge.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Six challenges: three on sound, three on circular motion. Use the controls, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: write the formula, put the numbers in with units, then say what it means: \"so the note sounds higher\".","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Play a 33⅓ record so that a 440 Hz A is heard at about 594 Hz.","check":"()=>Math.abs(heard()-594)<4","ok":"()=>`Yes: 440 × ${fmt(pitchRatio(),3)} = ${fmt(heard())} Hz. That's 45 ÷ 33⅓ = 1.35.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: you hear ${fmt(heard())} Hz. Which turntable speed multiplies 440 by 1.35?`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Find a needle position where a 1000 Hz note is exactly 0.30 mm long in the groove (33⅓ rpm).","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.rpm-100/3)<0.01&&s.f===1000&&Math.abs(speedAt(s.r)/s.f*1000-0.30)<0.02","ok":"()=>`Yes: λ = v ÷ f = ${fmt(speedAt(s.r),3)} ÷ 1000 = ${fmt(speedAt(s.r)/s.f*1000,2)} mm at ${fmt(s.r*100,1)} cm.`","no":"()=>s.f!==1000||Math.abs(s.rpm-100/3)>0.01?\"Set 33⅓ rpm and a 1000 Hz note first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(speedAt(s.r)/s.f*1000,2)} mm. Move the needle: closer to the centre is slower and shorter.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the loudness where the safe listening time is about 10 hours a week.","check":"()=>Math.abs(40*Math.pow(2,-(s.db-80)/3)-10)<2","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${s.db} dB. From 80 dB, +6 dB is two doublings, so 40 h ÷ 4 = 10 h.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${s.db} dB allows about ${fmt(40*Math.pow(2,-(s.db-80)/3),1)} h. Each +3 dB halves the time from 40 h at 80 dB.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"NCEA: set the turntable so the angular velocity is about 4.7 rad/s.","check":"()=>Math.abs(omega()-4.71)<0.1","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.rpm,1)} rpm = ${fmt(rps(),3)} Hz, ω = 2π × ${fmt(rps(),3)} = ${fmt(omega(),2)} rad/s.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ω = ${fmt(omega(),2)} rad/s at ${fmt(s.rpm,1)} rpm. Which speed gives f = 4.7 ÷ 2π = 0.75 Hz?`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"NCEA: on the LP at 33⅓ rpm, put the needle where the centripetal acceleration is 1.0 m/s².","check":"()=>s.format===\"lp\"&&Math.abs(s.rpm-100/3)<0.01&&Math.abs(acen(s.r)-1.0)<0.03","ok":"()=>`Yes: a = ω²r = ${fmt(omega(),2)}² × ${fmt(s.r,3)} = ${fmt(acen(s.r),2)} m/s² at ${fmt(s.r*100,1)} cm.`","no":"()=>s.format!==\"lp\"||Math.abs(s.rpm-100/3)>0.01?\"Choose the 12-inch LP at 33⅓ first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(acen(s.r),2)} m/s² at ${fmt(s.r*100,1)} cm. a = ω²r, so r = 1.0 ÷ ${fmt(omega()*omega(),2)} = ${fmt(100/(omega()*omega()),1)} cm.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"NCEA: find the LP needle position (33⅓ rpm) where the groove speed equals the 7-inch single's outer groove at 45 rpm.","check":"()=>s.format===\"lp\"&&Math.abs(s.rpm-100/3)<0.01&&Math.abs(vlin(s.r)-R_SINGLE*2*Math.PI*0.75)<0.012","ok":"()=>`Yes: the single's edge moves at 0.088 × 4.71 = ${fmt(R_SINGLE*2*Math.PI*0.75,2)} m/s; on the LP that is r = v ÷ ω = ${fmt(R_SINGLE*2*Math.PI*0.75/omega()*100,1)} cm, well inside the edge.`","no":"()=>s.format!==\"lp\"||Math.abs(s.rpm-100/3)>0.01?\"Choose the 12-inch LP at 33⅓ first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(vlin(s.r),2)} m/s at ${fmt(s.r*100,1)} cm. The single's edge does 0.41 m/s; r = v ÷ ω.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"select","id":"rpm","label":"Turntable"},{"type":"range","id":"f","label":"Note recorded"},{"type":"range","id":"needle","label":"Needle position"},{"type":"range","id":"amp","label":"Volume (amplitude)"},{"type":"range","id":"db","label":"Loudness"},{"type":"button","id":"hear","label":"▶ Hear it"},{"type":"select","id":"format","label":"Record"}],"shell":"kit"},"bike":{"id":"bike","title":"The downhill machine","href":"m/bike.html","desc":"Drop into a redwood-forest downhill trail with a helmet cam: speed–time graphs, balanced forces, energy into brake heat, a gap jump, stopping distance in the mud and a suspension spring.","blurb":"Speed–time graphs, forces, brake heat and stopping distance on a redwood trail, plus an NCEA ski run.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science","Forces and motion","NCEA L2 Physics 91171","NCEA L3 Physics 91524"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science · Forces and motion","crumb":"Year 9 and 10 Science · forces, motion and energy, with NCEA Level 1 ideas, plus four NCEA Level 2–3 steps on a ski run (AS91171, AS91524)","badge":{"label":"Years 9–11 · NCEA L2 91171 · L3 91524"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics, Earth and Space Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics-earth-and-space-science/"},{"label":"NZQA: Science past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"},{"label":"NZQA: L2 Physics 91171 (mechanics)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91171"},{"label":"NZQA: L3 Physics 91524 (mechanical systems)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91524"}],"truth":"The NCEA ski-run steps use the practice pack's figures (120 m drop over 400 m, 72 kg, 34 m/s); the turn's radius, entry speed and snow grip μ are estimates, and the skier is animated at three times real time with a constant average acceleration. A fictional trail inspired by the Redwoods, Whakarewarewa Forest, Rotorua: the forest (coast redwoods with tree ferns beneath, needle litter, haze and shafts of light) is an illustrative scene, not a map of a real trail. Uses g = 9.8 N/kg and air density 1.2 kg/m³. Bike masses (14.5–18.4 kg), drag areas (0.47–0.65 m²), rolling resistance (0.03–0.08) and tyre grip (μ 0.22–0.78) are typical estimates, not measurements of real bikes. The fork spring also stands in for your legs, so k is stiffer than a real fork spring. Rotor temperatures assume all the braking heat stays in the rotors (a top estimate; real rotors also lose heat to the air). The rider brakes to hold the speed you choose. Bikes and riders are drawn larger than the trail so you can see them. The Blender construction is illustrative, not contact physics or manufacturing validation. The chain coasts with no cadence model. The enlarged suspension cutaway uses schematic spacing. Browser reflections differ from the offline Blender render.","footer":"Covers Year 9–10 forces and motion: mass and weight, speed, distance–time and speed–time graphs, balanced and unbalanced forces, a = F/m, Ep and Ek, heat and Q = mcΔT, projectiles, stopping distance, pressure and Hooke's law. Energy ideas link to NCEA L1 AS 92047. The four NCEA steps cover L2 AS91171 (energy conservation, work, force components, circular motion) and L3 AS91524 (circular motion with multiple forces, banked turns). The 3D redwood crowns are grown with ez-tree by Daniel Greenheck (MIT licence); the bark, needle litter, trail dirt and fern textures are drawn from code for this machine, with no photographs.","mainLabel":"A downhill mountain bike and rider in a redwood forest, with a helmet camera view and force arrows","kupu":[{"mi":"tōpana","en":"force","ta":40375,"note":"","chip":["net"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"forces, fork"},{"mi":"tōpana taurite","en":"balanced forces","ta":40395,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"forces"},{"mi":"tere","en":"speed","ta":7953,"note":"","chip":["speed","avg","lip"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"drop, graph, jump, stop"},{"mi":"whakahohoro","en":"acceleration, speeding up","ta":12447,"note":"","chip":["accel"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"drop, graph"},{"mi":"pūngao","en":"energy","ta":40075,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"energy"},{"mi":"pūngao neke","en":"kinetic energy","ta":47721,"note":"","chip":["ek"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"energy"},{"mi":"pūngao moe","en":"potential energy","ta":47720,"note":"","chip":["ep"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"energy"},{"mi":"waku","en":"friction","ta":12421,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"garage, forces, stop"}],"steps":[{"k":"garage","lab":"The garage","cap":"Choose a bike and build it. Every part changes a real number in the physics, and the rest of the machine uses your build.","exam":"Mass is the amount of matter in an object, measured in kilograms. Weight is the force of gravity on that mass, measured in newtons: W = mg. A 72 kg rider and bike has a weight of 72 × 9.8 ≈ 706 N on Earth.","trap":"Mixing up mass and weight. Mass is in kg and is the same everywhere. Weight is a force in N and depends on gravity (W = mg).","big":"The big idea: mass, grip, rolling resistance, suspension stiffness and air resistance are all numbers you can change. Change the bike and every later step changes with it.","kupu":["waku"]},{"k":"drop","lab":"Drop in","cap":"Press play to drop into the trail. The helmet cam shows your speed. Speed = distance ÷ time: watch how far you go each second.","exam":"Average speed = distance ÷ time. If the rider covers 248 m in 22 s, the average speed is 248 ÷ 22 ≈ 11.3 m/s, which is 11.3 × 3.6 ≈ 41 km/h. The instantaneous speed is higher on the steep chute and lower in the berm.","trap":"Mixing units. Change km/h to m/s by dividing by 3.6 before using it in a formula.","big":"The big idea: average speed = total distance ÷ total time. Your speed at any moment (instantaneous speed) changes all the way down.","kupu":["tere","whakahohoro"]},{"k":"graph","lab":"Speed–time graph","cap":"The speed–time graph builds as you ride. Steep parts are big accelerations. Flat parts mean steady speed, where you're dragging the brakes. Change your build in the garage and race your last run.","exam":"On a speed–time graph the gradient shows acceleration: a = change in speed ÷ time. A horizontal line means constant speed, so the acceleration is zero. The area under the graph is the distance travelled.","trap":"Reading a distance–time graph as if it were speed–time. A flat line on distance–time means stopped; on speed–time it means moving at a steady speed.","big":"The big idea: on a speed–time graph, the gradient is the acceleration and the area under the line is the distance travelled.","kupu":["tere","whakahohoro"]},{"k":"forces","lab":"Forces on the rider","cap":"Arrows show the forces on the rider. When your speed is steady, the down-slope pull is cancelled by friction, air and brakes. Slide the speed limit up to let go of the brakes, or sit up and feel the air.","exam":"When the rider travels at a constant speed the forces along the slope are balanced: the component of weight down the slope equals friction, air resistance and the braking force, so the net force is zero and there is no acceleration. When the rider releases the brakes, the forces become unbalanced and the rider accelerates (a = F_net ÷ m).","trap":"Thinking you need a forward force to keep moving at a steady speed. At constant speed the forces are balanced.","big":"The big idea: balanced forces mean steady speed (or staying still). Unbalanced forces change speed: a = F ÷ m.","kupu":["tōpana","tōpana taurite","waku"]},{"k":"energy","lab":"Where the energy goes","cap":"Every metre you drop turns gravitational potential energy into other forms. Watch how much becomes speed and how much becomes heat in your brake rotors.","exam":"As the rider descends, gravitational potential energy (Ep = mgh) is transformed into kinetic energy (Ek = ½mv²). Some energy is transformed into heat in the brake rotors and by friction and air resistance, so Ek at the bottom is less than the Ep lost. Energy is not used up; it changes form.","trap":"Saying energy is 'lost' or 'used up'. Say it is transformed (changed) into heat and sound.","big":"The big idea: energy is conserved. Ep lost = Ek gained + heat (brakes, friction and air resistance).","kupu":["pūngao","pūngao moe","pūngao neke"]},{"k":"jump","lab":"The gap jump","cap":"At the lip you become a projectile. Gravity pulls you down at 9.8 m/s² while you keep moving forwards. Too slow and you'll case the landing. Your fork catches the landing.","exam":"Once the rider leaves the lip, the only significant force is gravity (plus a little air resistance). The rider keeps their horizontal speed but accelerates downwards at 9.8 m/s², so the path is a curve. A higher speed at the lip means the rider travels further horizontally before landing.","trap":"Thinking a heavier rider falls faster in the air. Without air resistance, mass does not change how fast you fall.","big":"The big idea: in the air the only forces are gravity and air resistance. Faster at the lip means more distance before you land.","kupu":["tere"]},{"k":"stop","lab":"Stopping distance","cap":"A log has fallen across the trail 25 m ahead. You need time to react, then the brakes need distance to stop you. Try mud, wet roots, more speed, a slower reaction, or different tyres in the garage.","exam":"Stopping distance = thinking distance + braking distance. Thinking distance = speed × reaction time, so it doubles when speed doubles. Braking distance depends on speed squared, so doubling the speed makes it about four times longer. Mud lowers the friction between the tyres and the trail, so the braking distance increases.","trap":"Assuming double the speed means double the braking distance. It's four times as far.","big":"The big idea: stopping distance = thinking distance + braking distance. Braking distance grows with speed squared.","kupu":["waku","tere"]},{"k":"fork","lab":"Suspension spring","cap":"Drop off a ledge and the fork spring catches you. A stiffer spring compresses less. Too soft and you bottom out the 200 mm of travel on your DROP.","exam":"The fork spring obeys Hooke's law, F = kx: the force is proportional to the compression. A spring with a larger spring constant k compresses less for the same force. On landing, the rider's kinetic energy is transferred into elastic potential energy in the spring, and the damper turns some of it into heat so the bike doesn't bounce.","trap":"Using millimetres in F = kx when k is in N/m. Convert to metres first: 120 mm = 0.12 m.","big":"The big idea: a spring obeys Hooke's law, F = kx. It stores energy when it's squashed (elastic potential energy) and gives it back.","kupu":["tōpana"]},{"k":"run","lab":"NCEA: the ski run","cap":"Level 2 and 3. Think Coronet Peak: a run dropping 120 m over 400 m of track. A 72 kg skier reaches the bottom at 34 m/s. Press play; the bars show the energy the slope gave and where it went.","exam":"Ep = mgh = 72 × 9.8 × 120 = 8.47 × 10⁴ J. At the bottom Ek = ½mv² = 0.5 × 72 × 34² = 4.16 × 10⁴ J, so (8.47 − 4.16) ÷ 8.47 ≈ 51 % of the original energy was dissipated by friction and air resistance.","trap":"Using the track length (400 m) as h in mgh. Only the vertical drop counts for gravitational potential energy.","big":"The big idea: energy conservation with a leak. Ep lost = Ek gained + work done against friction and air. The gap between mgh and ½mv² is what the run dissipated.","kupu":["pūngao moe","pūngao neke","waku"]},{"k":"work","lab":"NCEA: forces on the slope","cap":"Where did 43 kJ go? Work = force × distance along the track. Resolve the skier's weight: the component down the slope drives the run; an average resistive force opposes it.","exam":"sin θ = 120 ÷ 400 = 0.30, so θ = 17.5°. The component of weight down the slope is mg sin θ = 72 × 9.8 × 0.30 = 212 N. The 4.31 × 10⁴ J dissipated over 400 m gives an average resistive force of W ÷ d = 108 N, so the net force is about 104 N and the average acceleration is F ÷ m ≈ 1.4 m s⁻².","trap":"Resolving with cos instead of sin. Down the slope is mg sin θ; the normal force is mg cos θ.","big":"The big idea: resolve weight into mg sin θ (down the slope) and mg cos θ (into the slope). Work done against resistance W = Fd lets you turn an energy gap into an average force.","kupu":["tōpana","waku","whakahohoro"]},{"k":"model","lab":"NCEA: the frictionless model","cap":"The lab's twin computes an 'exit speed' with v = √(2gh), frictionless. Compare it with the real 34 m/s, and see why a course designer uses trail shape, not just slope, to keep skiers safe on a steeper pitch.","exam":"The frictionless prediction is v = √(2 × 9.8 × 120) = 48.5 m s⁻¹ against the real 34 m s⁻¹, an overestimate of about 43 % in speed, because the model ignores the work done against friction (W = Fd over 400 m) and drag, which grows with v². Designers add traverses, check turns and rolling terrain: shortening the effective fall line converts less Ep per metre travelled, so trail shape, not just slope, sets speed.","trap":"Comparing energies as if they were speeds. Halving the kinetic energy only cuts the speed to 71 % (v ∝ √E).","big":"The big idea: a model is only as good as what it leaves out. Because v ∝ √E, a 51 % energy loss is a 30 % speed loss, and the frictionless number overestimates by about 43 %. Excellence answers quantify the gap and say what to do about it.","kupu":["tere","pūngao"]},{"k":"turn","lab":"NCEA: the check turn","cap":"Level 2 circular motion, with a Level 3 berm. In a turn, friction between edged skis and snow must supply the centripetal force mv²/r. Change the radius, the entry speed and the grip: does the turn hold?","exam":"For a 30 m radius turn with μ = 0.5, the maximum friction is μmg = 0.5 × 72 × 9.8 = 353 N, and the turn needs mv²/r = 72 × 20² ÷ 30 = 960 N at 20 m s⁻¹, so the skier skids out. The fastest speed the turn can hold is v = √(μgr) = √(0.5 × 9.8 × 30) = 12.1 m s⁻¹. Level 3: on a banked turn with no friction, tan θ = v²/(rg).","trap":"Treating centripetal force as a separate force to add to the diagram. Draw the real forces (friction, normal, weight); their inward resultant is the centripetal force.","big":"The big idea: centripetal force is not an extra force; it is the name for the net inward force. On flat snow that is friction, limited to μN, so v max = √(μgr). On a bermed turn part of the normal force points inward too.","kupu":["tōpana","tere"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Eight challenges: five on the trail and three on the NCEA ski run. Use the controls from any step and the garage, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: say which forces act, whether they're balanced, and what that means for the speed. Show the formula, the numbers and the unit.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Clear the gap and finish in under 25 seconds without crashing. (Go to Drop in, set the brake speed, ride it, then come back.)","check":"()=>!!(s.lastRun&&s.lastRun.cleared&&s.lastRun.time<25)","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.lastRun.time,1)} s on ${s.lastRun.build} ${s.lastRun.limit>=80?\"with no brakes\":\"braking above \"+s.lastRun.limit+\" km/h\"}.`","no":"()=>s.lastRun?`Your last run: ${fmt(s.lastRun.time,1)} s, gap ${s.lastRun.cleared?\"cleared\":\"not cleared\"}. Brake less (raise the brake speed).`:\"No finished run yet. Ride the trail from Drop in.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"In the mud at 40 km/h, choose a reaction time that stops you before the log at 25 m.","check":"()=>s.grip===\"mud\"&&s.v0===40&&stopNums().total<25","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(stopNums().total,1)} m. Braking alone takes ${fmt(stopNums().brake,1)} m in mud on ${WH().n}, so you can only spend ${fmt(25-stopNums().brake,1)} m thinking.`","no":"()=>s.grip!==\"mud\"||s.v0!==40?\"Set the trail to wet and muddy and the speed to 40 km/h first.\":stopNums().brake+11.11*0.3>=25?`Braking alone takes ${fmt(stopNums().brake,1)} m on ${WH().n}, so even the fastest reaction won't do it. Fit grippier wheels in the garage.`:`Not yet: ${fmt(stopNums().total,1)} m. Thinking distance = 11.1 m/s × reaction time.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the spring so a 1.5 m drop uses between 75% and 95% of your DROP's 200 mm of travel.","check":"()=>s.fh===1.5&&forkNums().x>=0.75*TRAVEL()&&forkNums().x<=0.95*TRAVEL()","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(forkNums().x*1000)} mm of ${fmt(TRAVEL()*1000)} mm with k = ${s.k} N/mm. Plenty of travel used, no bottoming out.`","no":"()=>s.fh!==1.5?\"Set the drop height to 1.5 m first (Suspension spring step).\":`Not yet: ${fmt(forkNums().x*1000)} mm of ${fmt(TRAVEL()*1000)} mm. ${forkNums().x>0.95*TRAVEL()?\"Stiffer spring (bigger k).\":\"Softer spring (smaller k).\"}`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Garage: on wet roots at 30 km/h with a 0.5 s reaction time, build a bike that stops within 15 m.","check":"()=>stopNums(30,0.5,\"roots\").total<=15","ok":"()=>{const N=stopNums(30,0.5,\"roots\");return `Yes: ${WH().n} grip μ = ${fmt(N.mu,2)} on roots. ${fmt(N.think,1)} m thinking + ${fmt(N.brake,1)} m braking = ${fmt(N.total,1)} m.`}","no":"()=>{const N=stopNums(30,0.5,\"roots\");return `${WH().n}: μ = ${fmt(N.mu,2)} on roots, ${fmt(N.think,1)} + ${fmt(N.brake,1)} = ${fmt(N.total,1)} m. Braking distance = v² ÷ (2μg), so you need a bigger μ. Swap wheels in the garage.`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Garage: FLOW has small 180 mm rotors. Build a FLOW, brake above 45 km/h (or lower) on dry hardpack, and finish a run with the rotors under 125 °C. Which parts help, and why?","check":"()=>!!(s.lastRun&&s.lastRun.model===\"flow\"&&s.lastRun.grip===\"dry\"&&s.lastRun.limit<=45&&s.lastRun.rotorT<ROTOR_MAX)","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.lastRun.rotorT)} °C on ${s.lastRun.build}. Less mass means less Ep to get rid of; heavier rotors need more energy per degree (Q = mcΔT); more rolling resistance and drag take some energy so the brakes don't have to.`","no":"()=>!s.lastRun?\"No finished run yet. Build a FLOW, then ride from Drop in with the brake speed at 45 km/h or less.\":s.lastRun.model!==\"flow\"?`Your last run was on ${s.lastRun.build}. Choose FLOW in the garage, then ride again.`:s.lastRun.grip!==\"dry\"?\"Set the trail to dry hardpack (Forces step) and ride again.\":s.lastRun.limit>45?`Your last run ${s.lastRun.limit>=80?\"had no brakes\":\"braked above \"+s.lastRun.limit+\" km/h\"}. Set the brake speed to 45 km/h or less and ride again.`:`Your last run: ${fmt(s.lastRun.rotorT)} °C on ${s.lastRun.build}. Think Q = mcΔT: what makes Q smaller, or m bigger?`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"NCEA ski run (120 m drop): set a real exit speed at which less than 40% of the energy is dissipated.","check":"()=>s.ski.h===120&&SK.loss()<0.4","ok":"()=>`Yes: at ${fmt(s.ski.v)} m/s, Ek = ${kJ(SK.ek())} of ${kJ(SK.ep())}, so only ${fmt(SK.loss()*100)}% was lost.`","no":"()=>s.ski.h!==120?\"Set the vertical drop back to 120 m first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(SK.loss()*100)}% lost at ${fmt(s.ski.v)} m/s. You need ½mv² > 0.6 × mgh, so v > √(1.2 × 9.8 × 120) = ${fmt(Math.sqrt(1.2*g*120),1)} m/s.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Check turn: with μ = 0.50 and a 20 m/s entry, find the smallest radius (to the nearest 5 m) that holds the turn.","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.ski.mu-0.5)<1e-9&&s.ski.vt===20&&s.ski.r>=SK.rmin()&&s.ski.r<SK.rmin()+5","ok":"()=>`Yes: r min = v² ÷ (μg) = 400 ÷ 4.9 = ${fmt(SK.rmin(),1)} m, so ${fmt(s.ski.r)} m holds: friction ${fmt(s.ski.mu*s.ski.m*g)} N against mv²/r = ${fmt(SK.fc())} N.`","no":"()=>Math.abs(s.ski.mu-0.5)>1e-9||s.ski.vt!==20?\"Set μ = 0.50 and the entry speed to 20 m/s first.\":s.ski.r<SK.rmin()?`Not yet: at ${fmt(s.ski.r)} m the turn needs ${fmt(SK.fc())} N but friction can only give ${fmt(s.ski.mu*s.ski.m*g)} N. r ≥ v² ÷ (μg).`:`That holds, but a smaller radius would too. r min = v² ÷ (μg) = ${fmt(SK.rmin(),1)} m.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Trail design: keep the frictionless exit speed √(2gh) under 30 m/s by limiting the vertical drop.","check":"()=>SK.vfree()<30","ok":"()=>`Yes: a ${fmt(s.ski.h)} m drop gives √(2 × 9.8 × ${fmt(s.ski.h)}) = ${fmt(SK.vfree(),1)} m/s even with no friction. That is what traverses do: they split a big drop into small ones.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(s.ski.h)} m gives ${fmt(SK.vfree(),1)} m/s. v² = 2gh, so h must be under 30² ÷ (2 × 9.8) = ${fmt(900/(2*g),1)} m.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"toggle","id":"slow","label":"Slow motion"},{"type":"toggle","id":"pose","label":"Attack position"},{"type":"range","id":"limit","label":"Brake above"},{"type":"range","id":"m","label":"Rider mass"},{"type":"select","id":"grip","label":"Trail"},{"type":"range","id":"v0","label":"Speed"},{"type":"range","id":"tr","label":"Reaction time"},{"type":"range","id":"k","label":"Spring"},{"type":"range","id":"fh","label":"Drop height"},{"type":"range","id":"skm","label":"Skier mass"},{"type":"range","id":"skh","label":"Vertical drop"},{"type":"range","id":"skl","label":"Track length"},{"type":"range","id":"skv","label":"Real exit speed"},{"type":"range","id":"skr","label":"Turn radius"},{"type":"range","id":"skvt","label":"Entry speed"},{"type":"range","id":"skmu","label":"Snow grip μ"}],"shell":"kit"},"lines":{"id":"lines","title":"The straight-line machine","href":"m/lines.html","desc":"Two tanks and a plotter: y = mx + c, gradients, intercepts, two lines crossing, inequalities and a scooter-hire showdown.","blurb":"y = mx + c, gradients, intercepts and inequalities with two tanks and a plotter.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","NCEA L1 Maths 91947","Maths","Algebra"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Maths · Linear relationships","crumb":"Year 9–10 maths · NCEA Level 1 Mathematics and Statistics","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 Maths 91947","exam":"2026-11-10T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Tue 10 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/subject/mathematics-and-statistics"}],"truth":"The scooter companies and their prices are made up for practice. Tanks are idealised: the flow rate never changes.","footer":"Linear relationships (y = mx + c, rules from tables, simultaneous equations, inequalities) are Year 9–10 maths content and are examined through reasoning in NCEA Level 1 standard 91947.","mainLabel":"Water tanks filling and draining, with a graph of water against time","kupu":[{"mi":"kauwhata","en":"graph","ta":2460,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"start"},{"mi":"kauwhata rārangi","en":"line graph","ta":45723,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"meet"},{"mi":"rārangi torotika","en":"straight line","ta":45905,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"pānga rārangi","en":"linear relationship","ta":45815,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"rate, scooter"},{"mi":"rōnaki","en":"gradient, slope","ta":13240,"note":"","chip":["m"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"rate, drain"},{"mi":"haukotinga","en":"intercept","ta":45643,"note":"","chip":["c"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"start"},{"mi":"taurangi","en":"variable","ta":7765,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"rule"},{"mi":"whārite","en":"equation","ta":10185,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"rule, meet"}],"steps":[{"k":"start","lab":"Start level","cap":"Set how much water is in the tank before the tap opens. On the graph, that's where the line starts: the y-intercept.","exam":"The y-intercept is 10, which means the tank starts with 10 litres of water before any time has passed.","trap":"Reading the intercept off the horizontal axis. The y-intercept is on the vertical axis, where time = 0.","big":"The big idea: in y = mx + c, c is the value when x is 0, the starting amount. It's where the line crosses the vertical axis.","kupu":["kauwhata","haukotinga"]},{"k":"rate","lab":"Gradient","cap":"Open the tap wider. More litres every minute makes a steeper line. The gradient is rise over run.","trap":"Giving the gradient without units or meaning. Say what it is in context: \"3 litres per minute\".","big":"The big idea: the gradient m is the rate of change: how much y goes up for every 1 that x goes up.","kupu":["rōnaki","pānga rārangi"]},{"k":"drain","lab":"Draining","cap":"Make the rate negative and the tank drains. The line slopes down, and where it hits zero is when the tank is empty.","exam":"The rule is L = −4t + 80. The tank is empty when L = 0, so −4t + 80 = 0, which gives t = 20. The tank is empty after 20 minutes.","trap":"Dropping the negative sign. A draining tank must have a negative gradient.","big":"The big idea: a negative gradient means y goes down as x goes up. Solve mx + c = 0 to find when it runs out.","kupu":["rōnaki"]},{"k":"rule","lab":"Find the rule","cap":"Work backwards. Read the start from the table at t = 0 and the gradient from how much it changes each minute.","exam":"From the table, the water increases by 5 litres every minute, so m = 5. At t = 0 there are 20 litres, so c = 20. The rule is L = 5t + 20.","trap":"Using the first value in the table as c when the table doesn't start at x = 0. Work back to x = 0 first.","big":"The big idea: a table of values gives you the rule: the constant difference is m, the value at x = 0 is c.","kupu":["whārite","taurangi"]},{"k":"meet","lab":"Two tanks","cap":"Tank A fills while tank B drains. Where the lines cross, both tanks hold the same amount: the solution to both equations at once.","exam":"The tanks hold the same amount when 3t + 10 = −2t + 80. Adding 2t and subtracting 10 gives 5t = 70, so t = 14. At 14 minutes both tanks hold 52 litres.","trap":"Stopping at t = 14. Finish the answer: substitute back to get the amount, and say what it means.","big":"The big idea (Merit and Excellence): the point where two lines cross solves both equations at once. Set them equal and solve.","kupu":["whārite","kauwhata rārangi"]},{"k":"scooter","lab":"Scooter hire","cap":"Two made-up scooter companies. A charges $1 to unlock plus 40c a minute; B has no unlock fee but 60c a minute. Which is cheaper?","exam":"Scooter A costs C = 0.4t + 1 and scooter B costs C = 0.6t. They cost the same when 0.4t + 1 = 0.6t, so 0.2t = 1 and t = 5. For rides shorter than 5 minutes B is cheaper; for longer rides A is cheaper.","trap":"Answering \"A is cheaper\" without saying when. The answer depends on the length of the ride.","big":"The big idea: the unlock fee is the intercept and the per-minute price is the gradient. The cheaper option flips where the lines cross.","kupu":["pānga rārangi"]},{"k":"ineq","lab":"Inequalities","cap":"When does the tank hold at least 50 litres? Solve the inequality, and watch the shaded band on the time axis.","exam":"The tank holds at least 50 litres when 3t + 10 ≥ 50. Subtracting 10 gives 3t ≥ 40, so t ≥ 13⅓. From about 13.3 minutes on, the tank holds 50 litres or more.","trap":"Forgetting to flip the inequality sign when you divide by a negative number.","big":"The big idea: an inequality has a range of answers. Solve it like an equation, then describe the range.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Set the controls to answer each one, then press Check. The machine marks you with the same rules that draw the lines.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: for Merit, show each step in order. For Excellence, explain what your answer means and check it makes sense.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Make tank A's line pass through (0, 10) and (10, 30).","check":"()=>s.cA===10&&s.mA===2","ok":"Yes: it rises 20 litres in 10 minutes, so m = 2, and starts at 10. L = 2t + 10.","no":"()=>`Not yet: your line is ${rule(s.mA,s.cA)}. Work out the rise over the run first.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make the two tanks hold the same amount at exactly 8 minutes.","check":"()=>{const X=meet(s.mA,s.cA,s.mB,s.cB);return X&&Math.abs(X.t-8)<0.01}","ok":"()=>{const X=meet(s.mA,s.cA,s.mB,s.cB);return`Yes: both hold ${fmtN(Math.round(X.y*10)/10)} L at 8 minutes.`}","no":"()=>{const X=meet(s.mA,s.cA,s.mB,s.cB);return X?`Not yet: they meet at ${fmtN(Math.round(X.t*100)/100)} min.`:\"Not yet: parallel lines never meet. Give them different rates.\"}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make tank A run empty at exactly 12 minutes.","check":"()=>{const e=emptyAt(s.mA,s.cA);return e!==null&&Math.abs(e-12)<0.01}","ok":"Yes: c ÷ (rate) = 12. For example 60 L draining at 5 L a minute.","no":"Not yet. The rate must be negative, and the start divided by the drain rate must be 12.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"Reset"},{"type":"range","id":"cA","label":"A starts"},{"type":"range","id":"mA","label":"A rate"},{"type":"range","id":"cB","label":"B starts"},{"type":"range","id":"mB","label":"B rate"},{"type":"range","id":"feeA","label":"A unlock"},{"type":"range","id":"rateA","label":"A per min"},{"type":"range","id":"feeB","label":"B unlock"},{"type":"range","id":"rateB","label":"B per min"}],"shell":"kit"},"particles":{"id":"particles","title":"The particle machine","href":"m/particles.html","desc":"Ice to steam on a hotplate: heating curves, melting and boiling, why water boils at 87°C on Aoraki, and gas pressure in a syringe.","blurb":"Heating curves, melting, boiling and gas pressure, from ice to steam.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science · Matter and particles","crumb":"Year 9–10 science · foundation for NCEA Level 1 Chemistry and Biology","badge":null,"papers":[],"truth":"A simplified model: 0.5 kg of water, standard textbook values for specific heat and latent heat, no heat lost to the room, and particles drawn far bigger than they are. Boiling points use the standard atmosphere and are close to real values.","footer":"Matter and the particle model is core Year 9–10 science and underpins NCEA Level 1 Chemistry and Biology.","mainLabel":"Half a kilogram of ice in a beaker on a hotplate, shown as particles, with a thermometer","kupu":[{"mi":"matūriki","en":"particle","ta":50542,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"particles, heat"},{"mi":"totoka","en":"solid","ta":8538,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"particles, melt"},{"mi":"wē","en":"liquid","ta":13057,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"particles, melt"},{"mi":"haurehu","en":"gas","ta":39276,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"particles, boil, pressure"},{"mi":"paemahana","en":"temperature","ta":39927,"note":"","chip":["temp","boilpt"],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"heat, warm"},{"mi":"rewa","en":"to melt","ta":6746,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"melt"},{"mi":"pae rewa","en":"melting point","ta":40108,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"melt"},{"mi":"koropupū","en":"to boil, bubble up","ta":3056,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"boil, mountain"}],"steps":[{"k":"particles","lab":"Particles","cap":"Everything is made of tiny particles that never stop moving. In ice they are locked in place and can only vibrate.","exam":"In a solid the particles are held tightly in a fixed pattern by strong forces of attraction, so they can only vibrate on the spot. This is why a solid keeps its shape.","trap":"Saying the particles in a solid don't move. They do: they vibrate in fixed positions.","big":"The big idea: solids, liquids and gases are the same particles. What changes is how much they move and how tightly they hold on to each other.","kupu":["matūriki","totoka","wē","haurehu"]},{"k":"heat","lab":"Heat it","cap":"Turn on the hotplate. Heat energy makes the ice particles vibrate faster, so the temperature rises.","exam":"When the ice is heated, its particles gain kinetic energy and vibrate faster, so the temperature of the ice rises.","trap":"Mixing up heat and temperature. Heat is energy going in; temperature is how fast the particles are moving.","big":"The big idea: temperature measures how fast the particles are moving on average. Adding heat speeds them up.","kupu":["paemahana","matūriki"]},{"k":"melt","lab":"Melting","cap":"At 0°C the temperature stops rising even though heat keeps going in. The energy is breaking the ice's hold on its particles.","exam":"While the ice melts, the temperature stays at 0°C. The heat energy is used to overcome the forces of attraction holding the particles in their fixed positions, not to make them move faster.","trap":"Expecting the thermometer to keep climbing during melting. Look for the flat part of the heating curve.","big":"The big idea: during a change of state, heat breaks the forces between particles instead of speeding them up, so the temperature stays flat.","kupu":["rewa","pae rewa","totoka","wē"]},{"k":"warm","lab":"Warming water","cap":"Now it's all liquid. The particles slide past each other and move faster as the water heats up.","exam":"In a liquid the particles are close together but not in fixed positions, so they can slide past each other. As the water is heated, the particles move faster and the temperature rises.","trap":"Drawing liquid particles spread far apart. They stay almost as close as in a solid.","big":"The big idea: in a liquid the particles are still close together but can move past each other, so a liquid flows and takes the shape of its container.","kupu":["paemahana"]},{"k":"boil","lab":"Boiling","cap":"At boiling point the temperature goes flat again. Particles gain enough energy to escape as a gas.","exam":"At 100°C the water boils and the temperature stays the same. The heat energy is used to overcome the forces between the particles so they can escape and spread out as a gas.","trap":"Thinking the bubbles in boiling water are air. They are water vapour.","big":"The big idea: boiling takes far more energy than melting: 2,260 kJ for each kilogram, nearly seven times more.","kupu":["koropupū","haurehu"]},{"k":"mountain","lab":"Up a mountain","cap":"Take the experiment up Ruapehu or Aoraki. Lower air pressure lets particles escape more easily, so water boils at a lower temperature.","exam":"At high altitude the air pressure is lower, so the water particles need less energy to escape from the liquid. This means water boils at a lower temperature, about 87°C on the summit of Aoraki / Mt Cook.","trap":"Thinking water always boils at 100°C. That is only at sea-level pressure.","big":"The big idea: boiling point depends on the air pressure pushing on the water. On the summit of Aoraki / Mt Cook water boils at about 87°C.","kupu":["koropupū"]},{"k":"pressure","lab":"Gas pressure","cap":"Push the plunger in. The same gas particles hit the walls more often in a smaller space, so the pressure goes up.","exam":"Gas pressure is caused by particles colliding with the walls of their container. When the volume is made smaller, the particles hit the walls more often, so the pressure increases.","trap":"Saying the particles get smaller when a gas is squashed. The particles stay the same size; the spaces between them shrink.","big":"The big idea: gas pressure is particles hitting the walls. Halve the volume and the pressure doubles; heat the gas and the hits get harder and more frequent.","kupu":["haurehu"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Use the controls to set up each one, then press Check. The machine marks you using the same model that moves it.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: name the particles, say what they're doing, then link it to what you can see: \"the particles…, so…\".","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Get the water boiling on Aoraki / Mt Cook. What temperature does the thermometer show?","check":"()=>s.alt===\"3724\"&&stateFrom(s.Q,Tb()).zone===3","ok":"()=>`Yes: it boils at ${Tb().toFixed(1)}°C because the air pressure up there is only about ${pressureAt(3724).toFixed(0)} kPa.`","no":"Not yet. Set Where to Aoraki / Mt Cook, then heat until the water boils.","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Set the syringe so the pressure is double normal air pressure (about 200 kPa) at 20°C.","check":"()=>Math.abs(pressure()-202.6)<12&&s.gasT===20","ok":"Yes. Halving the volume from 60 mL to 30 mL doubles the pressure.","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${pressure().toFixed(0)} kPa. Keep the gas at 20°C and change the volume.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Stop the experiment while the ice is exactly half melted.","check":"()=>{const S=stateFrom(s.Q,Tb());return S.zone===1&&Math.abs(S.liquid-0.5)<0.08}","ok":"Yes. Notice the thermometer: still 0°C, even though heat has been going in the whole time.","no":"Not yet. Go back to Melting, press Play, and pause when the chip shows about 50% melted.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"range","id":"power","label":"Hotplate"},{"type":"select","id":"alt","label":"Where"},{"type":"range","id":"vol","label":"Volume"},{"type":"range","id":"gasT","label":"Gas temp"}],"shell":"kit"},"energy":{"id":"energy","title":"The energy machine","href":"m/energy.html","desc":"A Sky Tower drop, a jug, a vacuum flask and a circuit: Ep, Ek, E = Pt, mcΔT, mL, V = IR and P = VI with worked numbers.","blurb":"Ep, Ek, E = Pt, mcΔT and circuits on the Sky Tower, a jug and a flask.","tags":["NCEA L1 PESS 92047","Year 10"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Physics · Energy","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 Physics, Earth and Space Science · energy in physical systems","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 PESS 92047","exam":"2026-11-12T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 12 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics, Earth and Space Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics-earth-and-space-science/"}],"truth":"Uses g = 9.8 N/kg, c = 4200 J/kg°C and L = 2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg. SkyJump height (192 m) and top speed (about 85 km/h) are from the operator's published information; the braking profile is simplified. Cooling curves are illustrative.","footer":"Covers AS 92047: mechanical, thermal and electrical energy in physical systems, using Ek = ½mv², Ep = mgh, W = Fd, E = Pt, E = mcΔT, E = mL, P = VI and V = IR.","mainLabel":"Energy scenes: a jumper on the Sky Tower, a jug, a vacuum flask and a circuit","kupu":[{"mi":"pūngao","en":"energy","ta":40075,"note":"","chip":["epower"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"lift, wire"},{"mi":"pūngao moe","en":"potential energy","ta":47720,"note":"","chip":["ep"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"lift, drop, wire"},{"mi":"pūngao neke","en":"kinetic energy","ta":47721,"note":"","chip":["ek"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"drop, wire"},{"mi":"teitei","en":"height","ta":7895,"note":"","chip":["height"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"lift"},{"mi":"wera","en":"heat","ta":9229,"note":"","chip":["heat"],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"heat, boil, flask"},{"mi":"paemahana","en":"temperature","ta":39927,"note":"","chip":["temp"],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"heat, boil, flask"},{"mi":"hiko","en":"electricity","ta":1171,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"circuit"},{"mi":"pūngao hiko","en":"electrical energy","ta":47713,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"circuit"}],"steps":[{"k":"lift","lab":"Lift it","cap":"Winch a 70 kg jumper 192 m up the Sky Tower. Every metre adds gravitational potential energy: work done against gravity.","exam":"Lifting the 70 kg jumper 192 m does work against gravity: W = Fd = (70 × 9.8) × 192 = 131,712 J, about 132 kJ. This work is stored as gravitational potential energy, Ep = mgh.","trap":"Using the mass as the force. The force is the weight: mass × 9.8.","big":"The big idea: work done lifting something becomes stored gravitational potential energy, Ep = mgh.","kupu":["pūngao moe","teitei","pūngao"]},{"k":"drop","lab":"Drop it","cap":"Imagine no wire and no air. All the potential energy turns into kinetic energy and the jumper hits about 220 km/h.","exam":"If no energy is lost, all the gravitational potential energy becomes kinetic energy: mgh = ½mv². The mass cancels, so v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.8 × 192) = 61.3 m/s, about 220 km/h.","trap":"Thinking a heavier jumper falls faster with no air. The mass cancels out, so the speed is the same.","big":"The big idea: energy is conserved. With no losses, Ep at the top equals Ek at the bottom, so ½mv² = mgh.","kupu":["pūngao neke","pūngao moe"]},{"k":"wire","lab":"SkyJump","cap":"The real SkyJump runs on a guide wire with a brake that holds the speed to about 85 km/h. The rest of the energy becomes heat in the brake.","exam":"At 85 km/h (23.6 m/s) the 70 kg jumper has Ek = ½ × 70 × 23.6² = 19.5 kJ. The jumper started with 132 kJ of potential energy, so about 112 kJ has been transformed into heat in the brake and the surrounding air by then.","trap":"Saying energy is lost. Say it is transformed into heat (and some sound), which spreads into the surroundings.","big":"The big idea: energy isn't lost, it's transformed. The brake turns most of the 132 kJ into heat, so only a little ends up as kinetic energy.","kupu":["pūngao moe","pūngao neke","pūngao"]},{"k":"heat","lab":"Heat it","cap":"A 2.2 kW jug heats 1 kg of water from 15°C. Watch the energy from E = Pt pour into the water as E = mcΔT.","exam":"To heat 1 kg of water from 15°C to 100°C needs E = mcΔT = 1 × 4200 × 85 = 357,000 J. A 2200 W jug supplies 2200 J each second, so t = E ÷ P = 357,000 ÷ 2200 = 162 s, about 2.7 minutes, if no heat is lost.","trap":"Mixing up c (specific heat, per °C) and L (latent heat, for a change of state).","big":"The big idea: power is energy per second. E = Pt tells you energy delivered; E = mcΔT tells you how far that energy heats the water.","kupu":["wera","paemahana"]},{"k":"boil","lab":"Boil it","cap":"At 100°C the temperature stops rising. Now the energy boils water away instead: E = mL.","exam":"Once the water reaches 100°C, the energy goes into changing its state, not raising its temperature. Boiling 0.1 kg of water needs E = mL = 0.1 × 2.26 × 10⁶ = 226,000 J, which takes 226,000 ÷ 2200 = 103 s.","trap":"Expecting the temperature to go above 100°C while water is boiling at sea level.","big":"The big idea: changing state takes latent heat without changing temperature. Boiling 1 kg of water takes 2.26 MJ, over six times the energy to heat it from 15°C.","kupu":["wera","paemahana"]},{"k":"flask","lab":"Keep it warm","cap":"Two teas at 92°C. The open mug loses heat by conduction, convection and radiation. The vacuum flask blocks all three.","exam":"The vacuum flask keeps the tea hot because the vacuum between its walls has no particles, so heat cannot travel across it by conduction or convection. The shiny silver walls reflect infrared radiation back towards the tea, and the lid stops convection currents and evaporation from the surface.","trap":"Saying a vacuum stops radiation. Radiation can cross a vacuum; the shiny surfaces reduce it.","big":"The big idea: to slow heat loss, block each way heat moves: conduction and convection need particles, radiation doesn't.","kupu":["wera","paemahana"]},{"k":"circuit","lab":"Circuits","cap":"Turn up the voltage or change the lamp's resistance. The current, the power and the brightness follow V = IR and P = VI.","exam":"With a 12 V battery and a 24 Ω lamp, the current is I = V ÷ R = 12 ÷ 24 = 0.5 A. The power is P = VI = 12 × 0.5 = 6 W, so the lamp transforms 6 joules of electrical energy into light and heat every second.","trap":"Writing the unit of power as J. Power is in watts: joules per second.","big":"The big idea: voltage pushes, resistance resists, and current is the result. Power is the rate electrical energy is transformed.","kupu":["hiko","pūngao hiko"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Use the sliders to set up each one, then press Check. The machine marks you with the same formulas it runs.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: write the formula, substitute with units, give the answer with units, then say what it means in the context.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Set the height so a no-losses fall reaches 50 m/s at the bottom.","check":"()=>Math.abs(Math.sqrt(2*g*s.h)-50)<0.6","ok":"()=>`Yes: h = v² ÷ 2g = 2500 ÷ 19.6 ≈ 128 m. You set ${s.h} m.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: from ${s.h} m the speed would be ${fmt(Math.sqrt(2*g*s.h),1)} m/s. Rearrange v = √(2gh) for h.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make the lamp use exactly 6 W from a 12 V battery.","check":"()=>s.V===12&&s.R===24","ok":"Yes: I = 12 ÷ 24 = 0.5 A, and P = 12 × 0.5 = 6 W.","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(s.V*s.V/s.R,1)} W. Use P = V²/R to find R.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the jug so 1.5 kg of water heats from 15°C to 100°C in under 3 minutes.","check":"()=>s.km===1.5&&1.5*C_W*85/s.kP<180","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(1.5*C_W*85/s.kP)} s with a ${fmt(s.kP)} W jug.`","no":"()=>s.km!==1.5?\"Set the water to 1.5 kg first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(1.5*C_W*85/s.kP)} s. You need more power.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"range","id":"m","label":"Mass"},{"type":"range","id":"h","label":"Height"},{"type":"range","id":"km","label":"Water"},{"type":"range","id":"kP","label":"Jug power"},{"type":"range","id":"V","label":"Voltage"},{"type":"range","id":"R","label":"Resistance"}],"shell":"kit"},"genes":{"id":"genes","title":"The gene pool machine","href":"m/genes.html","desc":"An island of birds: Punnett squares, mutation, small populations (the black robin), survival, migration and a DNA-profile paternity test.","blurb":"Punnett squares, mutation and the black robin on an island of birds.","tags":["NCEA L1 Chem & Bio 92022","Year 10"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Biology · Genetic variation","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 Chemistry and Biology · genetic variation · also Year 9–10 genetics","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 Chem & Bio 92022","exam":"2026-11-19T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 19 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Chemistry and Biology","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/chemistry-and-biology/"},{"label":"DOC: black robin","url":"https://www.doc.govt.nz/news/media-releases/2025-media-releases/more-habitat-needed-for-black-robins/"}],"truth":"The birds and their feather gene are invented to show the ideas; real traits often involve many genes. Black robin facts (five birds in 1980, all descended from Old Blue, about 445 today) are from the Department of Conservation.","footer":"Covers AS 92022: sources of variation (mutation, sexual reproduction), what changes it in populations (small populations, survival, migration), allele frequency, and gene tracking with DNA profiles.","mainLabel":"A population of birds with dark and pale feathers","kupu":[{"mi":"ira","en":"gene","ta":1778,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"cross, pool, mutation"},{"mi":"pītau ira","en":"DNA","ta":50987,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"mutation, dna"},{"mi":"pūira","en":"chromosome","ta":40067,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"cross"},{"mi":"mātāira","en":"gene pool","ta":50460,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"pool, small, migration"},{"mi":"mātai iranga","en":"genetics","ta":39853,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"cross"},{"mi":"taupori","en":"population","ta":38688,"note":"","chip":["N"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"pool, small, migration"},{"mi":"momo","en":"type, kind, species","ta":4184,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"rauropi","en":"organism","ta":38797,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"survival"}],"steps":[{"k":"cross","lab":"Variation","cap":"Two dark parents can have a pale chick. Each parent passes on one of its two alleles at random, so sexual reproduction reshuffles variation every generation.","exam":"Each parent has two alleles for feather colour and passes one on, chosen at random during meiosis. Two Dd parents can both pass on d, giving a dd chick with pale feathers. The Punnett square predicts 25% pale chicks, but in a small brood the actual number varies by chance.","trap":"Treating the Punnett square as a guarantee. It gives probabilities, not exact counts.","big":"The big idea: meiosis and random fertilisation create new combinations of alleles, so siblings differ. That's variation from sexual reproduction.","kupu":["ira","pūira","mātai iranga"]},{"k":"pool","lab":"Gene pool","cap":"A big population of 500 birds. Every bird carries two alleles; together they make the gene pool. With nothing pushing it, the allele frequency stays steady.","exam":"The allele frequency of d is the proportion of all the feather-colour alleles in the population that are d. In a large population with no selection, migration or mutation, it stays about the same from one generation to the next.","trap":"Confusing allele frequency with the share of pale birds. Many d alleles are hidden in dark Dd carriers.","big":"The big idea: allele frequency is the share of all the alleles in a population that are one type. It changes only when something changes who survives or breeds.","kupu":["mātāira","ira","taupori"]},{"k":"mutation","lab":"Mutation","cap":"Mutation is the only source of brand-new alleles. Here a small mutation rate slowly turns d alleles into D.","exam":"A mutation is a permanent change in the DNA sequence of a gene. Mutations create new alleles, which is the original source of genetic variation. A mutation is only passed on if it happens in the cells that make gametes.","trap":"Saying mutations happen because an animal needs them. They're random.","big":"The big idea: mutations are random changes in DNA. Most do nothing, some harm, a few help. They're the original source of all variation.","kupu":["ira","pītau ira"]},{"k":"small","lab":"Small populations","cap":"In 1980 only five Chatham Island black robins were left. In a tiny population, chance decides which alleles get passed on, and alleles can vanish forever.","exam":"In a small population, chance events decide which individuals breed, so allele frequencies can change a lot between generations and alleles can be lost completely. The black robin fell to five birds in 1980, so today's population of about 445 has very little genetic variation, which makes it more vulnerable to disease.","trap":"Thinking a population that grows back also gets its variation back. Lost alleles only return through mutation or migration.","big":"The big idea: in small populations allele frequencies drift by chance, and alleles get lost. Every black robin alive today descends from one female, Old Blue.","kupu":["taupori","mātāira"]},{"k":"survival","lab":"Survival","cap":"A new predator spots pale birds more easily. Fewer pale birds survive to breed, so the d allele gets rarer, but never quite vanishes, because carriers hide it.","exam":"Pale dd birds are more likely to be eaten, so fewer survive to breed and pass on the d allele. Over generations the frequency of d falls. It falls slowly once it's rare, because most d alleles are in dark Dd carriers that aren't selected against.","trap":"Saying the birds change colour to survive. Individuals don't change; the population's allele frequencies do.","big":"The big idea: when one phenotype survives better, its alleles are passed on more. That's natural selection changing allele frequency.","kupu":["rauropi"]},{"k":"migration","lab":"Migration","cap":"Birds fly in from another island where nearly all carry D. Gene flow pulls this population's frequency towards theirs.","exam":"Migration brings new individuals into the population, and they breed and add their alleles to the gene pool. Because the migrants mostly carry D, the frequency of D increases. Migration can also bring in alleles that had been lost, increasing variation.","trap":"Forgetting that migrants must breed to change the gene pool.","big":"The big idea: migration moves alleles between populations: it can add new variation and makes populations more alike.","kupu":["taupori","mātāira"]},{"k":"dna","lab":"DNA profiling","cap":"Gene tracking: compare the chick's DNA bands with its mother and three possible fathers. Every band must come from one parent.","exam":"In a DNA profile each band is an allele at a marker. A chick inherits one allele per marker from each parent, so every band in its profile must match either the mother or the father. The male whose bands account for all of the chick's remaining bands is the father.","trap":"Looking for the male with the most matching bands overall. He must match every band the mother can't explain.","big":"The big idea: DNA profiling, sequencing and pedigrees let scientists track alleles through families, which is how conservation teams manage breeding in tiny populations.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Use the controls to answer each one, then press Check. The machine marks you with the same model it runs.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: name the source of variation (mutation, sexual reproduction) or the cause of change (small population, survival, migration), then explain how it changes the allele frequency.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Pick the chick's real father from the DNA profile.","check":"()=>s.gelPick===G.real","ok":"Yes: he carries every band the chick didn't get from its mother.","no":"Not that one. Find a chick band the mother doesn't have, then find the male who does.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Choose two parents that can never have a pale chick, but can have chicks that carry pale.","check":"()=>{const set=[s.pA,s.pB].sort().join();return set===\"DD,Dd\"||set===\"Dd,DD\"||set===\"DD,dd\"||set===\"dd,DD\"}","ok":"Yes: with one DD parent every chick gets a D, so all are dark, but some carry d.","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${s.pA} × ${s.pB} gives ${expPale()}. One parent must be DD and the other must carry d.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Run a population until one allele is lost completely.","check":"()=>s.p===0||s.p===1","ok":"Yes: with so few birds, chance alone removed an allele. Only mutation or migration can bring it back.","no":"Not yet. Make the population very small, press Play and watch the graph.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"range","id":"N","label":"Birds"},{"type":"range","id":"sel","label":"Pale birds eaten"},{"type":"range","id":"mig","label":"Migrants"},{"type":"range","id":"mu","label":"Mutation"},{"type":"select","id":"pA","label":"Mother"},{"type":"select","id":"pB","label":"Father"},{"type":"button","id":"breed","label":"Breed again"},{"type":"toggle","id":"pick0","label":"Male 1"},{"type":"toggle","id":"pick1","label":"Male 2"},{"type":"toggle","id":"pick2","label":"Male 3"},{"type":"button","id":"newgel","label":"New family"}],"shell":"kit"},"kokopu":{"id":"kokopu","title":"The kōkopu machine","href":"m/kokopu.html","desc":"A banded kōkopu in a shaded stream pool: a nocturnal rhythm on an actogram, free-running in constant darkness, hunting moths by the lateral line, eggs that wait out of the water for the next flood, and whitebait choosing adult odour in a choice chamber.","blurb":"Rhythms, the lateral line, a flood-timed hatch and whitebait navigation, with a banded kōkopu.","tags":["NCEA L3 Biology 91603","Year 13","Science"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Biology · Animal responses","crumb":"NCEA Level 3 Biology · responses of plants and animals to their external environment","badge":{"label":"NCEA L3 Biology 91603","exam":"2026-11-17T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Tue 17 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Biology past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/biology/"},{"label":"DOC: banded kōkopu","url":"https://www.doc.govt.nz/nature/native-animals/freshwater-fish/banded-kokopu/"},{"label":"NIWA: banded kōkopu","url":"https://niwa.co.nz/freshwater/nzffd/NIWA-fish-atlas/fish-species/banded_kokopu"}],"truth":"The pool, the clock and the numbers are simplified to show the ideas: the 24.7-hour free-running period, the 6-hour shift, the detection range and the choice percentages are illustrative. The banded kōkopu facts come from DOC, NIWA and published research: adults about 200 mm (up to 260 mm); mostly active at night; prey ripples sensed by the lateral line; spawning in streamside vegetation during autumn and early-winter floods, with eggs developing out of the water for 3–4 weeks and hatching on a later flood (Charteris et al. 2003); and whitebait attracted to the odour of adult banded kōkopu but not adult īnanga or kōaro, an attraction lost after 48 hours in 0.5 µg/L cadmium (C. F. Baker, University of Auckland PhD, 2000).","footer":"Covers AS 91603: biological rhythms and actograms (zeitgeber, entrainment, free-running period, activity periods), orientation and navigation cues (lateral line, odour, current), and a breeding response timed by an environmental cue.","mainLabel":"A banded kōkopu in a shaded stream pool","kupu":[{"mi":"kōkopu","en":"banded kōkopu (Galaxias fasciatus); also used for some other small native fish","ta":2868,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (sense 1 names *Galaxias fasciatus*)","stepsNote":"fish, ripples"},{"mi":"moeao","en":"nocturnal","ta":47541,"note":"","chip":["state"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"night, freerun"},{"mi":"piha","en":"gills (of a fish)","ta":5553,"note":"Also the blowhole of a whale.","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"fish"},{"mi":"rongo kakara","en":"sense of smell","ta":40229,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"fish, maze"},{"mi":"waipuke","en":"flood; to flood","ta":9067,"note":"","chip":["level"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"flood"},{"mi":"kukune","en":"embryo, fertilised egg","ta":11084,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"flood"},{"mi":"hekenga","en":"migration","ta":1044,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"maze"}],"steps":[{"k":"fish","lab":"The kōkopu","cap":"A banded kōkopu, found only in Aotearoa. Adults are about 200 mm long and live in shaded pools of small streams. Tap a part to see what it senses.","exam":"Banded kōkopu are endemic to New Zealand. Adults (about 200 mm, up to 260 mm) live in pools of small, shaded streams, shelter under banks, logs and boulders, and feed mostly at night on insects that fall onto the water.","trap":"Calling whitebait one species. Whitebait are the juveniles of several native fish, and banded kōkopu is one of them.","big":"The big idea: every response in this machine starts with a sense. Light reaches the eyes, water movement reaches the lateral line, and chemicals reach the nostrils.","kupu":["kōkopu","piha","rongo kakara"]},{"k":"night","lab":"Night shift","cap":"Lights on 06:00–18:00. Watch the actogram build: the kōkopu rests under the bank all day and comes out soon after dark.","exam":"The actogram shows the banded kōkopu is nocturnal: its activity period starts soon after the lights go off and ends around dawn. Light is the zeitgeber. Each day the light–dark cycle resets (entrains) the internal clock to a 24-hour period, so activity stays in step with night. Being active at night may let it feed on insects falling onto the water while lowering the risk from predators that hunt by sight.","trap":"Saying it's active because it's dark. Darkness is the cue that sets the clock; the internal clock drives the rhythm.","big":"The big idea: the kōkopu is nocturnal. The change from light to dark is the zeitgeber that entrains its biological clock, so activity starts at about the same time each evening.","kupu":["moeao"]},{"k":"freerun","lab":"No sunset","cap":"Switch the lights to constant darkness. With no zeitgeber the rhythm keeps going, but on its own period, so activity starts a little later every day. Then try a 6-hour shift.","exam":"In constant darkness there is no zeitgeber, so the rhythm free-runs. Activity still occurs in a regular cycle, but with a period of about 24.7 hours, so the onset is later each day and the actogram slants to the right. This shows the rhythm is endogenous. When a light–dark cycle returns, the clock is gradually re-entrained, shifting a limited amount each day.","trap":"Saying the rhythm stops in constant darkness, or that re-entrainment happens overnight. It keeps cycling, and it shifts a little each day.","big":"The big idea: a rhythm that continues without any cue is endogenous: it comes from an internal clock. Its free-running period is rarely exactly 24 hours, so it drifts until a zeitgeber entrains it again.","kupu":["moeao"]},{"k":"ripples","lab":"Ripples","cap":"Night in the pool. Tap the water (or drop a moth) and watch the kōkopu find it by the ripples. Then block the lateral line and try again.","exam":"The lateral line is a row of neuromasts along each side of the fish that detect water movement. Ripples from an insect struggling on the surface stimulate the neuromasts, so the banded kōkopu can locate and orient towards prey in the dark, when vision is limited.","trap":"Saying the fish sees its prey at night. It detects the water movement with its lateral line.","big":"The big idea: the lateral line senses water movement. At night the kōkopu turns towards the ripples of a struggling insect and strikes: a directional response to a mechanical stimulus. Block it in the model and the moth goes unnoticed.","kupu":["kōkopu"]},{"k":"flood","lab":"Flood cue","cap":"Autumn. Send a flood: the adults spawn among the flooded grass on the bank. When the water drops, the eggs sit out of the water. Send the next flood at the right time.","exam":"Banded kōkopu spawn in autumn and early winter during floods, laying eggs among flooded vegetation at the stream edge. When the water drops, the eggs develop out of the water for about 3–4 weeks, then wait. Hatching is triggered by the next flood that covers them, so the larvae are swept downstream to the sea, where they spend their first months.","trap":"Saying the eggs hatch after a fixed number of days. Development takes 3–4 weeks, but hatching waits for a flood.","big":"The big idea: an environmental cue times the response. Spawning happens on a flood, and hatching waits for the next one, so the larvae hatch straight into high water that carries them to the sea.","kupu":["waipuke","kukune"]},{"k":"maze","lab":"Choice chamber","cap":"Migrating whitebait in a choice chamber. Water flows down both arms; arm A comes from a tank of adult fish. Release whitebait and see which arm they pick. Change the adults, the flow or the water.","exam":"Migrating banded kōkopu whitebait swim against the current (positive rheotaxis). In a choice chamber they choose water carrying the odour of adult banded kōkopu, but show no preference for water from adult īnanga or kōaro. This species-specific chemical cue may help them select suitable stream habitat during their upstream migration. After exposure to a trace of cadmium, the preference disappears, so pollution can disrupt this navigation cue.","trap":"Saying they smell food. They are attracted to the odour of adults of their own species.","big":"The big idea: whitebait swim into the current, then follow the odour of adults of their own species. This chemical cue may help them choose streams where adult banded kōkopu already live.","kupu":["hekenga","rongo kakara"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Use the controls to answer each one, then press Check. The machine marks you with the same model it runs.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: name the stimulus, the receptor, the response and the type of response (taxis, rhythm, timed by a cue), then explain the advantage for survival or reproduction.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Make the rhythm free-run: keep the kōkopu in constant darkness for at least 5 days.","check":"()=>s.light===\"DD\"&&s.ddDays>=5","ok":"Yes: with no zeitgeber the onset drifts later each day. The rhythm is endogenous, with its own free-running period.","no":"()=>s.light!==\"DD\"?\"Not yet: set the lights to constant darkness and press Play.\":`Not yet: ${s.ddDays} day${s.ddDays===1?\"\":\"s\"} so far. ${s.playing?\"Keep it running.\":\"Press Play and keep it running.\"}`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Shift the light–dark cycle by 6 hours and keep going until the fish is re-entrained.","check":"()=>s.light===\"shift\"&&s.shiftDays>=3&&Math.abs(phaseErr())<1","ok":"Yes: the light cue reset the clock a little each day until activity started just after the new dusk.","no":"()=>s.light!==\"shift\"?\"Not yet: choose “Shifted 6 hours later” and press Play.\":\"Not yet: the onset is still catching up. Re-entrainment takes several days.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Hatch the eggs: spawn on one flood, let the eggs develop, then send the second flood.","check":"()=>s.eggs===\"hatched\"","ok":"Yes: the eggs waited out of the water until they were ready, then the next flood triggered hatching.","no":"()=>s.eggs===\"none\"?\"Not yet: send a flood so the adults can spawn in the flooded grass.\":s.eggs===\"dev\"?`Not yet: the eggs are ${Math.floor(s.eggAge)} days old. They need about 3 weeks.`:\"Nearly: the eggs are ready. Send a flood.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Show the whitebait's preference: get at least 20 choices with 65% or more choosing arm A.","check":"()=>{const m=mazeStats();return m.n>=20&&m.pref>=.65}","ok":"Yes: with flowing water and adult banded kōkopu in tank A, most whitebait chose the odour of their own species.","no":"()=>{const m=mazeStats();return m.n<20?`Not yet: only ${m.n} choices. Release more whitebait.`:\"Not yet: they're choosing at random. Which adults, and what water conditions, give them a cue to follow?\"}","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Start the record again"},{"type":"select","id":"light","label":"Lights"},{"type":"button","id":"moth","label":"Drop a moth"},{"type":"toggle","id":"ll","label":"Block the lateral line (lab test)"},{"type":"button","id":"flood","label":"Send a flood"},{"type":"button","id":"bank","label":"New autumn"},{"type":"select","id":"odour","label":"Tank A holds"},{"type":"toggle","id":"flow","label":"Water flowing"},{"type":"toggle","id":"metal","label":"Trace of cadmium (48 h)"},{"type":"button","id":"release","label":"Release 20 whitebait"},{"type":"button","id":"clear","label":"Empty the chamber"}],"shell":"kit"},"ecology":{"id":"ecology","title":"The ecology machine","href":"m/ecology.html","desc":"A shingle beach: walk a transect, link plant height to soil depth, explain distribution factors, and run a food chain with dotterels and stoats.","blurb":"Transects, plant distribution and a food chain on a shingle beach.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science · Ecology","crumb":"Year 9–10 science · ecosystems, distribution, food chains","badge":null,"papers":[{"label":"DOC: banded dotterels on shingle beaches","url":"https://www.doc.govt.nz/news/media-releases/2021-media-releases/banding-together-for-the-banded-dotterel/"}],"truth":"The transect data are made up for practice, following the pattern found on real shingle beaches. Population numbers in the food chain are illustrative, not survey counts.","footer":"Ecosystems, distribution of organisms and food chains are Year 9–10 science topics in the New Zealand Curriculum.","mainLabel":"Cross-section of a shingle beach with plants, soil and the sea","kupu":[{"mi":"pūnaha hauropi","en":"ecosystem","ta":40334,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"habitat"},{"mi":"nōhanga","en":"habitat","ta":4432,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"habitat, factors"},{"mi":"rauropi","en":"organism","ta":38797,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"chain"},{"mi":"momo","en":"type, kind, species","ta":4184,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"transect"},{"mi":"taupori","en":"population","ta":38688,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"transect, pyramid"},{"mi":"oneone","en":"soil","ta":4770,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"soil"},{"mi":"urutau","en":"to adapt","ta":40435,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"factors"},{"mi":"raupapa kame","en":"food chain","ta":40170,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"chain, pyramid"}],"steps":[{"k":"habitat","lab":"The beach","cap":"A shingle beach from the top (0 m) down to the sea (40 m). The plants change along the way. Your job: work out why.","exam":"The distribution of an organism is where it is found in an environment. It depends on abiotic factors, such as soil depth, light, temperature and salt spray, and biotic factors, such as food, predators and competition.","trap":"Calling soil or temperature biotic. Biotic means living things; abiotic means non-living conditions.","big":"The big idea: organisms are found where the conditions suit them. Abiotic factors (non-living: soil, water, light, temperature, salt) and biotic factors (living: food, predators, competition) decide the distribution.","kupu":["nōhanga","pūnaha hauropi"]},{"k":"transect","lab":"Walk a transect","cap":"Lay a tape from the top of the beach to the sea and place a quadrat every 5 m. Press Play to walk it and record the plant height in each quadrat.","exam":"The students used a line transect, measuring the height of the plants in a quadrat every 5 metres from the top of the beach towards the sea. The average plant height decreased from 42 cm at the top of the beach to 0 cm at 35 m, where no plants were found.","trap":"Describing the trend without numbers. Quote values from the data, with units.","big":"The big idea: a line transect with quadrats at regular intervals shows how organisms change across an environment.","kupu":["momo","taupori"]},{"k":"soil","lab":"Soil depth","cap":"The same transect, now measuring soil depth. Compare the two graphs: where the soil is deep, the plants are tall.","exam":"As the distance from the top of the beach increases, the soil gets shallower, from 18 cm at 0 m to 0 cm at 35 m. The plants also get shorter and fewer. This suggests deeper soil helps plants grow, because it holds more water and nutrients and gives roots something to anchor in.","trap":"Saying soil depth definitely causes the change. Other factors, like salt spray, also change along the beach, so say \"suggests\".","big":"The big idea (Merit and Excellence): link two sets of data and explain the link with science, not just describe it.","kupu":["oneone"]},{"k":"factors","lab":"Why there?","cap":"Pick a factor to see how it shapes where things live on this beach. Each one is a classic exam question.","exam":"Availability of nesting sites affects distribution because animals can only breed where there is a safe place to nest. For example, banded dotterels nest on open shingle above the high-tide line, so they are found at the upper part of shingle beaches.","trap":"Giving a factor without an example. These questions ask for a plant or animal each time.","big":"The big idea: for each factor, say what it is, how it changes across the environment, and give a real plant or animal example.","kupu":["urutau","nōhanga"]},{"k":"chain","lab":"Food chain","cap":"Who eats whom on the beach. Add stoats and watch the dotterels drop, and the insects they eat increase.","exam":"In the food chain plants → insects → banded dotterel → stoat, the plants are producers because they make their own food by photosynthesis. When stoats increase, they eat more dotterels and their eggs, so the dotterel population falls, and with fewer dotterels eating them, the insect population can increase.","trap":"Drawing arrows the wrong way. Arrows point to the eater: they show which way the energy goes.","big":"The big idea: a food chain shows the flow of energy. Change one level and the levels around it change too.","kupu":["raupapa kame","rauropi"]},{"k":"pyramid","lab":"Trophic levels","cap":"Each step up the food chain is a trophic level. Only about a tenth of the energy makes it to the next level.","exam":"Only about 10% of the energy at one trophic level is passed on to the next. The rest is used by the organisms for respiration and movement and is lost as heat. This is why food chains rarely have more than four or five levels.","trap":"Saying energy is recycled. Nutrients are recycled; energy flows through and is lost as heat.","big":"The big idea: energy is lost at every trophic level, mostly as heat from respiration, which is why there are far fewer top predators than plants.","kupu":["raupapa kame","taupori"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Use the controls to answer each one, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: describe the pattern with numbers, explain it with a factor, and give a named example.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Walk the transect to the first quadrat with no plants. How far from the top is it?","check":"()=>s.walk>=35&&s.walk<40","ok":"Yes: 35 m. Past here it is bare shingle with no soil, and salt spray from the waves.","no":"()=>`Not yet: you're at ${Math.floor(s.walk/5)*5} m. Press Play and pause at the first empty quadrat.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the number of stoats that gives the dotterels the best chance.","check":"()=>s.stoats===0","ok":"Yes: with no stoats the dotterel population is at its highest. That's why DOC and community groups trap predators around nesting beaches.","no":"Not yet. Which number of predators lets the most dotterels survive?","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Choose the factor that best explains why plants are tallest at the top of the beach.","check":"()=>s.factor===\"nutrients\"","ok":"Yes: deeper soil holds more nutrients and water, so plants grow taller.","no":"Not quite. Look back at the soil-depth data.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart walk"},{"type":"select","id":"factor","label":"Factor"},{"type":"range","id":"stoats","label":"Stoats"}],"shell":"kit"},"atoms":{"id":"atoms","title":"The atom machine","href":"m/atoms.html","desc":"Build atoms from protons, neutrons and electrons: shells, the first 20 elements, ions, isotopes, and Rutherford's gold foil.","blurb":"Shells, the first 20 elements, ions, isotopes and Rutherford’s gold foil.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science · Atoms","crumb":"Year 9–10 science · atoms, the periodic table, ions and isotopes","badge":null,"papers":[{"label":"Te Ara: Ernest Rutherford","url":"https://teara.govt.nz/en/biographies/3r37/rutherford-ernest"}],"truth":"Electrons are drawn as dots in fixed shells, a simplified model used at school. Sizes are not to scale. The gold foil simulation is a picture of the result, not a physics model.","footer":"Atomic structure, ions and isotopes are Year 9–10 science (matter and the periodic table) and lead into NCEA Level 1 Chemistry and Biology.","mainLabel":"An atom with its nucleus and electron shells","kupu":[{"mi":"ngota","en":"atom","ta":14992,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"build, foil"},{"mi":"karihi","en":"nucleus","ta":15725,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"build, foil"},{"mi":"iraoho","en":"proton","ta":40328,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"build"},{"mi":"iramoe","en":"neutron","ta":40330,"note":"","chip":["neut"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"build, isotopes"},{"mi":"irahiko","en":"electron","ta":40517,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"build, shells"},{"mi":"pūmotu","en":"element","ta":40052,"note":"","chip":["el"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"numbers"},{"mi":"tau iraoho","en":"atomic number","ta":40327,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"numbers"},{"mi":"tau karihi","en":"mass number","ta":40329,"note":"","chip":["mass"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"numbers"}],"steps":[{"k":"build","lab":"Build an atom","cap":"Add protons and the element changes. Protons decide what the atom is; neutrons add mass; electrons orbit in shells.","exam":"An atom has a nucleus containing protons (positive) and neutrons (no charge), with electrons (negative) moving around it in shells. A carbon atom has 6 protons, 6 neutrons and 6 electrons, so it has no overall charge.","trap":"Saying neutrons decide the element. Only the number of protons does.","big":"The big idea: an atom is a tiny nucleus of protons and neutrons with electrons around it. The number of protons is the atomic number, and it decides the element.","kupu":["ngota","karihi","iraoho","iramoe","irahiko"]},{"k":"numbers","lab":"Mass and atomic number","cap":"The small top number is the mass number: protons plus neutrons. The bottom number is the atomic number: protons.","exam":"Sodium has an atomic number of 11 and a mass number of 23. So it has 11 protons, 23 − 11 = 12 neutrons and, as a neutral atom, 11 electrons.","trap":"Adding electrons into the mass number. Mass number counts only protons and neutrons.","big":"The big idea: neutrons = mass number − atomic number. Electrons have almost no mass, so they're not counted.","kupu":["tau iraoho","tau karihi","pūmotu"]},{"k":"shells","lab":"Electron shells","cap":"Electrons fill shells from the inside out: 2 in the first, then 8, then 8. Watch calcium fill 2,8,8,2.","exam":"Calcium has 20 electrons arranged 2,8,8,2. It has four shells, so it is in period 4, and two electrons in its outer shell, so it is in group 2.","trap":"Putting 8 electrons in the first shell. The first shell only holds 2.","big":"The big idea: the electron arrangement gives the period (number of shells) and group (outer electrons) on the periodic table.","kupu":["irahiko"]},{"k":"ions","lab":"Ions","cap":"Take an electron away from sodium, or give one to chlorine. Atoms lose or gain electrons to get a full outer shell, becoming charged ions.","exam":"A sodium atom (2,8,1) loses its one outer electron to get a full outer shell. It now has 11 protons but only 10 electrons, so it is a sodium ion with a charge of 1+ (Na⁺).","trap":"Changing the protons to make an ion. Ions form by gaining or losing electrons only.","big":"The big idea: metals lose electrons to form positive ions; non-metals gain electrons to form negative ions. The protons never change.","kupu":[]},{"k":"isotopes","lab":"Isotopes","cap":"Same protons, different neutrons. Carbon-12 and carbon-14 are both carbon; carbon-14 is radioactive and is used to date old things.","exam":"Carbon-12 and carbon-14 are isotopes of carbon. Both have 6 protons, so they are the same element, but carbon-12 has 6 neutrons and carbon-14 has 8, so their mass numbers are different.","trap":"Saying isotopes have different numbers of protons. Then they'd be different elements.","big":"The big idea: isotopes are atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons, so they have different mass numbers.","kupu":["iramoe"]},{"k":"foil","lab":"Rutherford's foil","cap":"Fire alpha particles at thin gold foil, like Ernest Rutherford's team did. Most go straight through. A very few bounce back.","exam":"Most alpha particles passed straight through the gold foil, which showed that an atom is mostly empty space. A few were deflected or bounced back, which showed there is a very small, dense, positively charged nucleus that repels the positive alpha particles.","trap":"Saying the particles bounced off electrons. Electrons are far too light; it's the dense positive nucleus.","big":"The big idea: the gold foil experiment showed atoms are mostly empty space with a tiny, dense, positive nucleus. Rutherford grew up near Nelson.","kupu":["karihi","ngota"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Set the protons, neutrons and electrons for each one, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: show your counting. Protons from the atomic number, neutrons by subtracting, electrons from the charge.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Build a chloride ion, Cl⁻ (chlorine-35).","check":"()=>s.p===17&&s.n===18&&s.e===18","ok":"Yes: 17 protons, 18 neutrons, 18 electrons. One extra electron gives a full outer shell (2,8,8) and a 1− charge.","no":"()=>`Not yet: you have ${s.p} protons, ${s.n} neutrons, ${s.e} electrons.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Build carbon-14.","check":"()=>s.p===6&&s.n===8&&s.e===6","ok":"Yes: 6 protons and 8 neutrons give mass number 14.","no":"Not yet. Carbon always has 6 protons; the mass number is protons plus neutrons.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Build a neutral atom with three shells and a full outer shell.","check":"()=>s.p===18&&s.e===18","ok":"Yes: argon, 2,8,8. A noble gas, so it hardly reacts.","no":"Not yet. Which element fills its third shell exactly?","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"Clear screen"},{"type":"range","id":"p","label":"Protons"},{"type":"range","id":"n","label":"Neutrons"},{"type":"range","id":"e","label":"Electrons"}],"shell":"kit"},"shapes":{"id":"shapes","title":"The shape workshop","href":"m/shapes.html","desc":"Grid-paper shapes you can stretch: areas of parallelograms, triangles and trapeziums, circles unrolled, Pythagoras poured, parallel-line angles and prism volume.","blurb":"Areas, circles, Pythagoras, angles and prism volume on stretchy grid paper.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","NCEA L1 Maths 91947"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Maths · Measurement and geometry","crumb":"Year 9–10 maths · area, circles, Pythagoras, angles and volume · also NCEA Level 1 91947","badge":null,"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/mathematics-and-statistics/"}],"truth":"Shapes are drawn to scale on the grid (one square = 1 m), except the unrolled circle edge, which is drawn at half scale.","footer":"Area, circles, Pythagoras, angles with parallel lines and volume are Year 9–10 maths and are examined in NCEA Level 1 standard 91947.","mainLabel":"Shapes drawn on grid paper with their measurements","kupu":[{"mi":"horahanga","en":"area","ta":45667,"note":"","chip":["area","carea"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"para, tri, trap, prism"},{"mi":"tapawhā whakarara","en":"parallelogram","ta":45970,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"para"},{"mi":"tapatoru","en":"triangle","ta":7470,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"tri"},{"mi":"porowhita","en":"circle","ta":5893,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"circle"},{"mi":"pūtoro","en":"radius","ta":45887,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"circle"},{"mi":"paenga","en":"circumference, perimeter","ta":4870,"note":"","chip":["circ"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"circle"},{"mi":"tāroa","en":"hypotenuse","ta":7603,"note":"","chip":["hyp"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"pyth"},{"mi":"koki hāngai","en":"right angle","ta":45739,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"pyth"}],"steps":[{"k":"para","lab":"Parallelogram","cap":"Slide the top of the shape sideways. The area doesn't change, because the base and the perpendicular height don't change.","exam":"The area of the parallelogram is base × perpendicular height = 8 × 5 = 40 m². The slanted side isn't used because area depends on the height at right angles to the base.","trap":"Multiplying by the slanted side instead of the perpendicular height.","big":"The big idea: area = base × perpendicular height, for rectangles and parallelograms alike. Use the height at right angles to the base, not the slanted side.","kupu":["tapawhā whakarara","horahanga"]},{"k":"tri","lab":"Triangle","cap":"A triangle is exactly half of a parallelogram with the same base and height.","exam":"The area of the triangle is ½ × base × height = ½ × 8 × 5 = 20 m², which is half the area of the parallelogram with the same base and height.","trap":"Forgetting the ½.","big":"The big idea: area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height.","kupu":["tapatoru","horahanga"]},{"k":"trap","lab":"Trapezium","cap":"A trapezium has two parallel sides, a and b. Its area is the average of those sides times the height.","exam":"The area of the trapezium is ½ × (a + b) × h = ½ × (4 + 8) × 5 = 30 m².","trap":"Using a slanted side as the height.","big":"The big idea: area = ½ (a + b) × h. Add the parallel sides first, then halve, then times the height.","kupu":["horahanga"]},{"k":"circle","lab":"Circle","cap":"Press Play to unroll the edge of the circle. Its length, the circumference, is 2πr, a little over 6 radiuses.","exam":"For a circle with radius 3 m, the circumference is 2πr = 2 × π × 3 = 18.8 m, and the area is πr² = π × 3² = 28.3 m² (1 dp).","trap":"Using the diameter in πr². Halve the diameter first.","big":"The big idea: circumference = 2πr (a length, in m) and area = πr² (a space, in m²). Don't mix them up.","kupu":["porowhita","pūtoro","paenga"]},{"k":"pyth","lab":"Pythagoras","cap":"The two small squares on the short sides add up exactly to the big square on the longest side. Press Play to pour them in.","exam":"Using Pythagoras' theorem, c² = a² + b² = 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25, so c = √25 = 5 m.","trap":"Adding the sides before squaring. Square first, then add, then square-root.","big":"The big idea: in a right-angled triangle, a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse, the longest side opposite the right angle.","kupu":["tāroa","koki hāngai"]},{"k":"angles","lab":"Parallel lines","cap":"A line crossing two parallel lines makes matching angles. Change the angle and watch the others follow.","exam":"The two angles marked are corresponding angles on parallel lines, so they are equal: both are 62°. The angle next to it on the straight line is 180° − 62° = 118°.","trap":"Giving the angle without the reason. Geometry answers need the rule named.","big":"The big idea: corresponding angles are equal, alternate angles are equal, and co-interior angles add to 180°.","kupu":[]},{"k":"prism","lab":"Volume","cap":"A prism has the same shape all the way through. Its volume is the area of that shape times the length.","exam":"The cross-section is a triangle with area ½ × 8 × 5 = 20 m². The prism is 10 m long, so its volume is 20 × 10 = 200 m³.","trap":"Writing m² for a volume. Volume is in cubic units.","big":"The big idea: volume of any prism = area of cross-section × length. Units are cubed: m³.","kupu":["horahanga"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Set each shape up with the sliders, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: write the formula, substitute, calculate, and give units. That earns the marks even if the arithmetic slips.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Make a right-angled triangle whose longest side is exactly 10 m.","check":"()=>Math.abs(Math.hypot(s.ta,s.tb)-10)<1e-9","ok":"Yes: 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100, and √100 = 10.","no":"()=>`Not yet: √(${s.ta}² + ${s.tb}²) = ${f(Math.hypot(s.ta,s.tb),2)} m. Hint: double a 3-4-5 triangle.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make a trapezium with an area of exactly 36 m².","check":"()=>(s.a+s.b)*s.h/2===36","ok":"()=>`Yes: ½ × (${s.a} + ${s.b}) × ${s.h} = 36 m².`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ½ × (${s.a} + ${s.b}) × ${s.h} = ${f((s.a+s.b)*s.h/2)} m².`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Find a circle whose area is bigger than 100 m².","check":"()=>Math.PI*s.r*s.r>100","ok":"()=>`Yes: π × ${s.r}² = ${f(Math.PI*s.r*s.r)} m².`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${f(Math.PI*s.r*s.r)} m².`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"range","id":"b","label":"Base"},{"type":"range","id":"h","label":"Height"},{"type":"range","id":"sh","label":"Slide top"},{"type":"range","id":"a","label":"Top side a"},{"type":"range","id":"r","label":"Radius"},{"type":"range","id":"ta","label":"Side a"},{"type":"range","id":"tb","label":"Side b"},{"type":"range","id":"ang","label":"Angle"},{"type":"range","id":"len","label":"Length"}],"shell":"kit"},"civics":{"id":"civics","title":"The MMP and law machine","href":"m/civics.html","desc":"Fill a debating chamber with fictional parties: the 5% rule, electorates and overhang, forming a government, and a bill's journey to Royal assent.","blurb":"The 5% rule, electorates, forming a government and a bill’s path to Royal assent.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Social studies"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Social studies · Civics","crumb":"Year 9–10 social studies · civics: how government is formed and how a law is passed","badge":null,"papers":[{"label":"Electoral Commission: how MMP works","url":"https://vote.nz/"},{"label":"Parliament: how a bill becomes law","url":"https://www.parliament.nz/"}],"truth":"The parties and their votes are made up. Seat counting follows MMP's rules: 120 seats (71 electorates and 49 list seats in 2026), the 5% or one-electorate threshold, Sainte-Laguë allocation and overhang. Real elections have more detail, such as electorates won by independents.","footer":"Civics, including how New Zealand's government is formed and how a law is passed, is Year 9 social sciences content in the refreshed curriculum. This machine is non-partisan and does not represent any real party.","mainLabel":"Seats in Parliament coloured by fictional parties","kupu":[{"mi":"manapori","en":"democracy","ta":11071,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"govt"},{"mi":"Pāremata","en":"Parliament","ta":5295,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"vote, bill"},{"mi":"pōti","en":"to vote; a vote, an election","ta":5918,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"vote, electorate"},{"mi":"rōpū tōrangapū","en":"political party","ta":6909,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"vote, threshold"},{"mi":"rohe pōti","en":"electorate","ta":6860,"note":"","chip":["elecsum"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"electorate"},{"mi":"Pirimia","en":"Prime Minister","ta":5645,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"cabinet"},{"mi":"rūnanga o te kāwanatanga","en":"Cabinet","ta":13091,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"cabinet"},{"mi":"pire","en":"bill (a proposed law)","ta":13133,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"bill"}],"steps":[{"k":"vote","lab":"Party vote","cap":"You get two votes. The party vote decides how many of the 120 seats each party gets. Slide the votes and watch the chamber fill.","exam":"Under MMP each voter has two votes: an electorate vote for a local MP and a party vote. The party vote decides each party's share of the 120 seats, so a party with about 30% of the party vote gets about 30% of the seats.","trap":"Thinking the electorate vote decides the overall result. It's the party vote that sets each party's share.","big":"The big idea: MMP (mixed member proportional) means a party's share of seats roughly matches its share of the party vote.","kupu":["pōti","rōpū tōrangapū","Pāremata"]},{"k":"threshold","lab":"The 5% rule","cap":"The Lantern Party has 4.5% and no electorate seat, so it gets nothing. Now give it one electorate seat and watch it bring list MPs in with it.","exam":"A party only gets list seats if it wins at least 5% of the party vote or wins an electorate seat. A party on 4.5% with no electorate gets no seats, but if it wins one electorate it gets seats in proportion to its 4.5%.","trap":"Saying a party under 5% can never get into Parliament. Winning one electorate gets it in.","big":"The big idea: a party needs 5% of the party vote OR one electorate seat to share in the list seats.","kupu":["rōpū tōrangapū"]},{"k":"electorate","lab":"Electorates","cap":"71 MPs win a local electorate. List seats top each party up to its fair share. If a party wins more electorates than its share, it keeps them: an overhang.","exam":"Parties keep every electorate seat they win, and list seats are added until each party reaches its share of the party vote. If a party wins more electorate seats than its share, it keeps them and Parliament has more than 120 MPs. This is called an overhang.","trap":"Adding electorate and list seats separately. List seats fill the gap between a party's fair share and its electorate seats.","big":"The big idea: electorate seats come first, list seats top up. Extra electorate wins make Parliament bigger than 120.","kupu":["rohe pōti","pōti"]},{"k":"govt","lab":"Make a government","cap":"Tap parties to join the government. They need more than half the seats to pass laws and survive confidence votes.","exam":"To form a government, a party or group of parties needs a majority of seats in Parliament, more than half. Because MMP rarely gives one party a majority, parties usually form a coalition or agree to confidence and supply.","trap":"Thinking the party with the most votes automatically forms the government. It needs a majority, often with partners.","big":"The big idea: under MMP one party rarely has a majority, so parties usually work together in a coalition or with a confidence and supply deal.","kupu":["manapori"]},{"k":"cabinet","lab":"PM and Cabinet","cap":"The government's leader becomes Prime Minister and picks ministers. The biggest party outside government leads the Opposition.","exam":"The Prime Minister leads the government and chooses ministers, who are responsible for areas like health and education. The most senior ministers form Cabinet, which makes the government's main decisions. The Opposition questions and challenges the government.","trap":"Mixing up Parliament and the government. Parliament is all 120-plus MPs; the government is the parties with the majority.","big":"The big idea: Parliament makes laws; the Executive (PM, Cabinet and ministers) runs the country; the courts apply the law. That's the separation of powers.","kupu":["Pirimia","rūnanga o te kāwanatanga"]},{"k":"bill","lab":"A bill becomes law","cap":"Press Play to send a fictional bill through Parliament. It needs a majority at each reading. Try it with and without a majority.","exam":"A bill becomes law after it passes a first reading, is studied by a select committee, passes a second reading, is debated clause by clause in the committee of the whole House, and passes a third reading. It then receives Royal assent from the Governor-General and becomes an Act.","trap":"Leaving out the Governor-General. A bill isn't law until it gets Royal assent.","big":"The big idea: a bill goes through three readings and a select committee, then the Governor-General's Royal assent makes it an Act: the law.","kupu":["pire","Pāremata"]},{"k":"committee","lab":"Have your say","cap":"At select committee anyone, including you, can send a submission about a bill and sometimes speak to the MPs in person.","exam":"Citizens can participate in law-making by making a submission to a select committee. The committee hears submissions from the public and experts, and can recommend changes to the bill before it goes back to Parliament.","trap":"Thinking you have to be 18 to make a submission. Anyone can make one.","big":"The big idea: select committees are the main way ordinary people take part in law-making between elections.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Set up each one with the sliders and party buttons, then press Check. The machine counts seats with the real MMP rules.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: name the rule (5% threshold, overhang, majority), then explain what it does to the result.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Get the Lantern Party into Parliament without raising its party vote above 4.9%.","check":"()=>{const A=allocate();return s.vote[5]<5&&A.final[5]>0}","ok":"Yes: winning an electorate seat gets a party past the threshold, so its party vote counts.","no":"Not yet. Keep its vote under 5% and find another way in.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Make an overhang so Parliament has more than 120 seats.","check":"()=>allocate().total>120","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${allocate().total} seats. A party won more electorates than its party vote entitles it to and keeps them.`","no":"Not yet. Give a small party more electorate seats than its party vote would earn.","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Form a government with the smallest number of parties that still has a majority.","check":"()=>{const G=gov();if(G.g<G.need)return false;const A=G.A;const n=s.gov.filter(Boolean).length;\n      // any smaller combination with a majority?\n      for(let m=0;m<64;m++){const c=[...Array(6).keys()].filter(i=>m>>i&1);if(c.length>=n)continue;if(c.reduce((a,i)=>a+A.final[i],0)>=G.need)return false}return true}","ok":"Yes: a majority with as few partners as possible.","no":"()=>{const G=gov();return G.g<G.need?`Not yet: ${G.g} seats, ${G.need} needed.`:\"That works, but you could do it with fewer parties.\"}","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart bill"},{"type":"range","id":"v0","label":"Harbour vote"},{"type":"range","id":"v1","label":"Summit vote"},{"type":"range","id":"v2","label":"Forest vote"},{"type":"range","id":"v3","label":"Tide vote"},{"type":"range","id":"v4","label":"Plains vote"},{"type":"range","id":"v5","label":"Lantern vote"},{"type":"range","id":"e0","label":"Harbour electorates"},{"type":"range","id":"e1","label":"Summit electorates"},{"type":"range","id":"e2","label":"Forest electorates"},{"type":"range","id":"e3","label":"Tide electorates"},{"type":"range","id":"e4","label":"Plains electorates"},{"type":"range","id":"e5","label":"Lantern electorates"},{"type":"toggle","id":"g0","label":"Harbour Party"},{"type":"toggle","id":"g1","label":"Summit Party"},{"type":"toggle","id":"g2","label":"Forest Party"},{"type":"toggle","id":"g3","label":"Tide Party"},{"type":"toggle","id":"g4","label":"Plains Party"},{"type":"toggle","id":"g5","label":"Lantern Party"}],"shell":"kit"},"sky":{"id":"sky","title":"The Sun–Earth–Moon machine","href":"m/sky.html","desc":"An orrery for Aotearoa: seasons, NZ day lengths, Moon phases, tides and eclipses, with exam-ready sentences.","blurb":"Seasons, NZ day lengths, Moon phases, tides and eclipses on an orrery.","tags":["NCEA L1 PESS 92046","Year 9","Year 10"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Earth & Space · made for Aotearoa","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 · Physics, Earth and Space Science · also Year 9–10 science","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 PESS 92046","exam":"2026-11-12T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 12 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics, Earth and Space Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/subject/physics-earth-and-space-science"}],"truth":"A simplified model of the real sky, not a live feed. Sizes and distances are not to scale. Day lengths, noon Sun heights and Moon phases are calculated from standard formulas and are close to real values for 2026–27; tide sizes are relative, not metres for a real harbour.","footer":"Covers the observable patterns in AS 92046: day and night, seasons, Moon phases, tides, eclipses, and how they vary by place.","mainLabel":"Orrery: the Sun at the centre, the Earth on its orbit, the Moon around the Earth","kupu":[{"mi":"rā","en":"sun","ta":6405,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"spin, lat, eclipse"},{"mi":"marama","en":"moon","ta":3673,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"phase, tide, eclipse"},{"mi":"pou tāwhirowhiro","en":"axis of rotation","ta":40009,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"spin, tilt"},{"mi":"kaupeka","en":"season","ta":2441,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"tilt, season"},{"mi":"mata o te marama","en":"phases of the moon","ta":39883,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"phase"},{"mi":"tai pari","en":"high tide","ta":40275,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"tide"},{"mi":"tai timu","en":"low tide","ta":7187,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"tide"},{"mi":"āraitanga","en":"eclipse","ta":347,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"eclipse"}],"steps":[{"k":"spin","lab":"Spin it","cap":"The Earth turns once every 24 hours. Watch the dot for your place ride into the sunlight (sunrise) and out of it (sunset).","exam":"Day and night happen because the Earth spins on its axis once every 24 hours. The half of the Earth facing the Sun has day. As Aotearoa turns into the Sun's light we get sunrise, and as it turns away we get sunset.","trap":"Saying “the Sun moves across the sky” as the cause. It only looks that way because the Earth is turning.","big":"The big idea: the Sun does not go round us. The Earth spins 15° every hour, so each place takes turns facing the Sun.","kupu":["rā","pou tāwhirowhiro"]},{"k":"tilt","lab":"Tilt it","cap":"The axis leans 23.4° and keeps pointing the same way in space all year. Watch the axis on the orrery: it never swings round to follow the Sun.","exam":"The Earth's axis is tilted at 23.4° and points in the same direction in space as the Earth orbits the Sun. This means the Southern Hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun for part of the year and away from it six months later.","trap":"Drawing the axis tilting back and forth during the year. The tilt stays fixed; it is the Earth's position around the Sun that changes.","big":"The big idea: one tilt that never changes direction is enough to make the Southern Hemisphere lean towards the Sun in December and away in June.","kupu":["pou tāwhirowhiro","kaupeka"]},{"k":"season","lab":"Seasons","cap":"Run the year. In December the south leans in: sunlight hits Aotearoa more directly and the day lasts longer. In June it leans away.","exam":"In December the Southern Hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun, so sunlight hits New Zealand more directly and is concentrated on a smaller area, and the days are longer. More energy reaches each square metre for longer, so it is summer. In June we are tilted away, so the light is spread out and days are short, giving winter.","trap":"Blaming distance. The Earth is closest to the Sun in early January, which is summer for us but winter in the Northern Hemisphere, so distance cannot be the cause.","big":"The big idea: summer means direct sunlight plus long days, so more energy lands on each square metre for longer. Winter is the reverse.","kupu":["kaupeka"]},{"k":"lat","lab":"Compare places","cap":"Change the place. Invercargill swings from about 8½ to almost 16 hours of daylight. At the equator it barely moves from 12.","exam":"The further a place is from the equator, the more its day length changes through the year. On 21 December Invercargill gets about 15 h 49 min of daylight but Cape Reinga gets about 14 h 28 min, while at the equator day length stays close to 12 hours all year. This is because the tilt makes the circle of latitude cross more of the lit half of the Earth at higher latitudes.","trap":"Giving the pattern without numbers. Comparing two named places with real figures is what lifts an answer from Merit to Excellence.","big":"The big idea (Excellence): the effect varies with latitude. The further from the equator, the bigger the change in day length and Sun height through the year.","kupu":["rā"]},{"k":"phase","lab":"Moon phases","cap":"Half the Moon is always lit by the Sun. As it goes round us we see more or less of that lit half. From Aotearoa a growing Moon is lit on the left.","exam":"Half of the Moon is always lit by the Sun. As the Moon orbits the Earth we see different amounts of its lit half, which gives the phases. A full cycle of phases takes 29.5 days, a bit longer than the 27.3-day orbit, because the Earth has also moved along its orbit around the Sun.","trap":"Saying the phases are the Earth's shadow on the Moon. Earth's shadow only reaches the Moon during a lunar eclipse.","big":"The big idea: phases are about viewing angle, not shadow. The Moon orbits in 27.3 days, but phases repeat every 29.5 days because the Earth has moved on too.","kupu":["marama","mata o te marama"]},{"k":"tide","lab":"Tides","cap":"The Moon's pull makes two bulges of ocean. When the Sun lines up with the Moon (new or full) the bulges grow: spring tides. At right angles they shrink: neap tides.","exam":"The Moon's gravity pulls the ocean into two bulges, one facing the Moon and one on the opposite side. As the Earth spins through both, each place gets two high tides about every 24 h 50 min, so high tide is about 50 minutes later each day. At new and full Moon the Sun and Moon line up and their pulls add, giving spring tides with the biggest range. At first and last quarter they pull at right angles, giving neap tides with the smallest range.","trap":"Thinking spring tides only happen in spring. They happen twice every month, at new and full Moon.","big":"The big idea: the Sun's tidal pull is about 46% of the Moon's. Lined up they add, at right angles they partly cancel.","kupu":["tai pari","tai timu","marama"]},{"k":"eclipse","lab":"Eclipses","cap":"The Moon's orbit is tilted about 5°. Most months the new or full Moon sails above or below the Sun–Earth line. Run it until they line up.","exam":"The Moon's orbit is tilted about 5° to the Earth's orbit, so most months the new or full Moon passes above or below the line between the Sun and the Earth. An eclipse only happens when a new or full Moon occurs near the points where the two orbits cross. A solar eclipse needs a new Moon, when the Moon's shadow falls on the Earth. A lunar eclipse needs a full Moon, when the Moon moves into the Earth's shadow.","trap":"Asking why there isn't an eclipse every month and leaving out the 5° tilt of the Moon's orbit. That tilt is the whole answer.","big":"The big idea: an eclipse needs a new or full Moon that also sits where the two orbits cross. That only lines up in two short eclipse seasons a year.","kupu":["āraitanga","rā","marama"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Use the date slider and the place menu to set up each one, then press Check. The machine marks you using the same model that moves it.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: for Merit, explain why with a linking word (because, so, which means). For Excellence, compare how the effect changes with place or time, using numbers.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Give Invercargill its longest day of the year.","check":"()=>placeIdx===3&&decl(t)<-23.2","ok":"Yes. Around 21 December the south is tilted most towards the Sun, so Invercargill gets about 15 h 49 min of daylight.","no":"()=>placeIdx!==3?\"Pick Invercargill in the place menu first.\":`Not yet: daylight is ${hm(dayLen(-46.41,decl(t)))}. Find when the Sun is overhead at 23.4°S.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Find a spring tide.","check":"()=>tidePull(elong(t))>1.38","ok":"Yes. The Sun and Moon are lined up (new or full Moon), so their pulls add.","no":"()=>`Not yet: the Moon is ${phaseName(moonAge(t)).toLowerCase()}. Spring tides come at new and full Moon.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make an eclipse happen.","check":"()=>eclipse(t).includes(\"eclipse\")","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${eclipse(t).toLowerCase()}. The Moon is at a crossing point of the two orbits at the right phase.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${eclipse(t)===\"No\"?\"the Moon isn't new or full\":\"the Moon is \"+Math.abs(moonLat(t)).toFixed(1)+\"° off our orbit\"}. Try early February or early August 2027 on the slider.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"today","label":"Today"},{"type":"range","id":"date","label":"Date"},{"type":"select","id":"place","label":"Place"}],"shell":"kit"},"space":{"id":"space","title":"The space machine","href":"m/space.html","desc":"Fly through today's real solar system, switch to true distances and race a pulse of light to Neptune, watch the planets form, replay how moons were made, see Mars loop backwards in 2027, place real stars on an HR diagram, play out star life cycles, and compare apparent and absolute magnitude.","blurb":"The solar system, the HR diagram, star life cycles and magnitude, in 3D.","tags":["NCEA L2 ESS 91192","Year 12","Year 10","Science"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Earth and Space Science · Stars and planetary systems","crumb":"NCEA Level 2 Earth and Space Science · stars and planetary systems · also Year 10 Earth and space","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 ESS 91192","exam":"2026-12-01T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Tue 1 Dec, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Earth and Space Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/earth-and-space-science/"},{"label":"2025 paper (91192)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nqfdocs/ncea-resource/exams/2025/91192-exm-2025.pdf"},{"label":"2025 schedule","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nqfdocs/ncea-resource/schedules/2025/91192-ass-2025.pdf"},{"label":"2024 paper","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nqfdocs/ncea-resource/exams/2024/91192-exm-2024.pdf"},{"label":"2026 assessment specification","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nqfdocs/ncea-resource/specifications/2026/91192-spc-2026.pdf"}],"truth":"A model for learning. Planet positions are computed from JPL's Keplerian elements for approximate positions (valid 1800–2050) and JPL Small-Body Database elements for the dwarf planets; Mars's 2027 retrograde dates were checked against JPL Horizons. Planet, Sun and Moon facts: NASA Planetary Fact Sheets (updated 18 March 2025), NASA Saturnian Rings Fact Sheet, and NASA science pages (Kuiper Belt, asteroids, Moon formation, Mars moons, Triton, Pluto, dwarf planets, star types); moon sizes and orbits: JPL Solar System Dynamics. Moon counts change as new moons are found, so they're left out. Stars: apparent magnitudes, parallaxes and spectral types from SIMBAD (CDS); absolute magnitude = V + 5 + 5 log₁₀(parallax); temperatures from NASA (Sun), Ohnaka et al. 2013 (Antares) and SIMBAD's catalogued measurements (Sirius: 2018MNRAS.477.3343S; Sirius B: 2011ApJ.743.138G; Canopus: 1989ApJS.70.865R; Alpha Centauri A: 2015A&A.582A.49H; Proxima: 2012ApJ.757.112B; Betelgeuse: 2025A&A.693A.163T; Rigel: 2012A&A.543A.80F; Achernar: 2006MNRAS.371.252L; Arcturus: 2024A&A.684A.85V; Aldebaran: 2023A&A.676A.129H). The main sequence band is from Pecaut and Mamajek (2013). The HR diagram's luminosity scale uses the exam diagram's rule (5 magnitudes = 100 times, Sun +4.83), so it tracks visible light; very hot and very cool stars give out extra light we can't see. Magnitude definitions: IAU Office of Astronomy for Education glossary. Life-cycle thresholds: NASA. The formation timeline, moon replays, life-cycle tracks, belt objects and faint background stars are illustrative; bright background stars are real (SIMBAD, brighter than magnitude 5). Surfaces are generated impressions, not maps, and sizes and distances are not to scale unless the scale control says so. Spin is slowed when time runs fast.","footer":"Covers AS 91192: star characteristics and the Hertzsprung–Russell diagram, apparent and absolute magnitude, the role of gravity and energy in stars' life cycles, formation of planets and moons (accretion, capture, collision), and planetary system features (rings, retrograde motion, dwarf planets). Also Year 10 Earth and space: the scale of the solar system, stars, gravity and light-years.","mainLabel":"A 3D model of the solar system and nearby stars","kupu":[{"mi":"whānau a Tamanuiterā","en":"solar system","ta":13026,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"whetūao","en":"planet","ta":10295,"note":"Te Aka also lists aorangi.","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":"Te Aka also lists aorangi."},{"mi":"marama","en":"moon","ta":3673,"note":"Also means month.","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":"Also means month."},{"mi":"Matawhero","en":"Mars","ta":3844,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"tō ā-papa","en":"gravity","ta":12845,"note":"","chip":["balance"],"evidence":"balance","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"tau aho","en":"light year","ta":40314,"note":"","chip":["ly"],"evidence":"ly","stepsNote":""},{"mi":"whetū","en":"star","ta":10293,"note":"Sometimes also used for other bodies in the sky, such as comets.","chip":["star"],"evidence":"star","stepsNote":"Sometimes also used for other bodies in the sky, such as comets."},{"mi":"Takurua","en":"Sirius","ta":7316,"note":"As an ordinary word, takurua means winter.","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":"As an ordinary word, takurua means winter."},{"mi":"Rehua","en":"Antares","ta":6676,"note":"The star associated with summer.","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":"The star associated with summer."},{"mi":"Atutahi","en":"Canopus","ta":500,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"","stepsNote":""}],"steps":[{"k":"tour","lab":"Scale and light","cap":"Today's solar system, with every planet where it really is. Tap a world to fly to it. Switch to true distances to see how empty space is, then send a pulse of light from the Sun.","exam":"A light-year is a distance, not a time: it's how far light travels in one year, about 9.46 trillion km. Sunlight takes about 8 minutes 19 seconds to reach Earth, so we see the Sun as it was 8 minutes ago. The nearest star, Proxima Centauri, is about 4.2 light-years away, so the light we see left it over four years ago.","trap":"Calling a light-year a measure of time. It's a distance.","big":"The big idea: the solar system is mostly empty space. Light is the fastest thing there is, yet sunlight takes about 8 minutes to reach Earth and about 4 hours to reach Neptune. Distances to stars are so huge we measure them in light-years: the distance light travels in a year, about 9.46 trillion km.","kupu":["whānau a Tamanuiterā","whetūao","tau aho"]},{"k":"form","lab":"Formation","cap":"About 4.6 billion years ago a cloud of gas and dust collapsed. Press play or drag the time slider: the cloud flattens into a spinning disc, the Sun ignites, and grains clump into planets.","exam":"Gravity made the solar nebula collapse. As it shrank it spun faster and flattened into a protoplanetary disc, with the protosun at the centre. Gravitational potential energy turned into heat, and when the core was hot and dense enough, hydrogen nuclei fused into helium. In the disc, dust collided and stuck together (accretion) to form planetesimals. Inside the frost line only high-melting-point rock and metal could condense, forming the rocky planets. Beyond it, ices condensed too, so larger cores formed and their gravity pulled in hydrogen and helium to make the gas giants. The young Sun's solar wind then cleared leftover gas from the inner system.","trap":"Saying the planets formed from material thrown off the Sun. They formed in the disc around the young Sun, from the same cloud.","big":"The big idea: planets grow by accretion. Grains collide and stick; once a clump is big enough, its gravity pulls in more. Near the hot young Sun only rock and metal stayed solid, so the inner planets are small and rocky. Past the frost line, ice was solid too, so cores grew big enough to pull in gas: the giants.","kupu":["tō ā-papa","whetūao"]},{"k":"moons","lab":"Moons and rings","cap":"Moons form in three ways: accretion, capture and collision. Pick a case and watch it replay at the real planet. Rings and dwarf planets are here too.","exam":"The collision theory says a Mars-sized body hit the young Earth and threw molten debris into orbit, which clumped together to form the Moon. Evidence: Moon rocks are very similar to Earth's mantle rocks, even in their oxygen isotopes; the Moon was once covered in a magma ocean; and its rocks have little of the elements that boil off when heated, as you'd expect after a very hot impact. Capture can't easily explain the Moon's large size or Earth-like rocks.","trap":"Saying the Moon was captured. Capture fits small, lumpy moons like Phobos and Deimos, or backwards moons like Triton, not our Moon.","big":"The big idea: a moon's orbit and make-up are clues to how it formed. Moons that grew in a disc (accretion) orbit in the planet's equator plane, the same way it spins. Captured moons are often small, lumpy or orbit backwards. A giant collision explains why our Moon's rocks match Earth's.","kupu":["marama"]},{"k":"retro","lab":"Retrograde motion","cap":"Earth laps Mars about every 26 months. As we overtake it, Mars seems to drift backwards against the stars. These are the real dates: watch the view from Earth below.","exam":"Mars's retrograde motion is apparent, not real. Earth is closer to the Sun and moves faster in its orbit, so about every 26 months it catches up with and overtakes Mars. As it does, our line of sight to Mars swings backwards against the much more distant stars, so Mars seems to move west for a few weeks before moving east again. In 2027 this happens from about 11 January to 2 April.","trap":"Saying Mars actually turns around in its orbit. It never does; the loop is an effect of watching from a planet that's overtaking it.","big":"The big idea: retrograde motion is how Mars looks from a moving Earth, not how it really moves. Every planet orbits the Sun the same way all the time. (Some things really do go backwards: Venus spins backwards, and Triton orbits Neptune backwards.)","kupu":["Matawhero","whetūao"]},{"k":"hr","lab":"HR diagram","cap":"Every star is a point on this diagram: temperature along the bottom (hottest on the left), luminosity up the side. Tap a real star to read it, or build your own with the sliders.","exam":"Antares is cool, about 3,700 K, so it's red, yet it's more than 10,000 times as luminous as the Sun. A cool surface gives out less energy per square metre, so to be that luminous Antares must have a huge surface area: it's a red supergiant near the end of its life, fusing helium and heavier elements. Sirius B is hot, about 26,000 K, but very dim, so it must be tiny: a white dwarf.","trap":"Reading the temperature axis the wrong way. On an HR diagram temperature increases to the left. And on the magnitude scale, brighter stars have smaller or more negative numbers.","big":"The big idea: a star's place on the HR diagram tells you its temperature and colour, its luminosity, and so its size and life stage. Most stars, including the Sun, sit on the main sequence, fusing hydrogen into helium.","kupu":["whetū","Takurua","Rehua","Atutahi"]},{"k":"life","lab":"Life cycles","cap":"A star is a tug of war: gravity pulls in, and energy from fusion pushes out. Choose a mass, then play its life and watch which side wins.","exam":"On the main sequence, the outward pressure from energy released by hydrogen fusion balances gravity. When the core runs out of hydrogen, fusion there stops and gravity makes the core contract and heat up until helium nuclei fuse into carbon; the outer layers expand and cool into a red giant. A star of less than about 8 solar masses then sheds its outer layers as a planetary nebula, leaving a white dwarf. A more massive star fuses heavier elements up to iron. Fusing iron releases no energy, so gravity wins: the core collapses and the star explodes as a supernova, leaving a neutron star or a black hole.","trap":"Saying stars 'burn' their fuel, or that a very massive star can end as a white dwarf. It's nuclear fusion, not burning, and big stars end as neutron stars or black holes. NZQA's 2025 report flags both.","big":"The big idea: mass decides everything. Heavier stars squeeze their cores harder, fuse faster and die sooner. Stars under about 8 times the Sun's mass end as white dwarfs; heavier ones explode as supernovae and leave a neutron star or, for the heaviest, a black hole.","kupu":["tō ā-papa","whetū","Rehua"]},{"k":"mag","lab":"Magnitude","cap":"How bright a star looks depends on how much light it gives out and how far away it is. Move every star to the same distance, 10 parsecs, to compare them fairly.","exam":"Sirius has an apparent magnitude of −1.46 and Antares about +0.9, so Sirius looks brighter from Earth. But Sirius is only 8.6 light-years away and Antares is about 550. Their absolute magnitudes, about +1.4 for Sirius and −5.2 for Antares, compare them at the same distance, and show that Antares gives out far more light. Sirius only looks brighter because it's much closer.","trap":"Thinking a bigger magnitude number means brighter. It's the other way round: −1 is brighter than +1.","big":"The big idea: apparent magnitude is how bright a star looks from Earth. Absolute magnitude is how bright it would look from 10 parsecs (32.6 light-years), so it compares the stars' real light output. Smaller or more negative numbers mean brighter; 5 magnitudes is 100 times the brightness.","kupu":["tau aho","Takurua","Rehua"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Use the controls to answer each one, then press Check. The machine marks you with the same model it runs.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: NZQA's HR diagram has temperature along the bottom and both luminosity and absolute magnitude up the side. Read a single value off each scale, not a range, and link every reading to an explanation: fusion, gravity, mass or distance.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Switch to true distances, send light from the Sun, and fly to Neptune to see how long the light takes to get there.","check":"()=>s.scale!==\"display\"&&s.focus===\"neptune\"&&s.lightT0!=null&&lightAU()>=rOf(ephem(jdOf(s.days)).neptune)","ok":"()=>`Yes: sunlight took ${fmtLight(lightSec(rOf(ephem(jdOf(s.days)).neptune)))} to reach Neptune, ${num(rOf(ephem(jdOf(s.days)).neptune),1)} AU away. Even at the speed of light, that's over four hours.`","no":"()=>s.scale===\"display\"?\"Not yet: choose True distances on the Scale control first.\":s.lightT0==null?\"Not yet: press Send light from the Sun.\":s.focus!==\"neptune\"?\"Not yet: fly to Neptune.\":\"Nearly: press play and wait for the light to arrive at Neptune.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make Mars appear to move backwards (west) against the stars.","check":"()=>marsRate(s.days)<0","ok":"()=>`Yes: on ${fmtDate(s.days)} Earth is overtaking Mars, so Mars seems to drift west. It's an effect of our moving viewpoint, not a real change of direction.`","no":"Not yet: Mars is moving east. Move the date to when Earth overtakes Mars, early in 2027.","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Build a star that is more luminous than the Sun but cooler.","check":"()=>s.pT<5772&&s.pL>1","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${num(Math.round(s.pT/10)*10)} K and ${fmtL(s.pL)} times the Sun's luminosity. A cooler surface gives out less light from each square metre, so to be brighter it must be bigger than the Sun. It sits in the ${regionOf(s.pT,mOfLum(s.pL))} part of the diagram.`","no":"()=>s.pT>=5772?\"Not yet: make it cooler than the Sun (5,772 K). Slide the temperature to the right on the diagram.\":\"Not yet: make it more luminous than the Sun (more than 1 × Sun).\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Pick a star that looks brighter than Antares from Earth but gives out less light.","check":"()=>{const x=SBY[s.magPick],a=SBY.antares;return x.m<a.m&&x.M>a.M}","ok":"()=>{const x=SBY[s.magPick];return `Yes: ${x.n} looks brighter (apparent ${fmtMag(x.m)} vs +0.91) because it's ${x.id===\"sun\"?\"so close\":\"closer\"}, but its absolute magnitude (${fmtMag(x.M)}) shows it gives out less light than Antares (−5.24).`}","no":"()=>{const x=SBY[s.magPick],a=SBY.antares;return x.id===\"antares\"?\"That's Antares itself. Compare it with another star.\":x.m>=a.m?`Not yet: ${x.n} (apparent ${fmtMag(x.m)}) doesn't look brighter than Antares. Smaller magnitude = brighter.`:`Not yet: ${x.n} looks brighter, but its absolute magnitude (${fmtMag(x.M)}) is even more negative than Antares's, so it gives out more light.`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"select","id":"speed","label":"Time"},{"type":"select","id":"scale","label":"Scale"},{"type":"select","id":"focus","label":"Fly to"},{"type":"button","id":"light","label":"Send light from the Sun"},{"type":"range","id":"form","label":"Time"},{"type":"select","id":"case","label":"Show"},{"type":"range","id":"replay","label":"Replay"},{"type":"range","id":"date","label":"Date"},{"type":"select","id":"hrPick","label":"Star"},{"type":"range","id":"pT","label":"Your star: temperature"},{"type":"range","id":"pL","label":"Your star: luminosity"},{"type":"select","id":"mass","label":"Mass"},{"type":"range","id":"life","label":"Life"},{"type":"toggle","id":"core","label":"Show the core"},{"type":"select","id":"magPick","label":"Star"},{"type":"toggle","id":"same","label":"Move every star to 10 parsecs"}],"shell":"kit"},"data":{"id":"data","title":"The data detective","href":"m/data.html","desc":"Read a dot plot, turn it into a box plot, compare two groups, catch a graph with a sneaky axis, see how a biased sample misleads, and decide when to use rates instead of counts.","blurb":"Dot plots, box plots, sneaky graphs, biased samples, and rates vs counts.","tags":["NCEA L1 Maths 91946","Year 11"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Maths and stats · Data","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 Mathematics and Statistics · interpreting data","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 Maths 91946","exam":"2026-11-10T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Tue 10 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/mathematics-and-statistics/"}],"truth":"Every number here is made up: the Kōwhai College screen-time survey, the two schools' sleep figures, the 400 students on the map and the two towns' cycling injuries. Quartiles use the medians of the lower and upper halves; outliers are more than 1.5 × IQR beyond the box. Your teacher may use a slightly different quartile method.","footer":"Covers AS 91946: extracting information from unseen graphs and tables, explaining variation, comparing groups, critiquing misleading graphs and biased samples, and using rates to make informed judgements.","mainLabel":"Interactive graphs of made-up survey data","kupu":[],"steps":[{"k":"read","lab":"Read it","cap":"Thirty Year 11s logged their school-day screen time. Drag the line and count who is below it.","exam":"Half of the 30 students spent less than 3.75 hours a day on screens on a school day, and half spent more.","trap":"A stack's height is how many students; its position along the axis is how many hours.","big":"Read the title, the axis and the units before you read a single dot.","kupu":[]},{"k":"spread","lab":"Middle and spread","cap":"Watch the dots drop into a box plot, then switch each part on and off.","exam":"The median school-day screen time was 3.75 hours, and the middle half of students used screens for between 3 and 5 hours (IQR 2 hours). One student's 11 hours is unusually high, which stretches the range to 9.5 hours.","trap":"The median is the middle student's value, not the middle of the axis or the mean.","big":"Five numbers (minimum, Q1, median, Q3, maximum) sum up a whole pile of dots.","kupu":[]},{"k":"compare","lab":"Compare","cap":"Same students, school days vs weekends. Compare the middles, then the overlap.","exam":"For these students, weekend screen time tends to be higher than school-day screen time: the median is 5.75 hours compared with 3.75 hours. The weekend median is above the whole school-day box and the boxes overlap by only 0.5 hours, so the shift is clear.","trap":"Don't write \"always higher\": some students use screens more on a school day than others do at the weekend.","big":"Look for shift (the medians) and overlap (the boxes) before you make a call.","kupu":[]},{"k":"mislead","lab":"Sneaky graphs","cap":"A newspaper drew this chart. Slide the axis start back to 0 and watch the gap shrink.","exam":"The vertical axis starts at 46%, not 0, so Kōwhai College's bar looks about 3 times taller, but 52% is only 1.08 times 48%. The difference is small and the graph exaggerates it.","trap":"Comparing bar heights without checking where the vertical axis starts.","big":"The same numbers can tell a different story depending on how they're drawn.","kupu":[]},{"k":"sample","lab":"Who did they ask?","cap":"400 students, and 38% walk or bike. Choose who to ask and see what your sample says.","exam":"Asking friends or people at a bus stop isn't random: friends often live near each other and everyone at a bus stop is catching the bus, so these samples give a biased answer. A random sample of 100 or more is much more likely to be close to the true proportion.","trap":"A bigger sample doesn't fix a biased one: a thousand people at the bus stop still miss the walkers.","big":"Who you ask matters more than how many you ask.","kupu":[]},{"k":"rates","lab":"Counts or rates?","cap":"Town A has twice as many injuries. Is it riskier? Switch to rates and decide.","exam":"Town A had more injuries (120 vs 60), but it also had 4 times as many cyclists. Per 1000 cyclists, Town A had 30 injuries and Town B had 60, so a cyclist was less likely to be hurt in Town A.","trap":"Comparing raw counts when the groups are different sizes.","big":"Rates (per 100, per 1000) let you compare groups of different sizes fairly.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Four cases on the board. Use the controls to crack each one.","exam":"Back every judgement with a number from the data and a reason about how the data were collected or drawn.","trap":"","big":"Read it, sum it up, compare it, then question it.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Move the line to the median, so exactly half the students are below it.","check":"()=>s.line===F.med","ok":"Yes: 3.75 hours. 15 students are below it and 15 above.","no":"()=>{const b=below(s.line);return b<15?`Only ${b} are below ${fh(s.line)} h. Move the line right.`:b>15?`${b} are below ${fh(s.line)} h. Move it left.`:`15 are below, but ${onL(s.line)} sit on the line. Nudge it a quarter-hour.`}","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make the newspaper's bar chart fair.","check":"()=>s.axis===0","ok":"Fair: with the axis at 0, 52% and 48% look almost the same, because they are.","no":"()=>`The axis still starts at ${s.axis}%. Slide it down to 0.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Take a random sample of at least 100 students that lands within 5 points of the true 38%.","check":"()=>s.method===\"random\"&&s.n>=100&&Math.abs(sampPct()-TRUE)<=5","ok":"()=>`Nice: ${pc(sampPct())}% from ${s.sample.length} random students.`","no":"()=>s.method!==\"random\"?`${{friends:\"Asking your friends\",online:\"An online poll\",bus:\"Asking at the bus stop\"}[s.method]} isn't a random sample. Switch to Random.`:s.n<100?`Only ${s.n} students. Ask at least 100.`:`${pc(sampPct())}% this time. Take a new sample: random samples wobble.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Show the rates and pick the town where a cyclist is less likely to be hurt.","check":"()=>s.view===\"rates\"&&s.pick===SAFER","ok":"Yes: Town A has 30 injuries per 1000 cyclists, Town B has 60.","no":"()=>s.view!==\"rates\"?\"Switch the view to rates first.\":s.pick?\"Look again at the rate per 1000, not the pile of injuries.\":\"Pick a town.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"range","id":"line","label":"Line"},{"type":"button","id":"replay","label":"Replay"},{"type":"toggle","id":"tMed","label":"Median"},{"type":"toggle","id":"tBox","label":"Middle half (IQR)"},{"type":"toggle","id":"tWh","label":"Range"},{"type":"toggle","id":"tOut","label":"Outlier"},{"type":"toggle","id":"tShade","label":"Overlap"},{"type":"range","id":"axis","label":"Axis start"},{"type":"toggle","id":"tBrk","label":"Axis break"},{"type":"select","id":"method","label":"Ask"},{"type":"range","id":"n","label":"Random sample"},{"type":"button","id":"resample","label":"New sample"},{"type":"select","id":"view","label":"Show"},{"type":"select","id":"pick","label":"Less risky"}],"shell":"kit"},"text":{"id":"text","title":"The text studio","href":"m/text.html","desc":"An original poem on a lightbox: light up its techniques, name them, grow a web of connotations, trace its shape to the turning point, build a theme, then plan two aspects of your studied text.","blurb":"Light up a poem’s techniques, trace its turning point, build a theme, plan two aspects.","tags":["NCEA L1 English 91927","NCEA L1 English 91925","Year 11"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"English · Unfamiliar and studied texts","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 English · unfamiliar texts (91927) and studied text (91925)","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 English 91927","exam":"2026-11-17T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Tue 17 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: English","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/english/"}],"truth":"“Low Tide” is an original poem written for assembly. The techniques, moods and themes shown are one valid reading; other well-argued readings can earn the same credit.","footer":"Covers AS 91927 (language features, structure, purpose and ideas in unfamiliar texts, with evidence) and AS 91925 (two specific aspects of a studied text, linked to purpose and the wider world).","mainLabel":"The poem Low Tide on a lightbox, with techniques highlighted","kupu":[],"steps":[{"k":"spot","lab":"Lightbox","cap":"Switch on a technique and watch it glow in the poem. Tap a glowing phrase to see its name.","exam":"The poet personifies the estuary, which “breathes out at dawn”, so the place feels alive but tired before the day has even begun.","trap":"Naming a technique earns little on its own: quote the exact words and say what they do.","big":"Techniques are the writer’s tools; marks come from explaining what each one does to us.","kupu":[]},{"k":"name","lab":"Name it","cap":"One glowing phrase at a time. Pick the technique that fits.","exam":"The simile “like a sheet of tinfoil” makes the mud look bright, flat and artificial, so the morning feels harsh rather than beautiful.","trap":"Similes and metaphors both compare; only a simile uses “like” or “as”.","big":"Naming is step one; the effect on the reader is what the exam rewards.","kupu":[]},{"k":"effect","lab":"Zoom in","cap":"Pick a phrase and watch its meanings grow: words, then a feeling, then a big idea.","exam":"The repetition in “grip and grip and grip” suggests desperate holding on, as if the mangroves are afraid to let go. It builds an anxious mood that hints at the grandfather holding on to time.","trap":"Skip “it makes the reader want to read on”: say what the words make us feel and think.","big":"Connotations are the feelings a word carries beyond its dictionary meaning.","kupu":[]},{"k":"shape","lab":"Shape","cap":"Slide through the poem. Tension builds as the tide goes out, then one short line turns it.","exam":"The poem turns at the short sentence “Then the water turns.” After it, the harsh, tense images give way to soft, calm ones, showing acceptance.","trap":"Structure isn’t “it has twelve lines”: explain where the mood shifts and why.","big":"A turning point is where a poem changes direction, mood or idea.","kupu":[]},{"k":"theme","lab":"Theme","cap":"A theme is a subject plus what the text says about it. Build one strong enough for the exam.","exam":"The poem suggests that time can’t be held back, however tightly we grip, but that it can be met calmly, like the tide that returns “soft, certain”.","trap":"One word, like “time”, is a topic, not a theme: add what the text says about it.","big":"Theme = subject + message + evidence.","kupu":[]},{"k":"aspects","lab":"Two aspects","cap":"For your studied text: pick two different aspects and link them to the writer’s purpose and our world.","exam":"In my studied text, the writer uses the setting and a recurring symbol to warn us about [the big idea], a warning that still matters today because [a link to our world].","trap":"“Character” and “characters” are one aspect twice: pick two different, specific aspects.","big":"Strong 91925 answers link both aspects to why the text was written and why it still matters.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Four quick wins. Tap a tile to jump back to its step.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Light up all seven techniques on the lightbox at once.","check":"()=>litCount()===7","ok":"All seven glowing: that’s a full set of evidence.","no":"()=>`${litCount()} of 7 on. Go to Lightbox and switch on the rest.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Name at least 5 phrases correctly in Name it.","check":"()=>score()>=5","ok":"Five or more named. Now say what each one does.","no":"()=>`${score()} right so far. Use the hints in Name it.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Put the Shape marker on the poem’s turning point.","check":"()=>s.line===10","ok":"Line 10: “Then the water turns.” The shortest sentence is the turn.","no":"()=>`Line ${s.line} isn’t it. Look for the shortest sentence, where the mood flips.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Build a theme statement with strength 3 of 3.","check":"()=>strength()===3","ok":"Subject, message and evidence: that’s a theme.","no":"()=>strength()===0?\"Pick a subject and a message in Theme.\":`Strength ${strength()} of 3. Make the message specific and true to the poem.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"toggle","id":"t_pers","label":"Personification"},{"type":"toggle","id":"t_meta","label":"Metaphor"},{"type":"toggle","id":"t_sim","label":"Simile"},{"type":"toggle","id":"t_rep","label":"Repetition"},{"type":"toggle","id":"t_sib","label":"Sibilance"},{"type":"toggle","id":"t_short","label":"Short sentence"},{"type":"toggle","id":"t_sym","label":"Symbol"},{"type":"button","id":"allon","label":"All on"},{"type":"button","id":"clear","label":"Clear"},{"type":"button","id":"prev","label":"← Back"},{"type":"button","id":"next","label":"Next phrase →"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_pers","label":"Personification"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_meta","label":"Metaphor"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_sim","label":"Simile"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_rep","label":"Repetition"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_sib","label":"Sibilance"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_short","label":"Short sentence"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_sym","label":"Symbol"},{"type":"select","id":"ef","label":"Phrase"},{"type":"button","id":"grow","label":"Grow it again"},{"type":"range","id":"line","label":"Line"},{"type":"select","id":"subj","label":"Subject"},{"type":"select","id":"msg","label":"Message"},{"type":"select","id":"a1","label":"Aspect 1"},{"type":"select","id":"a2","label":"Aspect 2"},{"type":"select","id":"purp","label":"Purpose"}],"shell":"kit"},"materials":{"id":"materials","title":"The materials lab","href":"m/materials.html","desc":"Test seven materials on a bench, zoom into their particles, see why some conduct and others shatter, pick the right material for the job, and weigh copper against aluminium for power lines.","blurb":"Test seven materials, zoom into their particles, pick the right one for the job.","tags":["NCEA L1 Chem & Bio 92023","Year 11"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Chemistry · Materials","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 Chemistry and Biology · physical properties of materials","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 Chem & Bio 92023","exam":"2026-11-19T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 19 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Chemistry and Biology","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/chemistry-and-biology/"}],"truth":"Property values are typical textbook values: wax melts over a range (about 46–68 °C) and HDPE softens near 130 °C depending on the grade. Diamond and graphite change only above about 3500 °C. Particle diagrams are 2D sketches of 3D structures. Power-line cables are sized for the same resistance (0.1 Ω per km) using resistivities of 1.68 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m for copper and 2.65 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m for aluminium; sag is drawn in proportion to weight per metre.","footer":"Covers AS 92023: metallic, ionic, molecular, polymer and covalent network materials, how their particles and forces explain density, conductivity, melting point, solubility, malleability and hardness, and how those properties decide their uses.","mainLabel":"The materials lab: samples, tests and particle diagrams","kupu":[],"steps":[{"k":"bench","lab":"Test bench","cap":"Pick a sample and run four quick tests: does it conduct, melt, bend or dissolve?","exam":"Copper conducts and bends without breaking because it is a metal. Sodium chloride doesn't conduct as a solid and shatters when hit because it is ionic.","trap":"Dense doesn't mean hard: diamond is only 3.51 g/cm³ but it's the hardest natural material.","big":"Every property comes from the particles and how strongly they're held together.","kupu":[]},{"k":"zoom","lab":"Zoom in","cap":"Zoom right in to the particles. Turn up the heat: they vibrate harder, then break free and melt.","exam":"Copper is made of positive metal ions in a sea of delocalised electrons. The strong attraction between the ions and electrons acts in all directions, so a lot of energy is needed to separate the particles and it melts at a high temperature (1085 °C).","trap":"Melting a molecular solid doesn't break the bonds inside molecules, only the weak forces between them.","big":"Strong forces in all directions mean a high melting point; weak forces between molecules mean a low one.","kupu":[]},{"k":"conduct","lab":"Why it conducts","cap":"A current needs charged particles that are free to move. Change the state and watch what flows.","exam":"Copper conducts as a solid and when molten because its delocalised electrons are free to move through the structure and carry the current.","trap":"Dissolved salt conducts because its ions move, not because of free electrons.","big":"No charged particles free to move means no current.","kupu":[]},{"k":"hammer","lab":"Hammer test","cap":"Push the top layers along. Metal layers slide and stay bonded; ionic layers line up like charges and crack.","exam":"Metals are malleable because layers of ions can slide over each other while the delocalised electrons keep holding them together. Ionic solids are brittle: when a layer shifts, ions with the same charge line up, repel, and the crystal splits.","trap":"Hard isn't the same as tough: diamond is the hardest material, but a sharp blow can chip it.","big":"Metallic bonding doesn't point in one direction, so metal layers can slide without breaking.","kupu":[]},{"k":"use","lab":"Pick for the job","cap":"Choose a job, then the material whose properties fit it best. Watch the ticks.","exam":"Copper suits electrical wire: its delocalised electrons are free to move, so it conducts very well, and its layers of ions can slide, so it can be drawn into thin wire without breaking.","trap":"Don't just name a property: link it to the particles, then to the job.","big":"Property, particles, use: an Excellence answer explains all three links.","kupu":[]},{"k":"tradeoff","lab":"Trade-off","cap":"Same resistance, two metals. Aluminium needs a thicker cable, but it's about half the weight.","exam":"Copper conducts better, but aluminium's density is only 2.70 g/cm³ compared with 8.96 g/cm³. For the same resistance an aluminium cable has about half the mass, so it sags less, pylons can be further apart and it costs less. Real lines wrap aluminium strands around a steel core for strength.","trap":"The best conductor isn't automatically the best choice: weigh up mass, cost and strength too.","big":"Evaluating means weighing more than one property against the job.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Four challenges. Use the controls here or go back to any step: your choices carry over.","exam":"For an evaluate question: name the property the use needs, explain it with the particles and forces, then weigh up a trade-off.","trap":"Saying “it's strong” isn't enough: say which particles, which forces, and what that means for the job.","big":"Structure decides properties; properties decide uses.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Find the material that conducts only when it's molten or dissolved, and make it conduct.","check":"()=>s.mat===\"nacl\"&&(s.cstate===\"molten\"||s.cstate===\"dissolved\")","ok":"Yes: sodium chloride. Its ions are fixed in the solid but free to move when molten or dissolved.","no":"()=>s.mat!==\"nacl\"?\"Not that material. Which one is made of ions?\":\"Right material. Now melt it or dissolve it in water.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make the ionic crystal shatter in the hammer test.","check":"()=>s.hamMat===\"ionic\"&&s.shift>=.9","ok":"Crack! Like charges lined up and repelled, so the crystal split.","no":"()=>s.hamMat!==\"ionic\"?\"Switch the lattice to ionic (salt) first.\":\"Push the top layers further, until like charges sit on top of each other.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Get a perfect fit for three different jobs.","check":"()=>fullJobs()>=3","ok":"Three perfect fits: property, particles, use.","no":"()=>`${fullJobs()} of 3 so far. Pick a job, then find the material that ticks every need.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Choose the metal for long overhead power lines, and why.","check":"()=>s.line===\"al\"&&s.why===\"light\"","ok":"Yes: aluminium. Same resistance at about half the mass.","no":"()=>s.line===\"cu\"?\"Copper conducts better, but compare the mass per km.\":s.line!==\"al\"?\"Choose a metal for the line first.\":s.why===\"cond\"?\"Aluminium's right, but copper actually conducts better. What's aluminium's real advantage?\":\"Aluminium's right. Now pick the reason that matches the numbers.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"select","id":"mat","label":"Material"},{"type":"range","id":"temp","label":"Heat to"},{"type":"select","id":"cstate","label":"State"},{"type":"select","id":"hamMat","label":"Lattice"},{"type":"range","id":"shift","label":"Push top layers"},{"type":"select","id":"job","label":"Job"},{"type":"select","id":"pick","label":"Material for this job"},{"type":"range","id":"len","label":"Line length"},{"type":"select","id":"line","label":"Use for the line"},{"type":"select","id":"why","label":"Because"}],"shell":"kit"},"claims":{"id":"claims","title":"The claim checker","href":"m/claims.html","desc":"Pull apart a hype post for an invented brain drink: who's behind it, how many people were tested, whether the test was fair, linked versus caused, and the words scientists would actually use.","blurb":"Pull apart a hype post: who’s behind it, sample size, fair tests, linked vs caused.","tags":["NCEA L1 Science 91923","Year 11","Year 9","Year 10","Science"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science · Claims and evidence","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 Science · science-related claims","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 Science 91923","exam":"2026-11-25T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Wed 25 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/science/"}],"truth":"FocusFizz, @study.with.tia, the post, the sources and every study and data point here are invented for learning. The sample simulation gives both drinks exactly the same effect (none), so every gap you see is chance.","footer":"Covers AS 91923: identifying a science-related claim, critiquing the evidence behind it (sample size, fair testing, correlation and causation), the source and channel, and the use of science language.","mainLabel":"An invented social media post about a brain drink, and the tools to check its claim","kupu":[],"steps":[{"k":"apart","lab":"Pull it apart","cap":"A post makes a big promise. Light up each lens: who's behind it, the hype, the claim and the evidence.","exam":"The claim is that drinking FocusFizz raises test scores by 20%. The only evidence is the company's own study of 12 students, compared with their last quiz instead of a control group, so it can't show the drink caused the change.","trap":"Don't just repeat the claim: say what evidence backs it, and what's missing.","big":"Every claim has three parts to check: what's claimed, the evidence behind it, and who's saying it.","kupu":[]},{"k":"source","lab":"Who says so?","cap":"Same claim, different messenger. Pick a source and watch the trust dial move.","exam":"The post was paid for by FocusFizz, which sells the drink, so the source has a conflict of interest. A peer-reviewed article by independent scientists that shows its data would be more reliable.","trap":"Lots of likes and views don't make a source reliable: popularity isn't peer review.","big":"Trust grows when a source is expert, independent, peer-reviewed and shows its data.","kupu":[]},{"k":"sample","lab":"How many?","cap":"Here the drink does nothing at all. Run studies and watch chance fake a result when groups are small.","exam":"With only 12 students, random variation alone could produce a 20% difference in scores. A much larger sample reduces the effect of chance, so its result would be more reliable.","trap":"A big percentage from a tiny sample isn't strong evidence: it may just be chance.","big":"Small samples swing by chance; bigger samples settle close to the truth.","kupu":[]},{"k":"fair","lab":"Fair test","cap":"Design a fair test for FocusFizz. Each feature you switch on fills the fairness meter.","exam":"A fair test needs a control group given water, students put into groups at random, the same test for both groups, and blinding so nobody knows who had which drink. The FocusFizz study had none of these.","trap":"Comparing with “last time” isn't a control: students may simply have studied more or had an easier quiz.","big":"A fair test changes one thing and keeps everything else the same.","kupu":[]},{"k":"cause","lab":"Linked or caused?","cap":"Ice-block sales and sunburn rise together. Does one cause the other? Reveal what's hiding.","exam":"Ice-block sales and sunburn cases show a strong positive correlation (r ≈ 0.93), but ice blocks don't cause sunburn. Both go up on hot, sunny days, which is the hidden factor.","trap":"Correlation doesn't prove causation: look for a hidden factor that drives both.","big":"Two things rising together doesn't mean one causes the other.","kupu":[]},{"k":"words","lab":"Science words","cap":"Hype words sound scientific. Flip each card to see what a scientist would write instead.","exam":"“Chemical-free” is misleading because everything, including water, is made of chemicals. Scientists use cautious words like “the results suggest” because one small study can't prove a claim.","trap":"“Natural” doesn't mean safe, and it doesn't mean it works.","big":"Science language is precise and careful; hype language is vague and certain.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Your verdict","cap":"Check the claim like an examiner: the source, the sample, a fair test, then your verdict.","exam":"Overall the claim is weak. It rests on a tiny study of 12 students with no control group, run by the company that sells the drink, and it uses hype words instead of data.","trap":"A judgement needs reasons: “it's weak” on its own won't earn the marks.","big":"Judge a claim on its evidence, not on how confident it sounds.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Pick a source that passes all four trust checks.","check":"()=>s.src===\"journal\"","ok":"Yes: expert, independent, peer-reviewed, and it shows its data.","no":"()=>`${SRC[s.src].name} passes ${score(s.src)} of 4. Which source is checked by other scientists and shows its data?`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"No real effect here. Run tiny studies (5 or 10 per group) until chance “finds” a gain of +15% or more.","check":"()=>s.swing","ok":"There it is: a big “effect” from pure chance. That's why 12 students isn't enough.","no":"Not yet. Set students per group to 5 or 10 and keep pressing Run a study.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Now run 10 studies in a row with 200 or more per group. Can chance still fake a big gain?","check":"()=>s.steady","ok":"No: all ten land close to zero. Big samples drown out chance.","no":"()=>`${bigCount()} of 10 so far with 200+ per group. Slide students per group to 200 or more and keep running.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Switch on all five fair-test features, then give your verdict on the FocusFizz post.","check":"()=>fairN()===5&&s.verdict===\"weak\"","ok":"Spot on: a fair test would settle it, and the post's evidence is weak.","no":"()=>fairN()<5?`${fairN()} of 5 features on. Which ones are still missing?`:s.verdict?\"Check your verdict: what does the evidence actually show?\":\"All five on. Now choose your verdict.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"toggle","id":"hlWho","label":"Who's behind it"},{"type":"toggle","id":"hlHype","label":"Hype words"},{"type":"toggle","id":"hlClaim","label":"The claim"},{"type":"toggle","id":"hlEv","label":"The evidence"},{"type":"button","id":"hlAll","label":"Show all"},{"type":"select","id":"src","label":"Source"},{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"run","label":"Run a study"},{"type":"range","id":"n","label":"Students per group"},{"type":"button","id":"clear","label":"Clear"},{"type":"toggle","id":"fControl","label":"Control group"},{"type":"toggle","id":"fRandom","label":"Random groups"},{"type":"toggle","id":"fSame","label":"Same test"},{"type":"toggle","id":"fBlind","label":"Blind"},{"type":"toggle","id":"fBig","label":"Big sample"},{"type":"toggle","id":"reveal","label":"Reveal the hidden factor"},{"type":"select","id":"word","label":"Hype word"},{"type":"select","id":"verdict","label":"Your verdict"}],"shell":"kit"},"vend":{"id":"vend","title":"The vending machine","href":"m/vend.html","desc":"A see-through 3D vending machine you can turn and take apart: a coin's free fall, the torque on a coil motor, a 350 g can dropping 0.80 m, the impulse when it lands, why \"twice as fast\" is a worse design, and what a day's refrigeration costs per can.","blurb":"A see-through 3D vending machine: free fall, torque, impulse and the cost of a cold can.","tags":["NCEA L2 Physics 91171","Year 12"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Physics · Mechanics","crumb":"NCEA Level 2 Physics · AS91171 Demonstrate understanding of mechanics","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 Physics 91171","exam":"2026-11-18T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Wed 18 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics past exams and exemplars","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics/"},{"label":"NZQA: AS91171 standard and specifications","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91171"}],"truth":"A stylised model of a spiral vending machine: cabinet 1830 × 990 × 850 mm, 6 trays × 5 coils, a 350 g can dropping 0.80 m from tray 3 and stopping in 0.050 s on a rubber pad (figures from the practice pack). Coil radius, tray friction (μ), motor speed, the coin's 0.15 m drop and the fridge powers (120 W compressor, 20 W lights, 10 W standby, 30 c/kWh) are estimates. g = 9.8 m s⁻², no air resistance, and the falls are slowed about three times so you can see them. Parts list: vend.parts.md.","footer":"Covers AS91171 (L2 Mechanics): kinematic equations and free fall, Ep and Ek, momentum and impulse (F = Δp/Δt), torque (τ = Fr) and rotational power (P = τω), and energy, power and cost (E = Pt). Add ?lite to the address for a still image instead of the 3D model.","mainLabel":"A see-through 3D vending machine","kupu":[{"mi":"tōpana","en":"force","ta":40375,"note":"","chip":["force","F"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"apart, coil, impact"},{"mi":"papatipu","en":"mass","ta":5179,"note":"Papatipu also means ancestral land. In science it means mass.","chip":["mass"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"look"},{"mi":"tere","en":"speed","ta":7953,"note":"Also means quick or fast.","chip":["v","coinv"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"coin, fall, impact, faster"},{"mi":"whakahohoro","en":"acceleration, speeding up","ta":12447,"note":"A general word for speeding up. Science classes may use another term, so check with your teacher.","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"coin"},{"mi":"tō ā-papa","en":"gravity","ta":12845,"note":"Te Aka also lists kume ā-papa.","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"coin, fall"},{"mi":"pūngao neke","en":"kinetic energy","ta":47721,"note":"Te Aka also lists pūngao konikoni.","chip":["ek"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"fall, faster"},{"mi":"pūngao moe","en":"potential energy","ta":47720,"note":"","chip":["ep"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"look"},{"mi":"teitei","en":"height","ta":7895,"note":"","chip":["h","coinh"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"look, faster"}],"steps":[{"k":"look","lab":"Look inside","cap":"A real spiral vending machine, drawn see-through so you can watch it work. Drag to turn it. Six trays of coils, a coin mechanism in the right-hand column, a delivery bin at the bottom and a fridge deck behind.","exam":"Gravitational potential energy Ep = mgh, measured from the level where the object stops. A 0.350 kg can on tray 3, 0.80 m above the landing pad, has Ep = 0.350 × 9.8 × 0.80 = 2.74 J relative to the pad.","trap":"Measuring h from the floor instead of from where the can stops. The h in Ep = mgh is the drop, so choose your reference level and say what it is.","big":"The big idea: every question in this machine is about one object at a time: a coin, a can, a coil. Pick the object, draw its forces, choose the equation.","kupu":["teitei","papatipu","pūngao moe"]},{"k":"apart","lab":"Take it apart","cap":"Slide the parts out to see how it fits together: the glass door and the service panel come forward, the trays spread out, the coils and cans slide out of their trays, the fridge deck drops below.","exam":"The motor exerts a torque on the coil, τ = Fr. Each turn of the coil moves the row of cans forward one pitch (70 mm), doing work W = Fd against friction. Once the front can leaves the tray its only significant force is gravity, so it accelerates downwards at 9.8 m s⁻².","trap":"Treating the whole machine as one object. Pick the object the question is about (the can, the coin, the coil) and draw the forces on it alone.","big":"The big idea: a vending machine is a chain of simple mechanisms. The motor applies a torque to the coil (τ = Fr), the coil does work pushing a can forward (W = Fd), gravity does the rest once the can leaves the tray.","kupu":["tōpana"]},{"k":"coin","lab":"The coin drop","cap":"Press play to post a $2 coin (10 g). It falls about 0.15 m past the validator's sensors, which time it to check it's real. How long does that take, and how fast is it going at the bottom?","exam":"Taking down as positive with u = 0: s = ut + ½at², so t = √(2s ÷ g) = √(2 × 0.15 ÷ 9.8) = 0.175 s, and v = u + at = 9.8 × 0.175 = 1.71 m s⁻¹ (or v² = u² + 2as). The mass of the coin does not appear, because all objects fall with the same acceleration.","trap":"Mixing units (15 cm must become 0.15 m) and choosing an equation that needs a quantity you don't have. If you have no time, use v² = u² + 2as.","big":"The big idea: the kinematic equations. With u = 0 and a = g you can find time from distance (s = ½gt²) and speed from either (v = gt, or v² = 2gs). The coin's mass never appears.","kupu":["tō ā-papa","whakahohoro","tere"]},{"k":"coil","lab":"The coil turns","cap":"The motor turns the coil one full turn and the whole row of cans moves forward 70 mm. The more cans in the coil and the rougher the tray, the more friction the motor must overcome, and the more torque it needs.","exam":"To push 7 cans with μ = 0.3, the friction to overcome is F = μmg × 7 = 0.3 × 0.350 × 9.8 × 7 = 7.2 N, acting at the coil radius r = 0.072 m, so the motor's torque is τ = Fr = 0.52 N m. At 30 rpm, ω = 2π × 30 ÷ 60 = 3.14 rad s⁻¹, so the power is P = τω ≈ 1.6 W.","trap":"Using rpm as ω. Convert: ω = 2π × (rpm ÷ 60) in rad s⁻¹. One full turn is 2π radians.","big":"The big idea: torque is force times radius, τ = Fr. Rotating things also have power: P = τω, with ω in radians per second.","kupu":["tōpana"]},{"k":"fall","lab":"The can falls","cap":"The front can tips off the tray and free-falls to the rubber pad. Change the drop height (which tray) and watch the speed it lands at. Tray 3 is the pack's 0.80 m drop.","exam":"v² = u² + 2as = 0 + 2 × 9.8 × 0.80 = 15.68, so v = 3.96 ≈ 4.0 m s⁻¹. The fall takes t = √(2h ÷ g) = √(1.6 ÷ 9.8) = 0.40 s. The can's kinetic energy on landing equals the potential energy it lost, ½mv² = mgh = 2.7 J.","trap":"Forgetting the square root (giving 15.7 m s⁻¹), or rounding too early. Keep 3.96 for the next step and round the final answer.","big":"The big idea: v² = u² + 2as. Dropped from rest, landing speed depends only on height and g, not on mass.","kupu":["tere","tō ā-papa","pūngao neke"]},{"k":"impact","lab":"Landing: impulse","cap":"The can is brought to rest by the pad. Momentum has to go to zero either way; the time it takes decides the force. Compare the rubber pad (0.050 s) with a bare steel tray.","exam":"Δp = mΔv = 0.350 × 3.96 = 1.39 kg m s⁻¹. F = Δp ÷ Δt = 1.39 ÷ 0.050 ≈ 27.7 N. The rubber pad lengthens Δt, so for the same change in momentum the average force is smaller and the can (and the machine) survives. On a steel tray the stop might take 0.010 s, five times the force.","trap":"Confusing impulse with force. The impulse (Δp) is fixed by the landing speed; the stopping time decides the force. If you ignore the can's weight (3.4 N) in the net force, say so.","big":"The big idea: impulse. Δp = FΔt, so F = Δp ÷ Δt. Longer stopping time, smaller force, for the same change in momentum.","kupu":["tōpana","tere"]},{"k":"faster","lab":"\"Twice as fast\"","cap":"A student claims the machine could dispense \"twice as fast\" by doubling the drop height. Set the drop to 1.60 m and compare with the 0.80 m baseline: speed, momentum, energy and time saved.","exam":"Doubling h to 1.6 m gives v = √(2 × 9.8 × 1.6) = 5.6 m s⁻¹, only √2 ≈ 1.41 times faster, because v ∝ √h, not h. The impact momentum rises by 41 % and the kinetic energy doubles (Ek = mgh). The can arrives only 0.17 s sooner but hits harder: louder, dented cans and broken bottles. \"Faster\" is a worse design outcome.","trap":"\"Double the height, double the speed.\" Speed grows with the square root of height; energy grows in proportion to it.","big":"The big idea: v ∝ √h. Doubling the height multiplies the landing speed by √2 ≈ 1.41, the momentum by 1.41 and the energy by 2. Excellence answers quantify the trade-off.","kupu":["tere","pūngao neke","teitei"]},{"k":"cold","lab":"Keeping it cold","cap":"Behind the bin, a compressor pumps refrigerant to the evaporator at the back of the trays; the fans move the air. It cycles on and off. What does a day cost, and what is that per can sold? (Powers are estimates.)","exam":"Energy = power × time. A 120 W compressor running 50 % of the day uses 0.120 kW × 12 h = 1.44 kWh; with 20 W of lights and 10 W standby all day the machine uses about 2.2 kWh a day, costing about 65 cents at 30 c/kWh. Spread over 40 vends that is about 1.6 cents a can.","trap":"kW and kWh. kW is a rate (power); kWh is an amount of energy. A 120 W compressor does not \"use 120 W per hour\".","big":"The big idea: energy = power × time. Power is a rate (W or kW); energy is an amount (J or kWh). Electricity is sold by the kilowatt-hour.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Five challenges. Use the controls from any step, then press Check. The machine marks you with the same physics that moves the figure.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: state the equation, substitute with units, then answer the question that was asked: a number with a unit, or a comparison with a reason.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Choose the drop height that makes the can land at about 4.0 m s⁻¹.","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.h-0.8)<1e-6","ok":"()=>`Yes: tray 3, h = 0.80 m. v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.8 × 0.80) = ${fmt(vFall(0.8),2)} m s⁻¹.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: from ${fmt(s.h,2)} m the can lands at ${fmt(vFall(s.h),2)} m s⁻¹. Rearrange v² = 2gh for h.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Keep the 0.80 m drop, and set a stopping time that keeps the average force on the can under 15 N.","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.h-0.8)<1e-6&&Fimp()<15","ok":"()=>`Yes: Δt = ${fmt(s.dt,3)} s gives F = ${fmt(pFall(0.8),2)} ÷ ${fmt(s.dt,3)} = ${fmt(Fimp(),1)} N. Same impulse, longer time, smaller force.`","no":"()=>Math.abs(s.h-0.8)>1e-6?\"Set the drop to 0.80 m first.\":`Not yet: F = ${fmt(pFall(0.8),2)} ÷ ${fmt(s.dt,3)} = ${fmt(Fimp(),1)} N. You need Δt > 1.39 ÷ 15 = 0.093 s.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Load the coil so the motor has to supply more than 0.5 N m of torque.","check":"()=>torque()>0.5","ok":"()=>`Yes: F = μ n m g = ${fmt(s.mu,2)} × ${s.n} × 0.350 × 9.8 = ${fmt(Ffric(),2)} N, so τ = Fr = ${fmt(Ffric(),2)} × 0.072 = ${fmt(torque(),2)} N m.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: τ = ${fmt(torque(),2)} N m. τ = μ n m g r, so add cans or make the tray rougher (μ).`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Double the drop to 1.60 m. By what factor does the landing speed rise, and the kinetic energy?","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.h-1.6)<1e-6","ok":"()=>`Yes: v = ${fmt(vFall(1.6),2)} m s⁻¹, ${fmt(vFall(1.6)/vFall(0.8),2)}× the 0.80 m speed (√2, not 2), while Ek doubles to ${fmt(ekFall(1.6),2)} J and the impact momentum is up ${fmt((vFall(1.6)/vFall(0.8)-1)*100)}%.`","no":"()=>`Set the drop to 1.60 m (twice 0.80 m), then check. v ∝ √h.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Bring the running cost under 1 cent per can sold (keep the compressor duty at 50%).","check":"()=>s.duty===50&&costVend()<0.01","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(kWhDay(),2)} kWh a day costs $${fmt(costDay(),2)}; over ${s.vends} vends that is ${fmt(costVend()*100,2)} c each. The fridge runs whether or not anyone buys.`","no":"()=>s.duty!==50?\"Set the compressor duty to 50% first.\":`Not yet: $${fmt(costDay(),2)} a day ÷ ${s.vends} vends = ${fmt(costVend()*100,1)} c a can. You need more than ${Math.ceil(costDay()/0.01)} vends a day.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"button","id":"vfront","label":"Front"},{"type":"button","id":"vside","label":"Side"},{"type":"button","id":"vinside","label":"Look inside"},{"type":"range","id":"explode","label":"Take apart"},{"type":"range","id":"h","label":"Drop height"},{"type":"range","id":"dt","label":"Stopping time"},{"type":"button","id":"rubber","label":"Rubber pad"},{"type":"button","id":"steel","label":"Steel tray"},{"type":"range","id":"n","label":"Cans in coil"},{"type":"range","id":"mu","label":"Friction μ"},{"type":"range","id":"rpm","label":"Coil speed"},{"type":"range","id":"duty","label":"Compressor on"},{"type":"range","id":"vends","label":"Cans a day"}],"shell":"kit"},"pinball":{"id":"pinball","title":"The Physics Lab pinball","href":"m/pinball.html","desc":"A playable, see-through tabletop pinball machine with real 2D physics on a 6.5° slope: call the launch speed from ½kx², the pop bumper's rebound (elastic bounce plus a kick), where the lock kicker's projectile lands (R = v² sin 2θ ÷ g sin 6.5°) and the flipper's impulse (J = FΔt), then an energy audit of a whole ball. Z and / flip, hold Space to pull the plunger.","blurb":"A playable see-through pinball table: call the launch speed, the bumper rebound, the kicker's range and the flipper's impulse, then watch the ball mark you.","tags":["NCEA L2 Physics 91171","Year 12"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Physics · Mechanics","crumb":"NCEA Level 2 Physics · AS91171 Demonstrate understanding of mechanics","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 Physics 91171","exam":"2026-11-18T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Wed 18 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics past exams and exemplars","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics/"},{"label":"NZQA: AS91171 standard and specifications","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91171"}],"truth":"A stylised tabletop pinball machine: playfield 520 × 800 mm sloping 6.5°, a standard 27 mm, 80 g steel ball treated as a sliding point mass (no rolling energy), a 400 N/m plunger spring with a light rod, pop bumpers with e = 1.0 and a 1.0 m/s solenoid kick, slingshots with e = 1.1 and a 0.45 m/s kick, rubber e = 0.7, steel rails e = 0.55, flipper rubber e = 0.6, rolling resistance μ = 0.001; g = 9.8 m s⁻², no air resistance. The physics runs at a fixed 240 steps a second whatever the frame rate, so the readouts are the same on every device. Spring, kick and restitution values are estimates typical of a small table. Parts list: pinball.parts.md.","footer":"Covers AS91171 (L2 Mechanics): elastic potential energy and springs (½kx²), kinetic energy and conservation of energy, momentum and impulse (J = FΔt = Δp), elastic and inelastic collisions, projectile motion (R = v² sin 2θ / g) and motion on a slope (g sin θ). Add ?lite to the address for a plain drawing instead of the 3D model.","mainLabel":"A see-through 3D pinball table","kupu":[{"mi":"tōpana","en":"force","ta":40375,"note":"","chip":["F"]},{"mi":"papatipu","en":"mass","ta":5179,"note":"Papatipu also means ancestral land. In science it means mass.","chip":["mass"]},{"mi":"tere","en":"speed","ta":7953,"note":"Also means quick or fast.","chip":["v","callV","measV","u0","uImp","callReb","measReb","kickV"]},{"mi":"whakahohoro","en":"acceleration, speeding up","ta":12447,"note":"A general word for speeding up. Science classes may use another term, so check with your teacher.","chip":["geff"]},{"mi":"tō ā-papa","en":"gravity","ta":12845,"note":"Te Aka also lists kume ā-papa.","chip":[]},{"mi":"pūngao","en":"energy","ta":40075,"note":"","chip":["kicks","losses","leftover"]},{"mi":"pūngao neke","en":"kinetic energy","ta":47721,"note":"Te Aka also lists pūngao konikoni.","chip":[]},{"mi":"pūngao moe","en":"potential energy","ta":47720,"note":"","chip":["Es"]},{"mi":"teitei","en":"height","ta":7895,"note":"","chip":["h","apex"]},{"mi":"koki","en":"angle","ta":2852,"note":"","chip":["kickTh"]}],"steps":[{"k":"look","lab":"Look inside","cap":"A tabletop pinball machine with a see-through sky-blue case, so you can watch the mechanism. Drag to turn it. A spring launcher, two flippers on pivots with solenoids under the playfield, three pop bumpers, two slingshots, a wire ramp into a lock with a kicker, a row of rollovers, a standup target, the outlanes and the drain.","exam":"On a slope of angle θ the component of gravity along the slope is g sin θ. The playfield slopes at 6.5°, so a free ball accelerates down the table at a = 9.8 × sin 6.5° = 1.11 m s⁻², whatever its mass. The 80 g steel ball weighs 0.78 N; the component along the slope is 0.089 N.","trap":"Using g = 9.8 m s⁻² for motion along a slope. Only the component along the slope accelerates the ball: g sin θ. (The component into the table, g cos θ, is what the playfield pushes back against.)","big":"The big idea: a pinball table is a mechanics exam laid out flat. The playfield slopes 6.5°, so the ball's acceleration down the table is g sin 6.5° = 1.11 m s⁻², and everything else (springs, collisions, impulses, projectiles) happens on that slope.","kupu":["tō ā-papa","whakahohoro","papatipu"]},{"k":"launch","lab":"Launch: ½kx²","cap":"Round 1. Pull the plunger back a distance x and the spring (k = 400 N m⁻¹) stores ½kx². On release that becomes the ball's kinetic energy. Call the launch speed before you fire; the table measures the real one at the moment the ball leaves the plunger.","exam":"The spring stores Ep = ½kx² = ½ × 400 × 0.040² = 0.32 J. If all of it becomes kinetic energy, ½mv² = 0.32 J, so v = √(2 × 0.32 ÷ 0.080) = 2.8 m s⁻¹. (The real launch is a little slower because the ball climbs 40 mm of slope during the stroke, and the plunger rod has mass.)","trap":"Forgetting to square x, or leaving it in millimetres. 40 mm is 0.040 m; ½kx² with x in mm gives an answer 10⁶ times too big.","big":"The big idea: elastic potential energy, Ep = ½kx², and energy conservation: ½kx² = ½mv², so v = x√(k ÷ m). Double x and you double v (and quadruple the energy).","kupu":["pūngao moe","pūngao neke","tere"]},{"k":"bumper","lab":"Bumper: momentum","cap":"Round 2. A pop bumper's cap is almost perfectly elastic (e = 1.0), and when its skirt switch closes a solenoid kicks the ball away at an extra 1.0 m s⁻¹. The test rig sends the ball straight up at bumper 3. Call the rebound speed; the readout shows the speed it will arrive at after slowing on the slope.","exam":"The ball (0.080 kg) arrives at 1.41 m s⁻¹ and leaves at 2.41 m s⁻¹ in the opposite direction. Taking away from the bumper as positive, Δp = m(v′ − u) = 0.080 × (2.41 − (−1.41)) = 0.31 kg m s⁻¹. Momentum is conserved in the collision: the bumper, bolted to the table (and the Earth), gains an equal and opposite momentum that we never notice.","trap":"Saying momentum is not conserved because the ball bounces back faster. The system is ball + bumper + table; the ball's momentum changes, the system's does not. And remember the sign: a reversal of direction makes Δp bigger than either momentum alone.","big":"The big idea: in a collision with something fixed and heavy, momentum is still conserved: the table takes the ball's momentum change. An elastic bounce reverses the ball's velocity (v′ = u); the kick adds to it. The change in momentum Δp = m(v′ + u) is what the bumper's impulse supplies.","kupu":["tere","tōpana"]},{"k":"ramp","lab":"Ramp: projectile","cap":"Round 3. The ball rides the wire ramp into the lock. The kicker then fires it across the sloping table at a speed v and angle θ to the landing line. On the slope it is a projectile with g_eff = g sin 6.5°, and it comes back to the line a distance R = v² sin 2θ ÷ g_eff along it. Call R, then fire.","exam":"Kicked at 0.50 m s⁻¹ at 35° to the line, with g_eff = 9.8 sin 6.5° = 1.11 m s⁻²: time of flight t = 2 × 0.50 × sin 35° ÷ 1.11 = 0.52 s, and R = 0.50 × cos 35° × 0.52 = 0.21 m (or R = v² sin 2θ ÷ g_eff = 0.25 × 0.94 ÷ 1.11 = 0.21 m). The apex is v² sin² θ ÷ 2g_eff = 0.037 m above the line.","trap":"Using sin 2θ as 2 sin θ, or plugging 9.8 in for g_eff on a slope. Also: the range formula only works when the ball lands back at the level it left from, which is why the lock sits on the landing line.","big":"The big idea: projectile motion is two independent motions. Along the line, constant speed v cos θ; across it, constant acceleration g_eff. Time of flight t = 2v sin θ ÷ g_eff, so R = v cos θ × t = v² sin 2θ ÷ g_eff. Biggest range at 45°.","kupu":["koki","tere","tō ā-papa","teitei"]},{"k":"flipper","lab":"Flipper: impulse","cap":"Round 4. Impulse is force × time, J = FΔt, and it equals the ball's change in momentum. The flipper is in contact with the ball for only a few milliseconds, so even a small impulse means a large force. Feed a ball, press Z as it arrives, and hit the standup target using as little impulse as you can.","exam":"The flipper changes the ball's velocity by 2.5 m s⁻¹ in 0.012 s. Impulse J = Δp = mΔv = 0.080 × 2.5 = 0.20 N s. Average force F = J ÷ Δt = 0.20 ÷ 0.012 = 17 N, about 21 times the ball's weight. The force is not constant during the contact; 17 N is the average.","trap":"Confusing impulse with force. Impulse is the whole effect of a force over the time it acts (N s, the same unit as kg m s⁻¹). The force is impulse ÷ time, so it depends on how long the contact lasts.","big":"The big idea: J = FΔt = Δp. The meter integrates the force the flipper applies over the contact time. Same impulse, shorter contact, bigger force; a well-timed gentle flip moves the ball with far less force than a wild one.","kupu":["tōpana","tere"]},{"k":"audit","lab":"Energy audit","cap":"Round 5. Energy is never lost, only moved. Play one whole ball and the table keeps the books: ½kx² in from the spring, energy added by every bumper, slingshot and flipper kick, energy taken by the rubber, the rails and rolling friction, and what is left when the ball reaches the drain.","exam":"Mechanical energy is not conserved in an inelastic collision: with e = 0.7 the ball keeps e² = 49% of the kinetic energy associated with its motion into the rubber; the rest becomes heat and sound. Momentum, though, is conserved in every collision, elastic or not: that is the difference between the two conservation laws.","trap":"Treating \"energy is conserved\" as \"kinetic energy is conserved\". Only elastic collisions conserve kinetic energy. Momentum is conserved in all of them (when no outside force acts during the collision).","big":"The big idea: conservation of energy as an audit. Kicks are work done on the ball (energy from the solenoids' electricity). Inelastic bounces turn kinetic energy into heat and sound. On this table the rubber and rails take far more than friction does.","kupu":["pūngao","pūngao neke","pūngao moe"]},{"k":"free","lab":"Free play","cap":"Three balls, full table, nothing to predict. Z and / for the flippers, hold Space (or the Pull button) to pull the plunger and release to fire. Bumpers 100, slingshots 50, rollovers 25, the lock 250, the standup target 300, the ramp 500. Your points from the rounds carry over.","exam":"A ball moving up the table at 2.0 m s⁻¹ climbs until its kinetic energy has become potential energy along the slope: ½mv² = m g sin θ × d, so d = v² ÷ (2 g sin θ) = 4.0 ÷ (2 × 1.11) = 1.8 m. Longer than the table, so it reaches the top; a 1.0 m s⁻¹ ball climbs only 0.45 m.","trap":"Reading \"height\" straight off a sloping table. On the slope the vertical rise is d sin θ, and the potential energy is m g d sin θ, which is why a pinball needs so little speed to reach the top.","big":"The big idea: everything you see now is the five rounds happening at once. Watch the speed arrow and the data card: the ball slows as it climbs (Ek → Ep), speeds up as it falls, loses a little at every rubber and gains at every kick.","kupu":["tere","teitei","pūngao"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Five challenges. Set the controls from any round, then press Check. The machine marks you with the same physics that moves the ball.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: name the law first (energy conservation, momentum conservation, impulse = change in momentum), then the equation, then the numbers with units. Say what your system is.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Set the plunger so the launch speed is 3.0 m s⁻¹ (within 0.1).","check":"()=>Math.abs(vLaunch(s.x)-3.0)<0.1","ok":"()=>`Yes: x = ${fmt(s.x)} mm. ½kx² = ${fmt(0.5*K*(s.x/1000)**2,3)} J = ½mv², so v = x√(k ÷ m) = ${fmt(vLaunch(s.x),2)} m s⁻¹.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(s.x)} mm gives v = ${fmt(vLaunch(s.x),2)} m s⁻¹. Rearrange ½kx² = ½mv²: x = v√(m ÷ k) = ${fmt(3*Math.sqrt(M/K)*1000)} mm.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Choose a test speed so the ball leaves bumper 3 at 2.5 m s⁻¹ (within 0.1). Remember it slows on the slope before it arrives.","check":"()=>Math.abs(uImpact(s.u0)+T.BUMPER.kick/1000-2.5)<0.1","ok":"()=>`Yes: it arrives at ${fmt(uImpact(s.u0),2)} m s⁻¹, bounces back at the same speed (e = 1) and the kick adds 1.0, so v′ = ${fmt(uImpact(s.u0)+1,2)} m s⁻¹. Δp = ${fmt(M*(uImpact(s.u0)*2+1),3)} kg m s⁻¹.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: v′ = u + 1.0 = ${fmt(uImpact(s.u0)+1,2)} m s⁻¹. You need u = 1.5 m s⁻¹ at impact; it loses v² = u₀² − 2 g_eff d over the 0.11 m run-up, so u₀ ≈ ${fmt(Math.sqrt(1.5*1.5+2*GE*0.1095),2)} m s⁻¹.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the kicker so the ball comes back to the line 0.25 m along it (within 0.015 m).","check":"()=>Math.abs(rangeOf(s.kickV,s.kickTh)-0.25)<0.015","ok":"()=>`Yes: R = v² sin 2θ ÷ g_eff = ${fmt(s.kickV,2)}² × sin ${2*s.kickTh}° ÷ ${fmt(GE,2)} = ${fmt(rangeOf(s.kickV,s.kickTh),3)} m.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: R = ${fmt(rangeOf(s.kickV,s.kickTh),3)} m. At ${s.kickTh}° you need v = √(R g_eff ÷ sin 2θ) = ${fmt(Math.sqrt(0.25*GE/Math.sin(2*s.kickTh*Math.PI/180)),2)} m s⁻¹.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Keep the speed and change only the angle so the range is as large as it can be.","check":"()=>s.kickTh===45","ok":"()=>`Yes: sin 2θ is biggest at θ = 45°, where R = v² ÷ g_eff = ${fmt(rangeOf(s.kickV,45),3)} m for ${fmt(s.kickV,2)} m s⁻¹.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: at ${s.kickTh}° sin 2θ = ${fmt(Math.sin(2*s.kickTh*Math.PI/180),2)}. The range is largest when 2θ = 90°.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"In the flipper round, hit the target with an impulse under 0.25 N s.","check":"()=>!!(s.round.flipper&&s.round.flipper.J<0.25)","ok":"()=>`Yes: your best shot was J = ${fmt(s.round.flipper.J,3)} N s in ${fmt(s.round.flipper.dt*1000,0)} ms, an average force of ${fmt(s.round.flipper.F,1)} N.`","no":"()=>s.round.flipper?`Not yet: your best is ${fmt(s.round.flipper.J,3)} N s. A later, softer flip (the bat moving slower when it meets the ball) delivers less impulse.`:\"Go to the flipper round, feed a ball and hit the target first.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"fire","label":"Fire"},{"type":"button","id":"feed","label":"Feed a ball"},{"type":"button","id":"newgame","label":"New game"},{"type":"button","id":"vlook","label":"Look"},{"type":"button","id":"vplay","label":"Play view"},{"type":"button","id":"vside","label":"Side"},{"type":"button","id":"vabove","label":"Above"},{"type":"range","id":"x","label":"Spring compression x"},{"type":"range","id":"callV","label":"Your call: launch speed"},{"type":"range","id":"u0","label":"Test speed u₀"},{"type":"range","id":"callReb","label":"Your call: rebound speed"},{"type":"range","id":"kickV","label":"Kicker speed v"},{"type":"range","id":"kickTh","label":"Angle θ to the line"},{"type":"range","id":"callR","label":"Your call: distance R"}],"shell":"kit"},"phone":{"id":"phone","title":"The phone machine","href":"m/phone.html","desc":"A 3 349 mAh phone battery on a 20 W charger: charge in coulombs, energy and average power, the \"50% in 30 minutes\" claim, why charging slows above 80%, internal resistance, loads in parallel, wireless induction and a charging capacitor.","blurb":"Coulombs, joules and watts in a phone battery, fast charging, internal resistance and capacitors.","tags":["NCEA L2 Physics 91173","NCEA L3 Physics 91526","Year 12","Year 13"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Physics · Electricity and electrical systems","crumb":"NCEA Level 2 Physics AS91173 and Level 3 AS91526","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 91173 · L3 91526","exam":"2026-11-18T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"L2 Wed 18 Nov · L3 Thu 12 Nov"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics past exams and exemplars","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics/"},{"label":"NZQA: AS91173 (L2 electricity)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91173"},{"label":"NZQA: AS91526 (L3 electrical systems)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91526"}],"truth":"Battery 3 349 mAh at 3.87 V, a 20 W charger and 5.0 h of screen time are the practice pack's figures. The cell model (emf 3.5–4.2 V, internal resistance 0.08 Ω, charger at 4.2 V), the screen and modem currents, the wireless-charging emf and efficiency, and the phone temperature are estimates that show the shape of the physics, not measurements of any real phone. Charging is sped up and the capacitor is slowed down so you can watch them. The Blender-built apparatus is a generic educational design; circuit boards are enlarged diagrams. Live wireless spacing is shown at 5× for visibility; gap controls and calculations still use actual millimetres. Charge and field markers are illustrative, not measured electrons or a field simulation. Offline render glass and browser transparency differ.","footer":"Covers AS91173 (L2): charge, current, energy and power (Q = It, E = VQ, P = VI), DC circuits in parallel, and electromagnetic induction (Faraday and Lenz). AS91526 (L3): internal resistance (ε = V + Ir), capacitors (Q = CV, E = ½CV², τ = RC) and the charging curve.","mainLabel":"An electrical learning apparatus: phone and charger, cell resistance, parallel loads, wireless coils or capacitor plates for the selected topic","kupu":[{"mi":"hiko","en":"electricity","ta":1171,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"wireless"},{"mi":"iahiko","en":"electric current","ta":1648,"note":"","chip":["Iavg","Inow","Itot","Ichg"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"charge, fast, ccv, circuit"},{"mi":"ngaohiko","en":"voltage","ta":40454,"note":"","chip":["Vterm","pd"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"energy, ccv, internal, wireless"},{"mi":"ara iahiko","en":"electric circuit","ta":343,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"circuit"},{"mi":"pūhiko","en":"battery, electrical cell","ta":40049,"note":"","chip":["mAh"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (RP 2009:333) cited in entry; Te Aka also lists the loanwords pāteri and pēteri","stepsNote":"charge"},{"mi":"whana hiko","en":"electric charge","ta":40518,"note":"","chip":["Q","Qcap"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (RP 2009:455) cited in entry","stepsNote":"charge, capacitor"},{"mi":"pūngao hiko","en":"electrical energy","ta":47713,"note":"","chip":["E","Ecap"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"energy, fast, capacitor"},{"mi":"parenga","en":"resistance, resistor","ta":5300,"note":"Te Aka also lists parenga iahiko for a resistor.","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation); parenga iahiko 46887 is listed for a resistor","stepsNote":"ccv, internal, circuit, capacitor"}],"steps":[{"k":"charge","lab":"Charge stored","cap":"A modern phone battery is about 3 349 mAh at 3.87 V. Milliamp-hours are a charge: current × time. Turn it into coulombs.","exam":"Q = It = 3.349 A h × 3600 s = 12 056 C ≈ 1.21 × 10⁴ C. (3 349 mAh is 3.349 A for one hour, and one hour is 3600 s.)","trap":"Leaving mAh as mA (÷1000 to get amps) or hours as hours (×3600 to get seconds). Q must be in coulombs before it meets V.","big":"The big idea: charge is current × time, Q = It. A battery's mAh rating is a charge in disguise: 1 mAh = 3.6 C.","kupu":["whana hiko","pūhiko","iahiko"]},{"k":"energy","lab":"Energy and power","cap":"Energy stored is voltage × charge. If a full battery lasts exactly 5.0 hours of screen-on time, what is the average power draw?","exam":"E = VQ = 3.87 × 12 056 ≈ 4.67 × 10⁴ J. P = E ÷ t = 46 700 ÷ (5.0 × 3600) ≈ 2.6 W average. The average current is I = P ÷ V = 2.6 ÷ 3.87 ≈ 0.67 A.","trap":"Multiplying mAh by V and calling it joules. 3 349 mAh × 3.87 V = 12.96 Wh, which is 12.96 × 3600 = 46 700 J.","big":"The big idea: E = VQ (or E = VIt) and P = E ÷ t. A phone's whole day runs on a few watts.","kupu":["pūngao hiko","ngaohiko"]},{"k":"fast","lab":"\"50% in 30 min\"","cap":"A 20 W USB-C charger claims 50% in 30 minutes. Half the battery's energy ÷ the power actually reaching the cell = the time. Try other chargers and efficiencies.","exam":"Half of E is 23.3 kJ. At 20 W, t = E ÷ P = 23 300 ÷ 20 = 1167 s ≈ 19.4 min, so the claim is plausible only at ~100 % efficiency; at a realistic 85 % it takes about 23 min, still inside the \"about 30 minutes\" margin.","trap":"Using the charger's rated power as if all of it reached the battery. Heat in the cable, the converter and the cell takes 10–30 %.","big":"The big idea: t = E ÷ P, but only the power that reaches the cell counts. State your efficiency assumption; examiners reward it.","kupu":["pūngao hiko","iahiko"]},{"k":"ccv","lab":"Why charging slows","cap":"Watch the current. In this model it is limited by the charger or by the voltage difference across the internal resistance, whichever allows less current. As the cell emf rises, the driving pd shrinks and current tapers. The transition depends on charger power, efficiency and internal resistance.","exam":"The model takes the smaller of the charger current limit and (V_charger − ε) ÷ r. As the cell emf rises toward the charger voltage, the driving potential difference falls and the current tapers. There is no fixed percentage for the transition: changing charger power, efficiency or internal resistance changes where it occurs.","trap":"Assuming tapering starts at one fixed percentage for every setting, or mixing up cell emf and charger voltage. At the default settings, the model current starts falling between 49% and 50% charge. Follow the current curve as you change the settings.","big":"The big idea: current is driven by a potential difference across a resistance. As the cell's emf rises toward the charger's voltage, the difference falls, so the current falls: the flat tail of every charging curve.","kupu":["ngaohiko","iahiko","parenga"]},{"k":"internal","lab":"Internal resistance","cap":"Level 3. A cell has internal resistance r. When a game pulls 3 A, some volts are lost inside the cell (Ir) and I²r of heat warms the phone. Change the load and r.","exam":"ε = V + Ir, so the terminal voltage is V = ε − Ir = 3.87 − 3.0 × 0.08 = 3.63 V. The power lost inside the cell is I²r = 3.0² × 0.08 = 0.72 W, which heats the phone; the power delivered is VI = 3.63 × 3.0 = 10.9 W.","trap":"Using ε as the voltage across the load. Under load the terminal voltage is lower by Ir; at no load (I = 0) the terminal voltage equals ε.","big":"The big idea: ε = V + Ir. The terminal voltage drops under load; the lost volts become heat inside the cell. A graph of V against I has intercept ε and gradient −r.","kupu":["parenga","ngaohiko"]},{"k":"circuit","lab":"Loads in parallel","cap":"The screen and the modem sit in parallel across the battery: each gets the full 3.87 V and the currents add. Set each load's current and see the total, the power and the time to flat.","exam":"In parallel each load has the full 3.87 V across it, so the currents add: I = 1.2 + 0.5 = 1.7 A. P = VI = 3.87 × 1.7 = 6.6 W. The battery lasts about 3.349 A h ÷ 1.7 A ≈ 2.0 h. The combined resistance is V ÷ I = 2.3 Ω, less than either branch (3.2 Ω and 7.7 Ω).","trap":"Adding resistances in parallel as if they were in series. Add the currents (or the reciprocals of the resistances), not the resistances.","big":"The big idea: in parallel, every branch has the same voltage and the total current is the sum. The combined resistance is smaller than any branch: 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂.","kupu":["ara iahiko","iahiko","parenga"]},{"k":"wireless","lab":"Wireless charging","cap":"A Qi pad is a transformer with an air gap. Alternating current in the pad's coil makes a changing magnetic field; the phone's coil sits in it and an emf is induced. Turns, frequency and the gap all matter.","exam":"The alternating current in the pad coil produces a changing magnetic field. The flux through the phone's coil changes, so an emf is induced (ε = −N ΔΦ ÷ Δt, Faraday's law). Increasing the number of turns or the frequency increases the rate of change of flux linkage and so the emf; a larger gap reduces the flux and the efficiency.","trap":"Saying a steady field induces a voltage. Only a changing flux induces an emf; that is why the pad uses alternating current.","big":"The big idea: Faraday's law. An emf is induced only while the flux through the coil is changing: ε = −N ΔΦ ÷ Δt. More turns, faster change (higher frequency) or more flux (smaller gap) → bigger emf. Lenz's law gives the minus sign.","kupu":["hiko","ngaohiko"]},{"k":"capacitor","lab":"A capacitor charges","cap":"Level 3. The phone's power rail uses capacitors to hold the voltage steady. Through a resistor, a capacitor charges exponentially with time constant τ = RC. Change C and R, then press play.","exam":"τ = RC = 100 × 1000 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.10 s. The capacitor reaches 63 % of 3.87 V after 0.10 s and is effectively full after 5τ = 0.5 s. Charge stored Q = CV = 1000 × 10⁻⁶ × 3.87 = 3.87 × 10⁻³ C; energy E = ½CV² = 0.5 × 10⁻³ × 3.87² = 7.5 × 10⁻³ J.","trap":"Forgetting the 10⁻⁶ on microfarads, and using ½QV with Q in mAh. Convert first; then E = ½CV² = ½QV.","big":"The big idea: Q = CV, E = ½CV² and τ = RC. After one time constant the capacitor is at 63 % of the supply; after 5τ it is essentially full.","kupu":["whana hiko","pūngao hiko","parenga"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Five challenges, two of them Level 3. Use the controls from any step, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: convert every unit first (mAh → A, h → s, μF → F), write the equation, then substitute. Say what you assumed about efficiency.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Choose a battery that stores about 1.8 × 10⁴ C.","check":"()=>Math.abs(Q()-18000)<400","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.mAh)} mAh = ${fmt(s.mAh/1000,3)} A h × 3600 = ${fmt(Q())} C.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(s.mAh)} mAh stores ${fmt(Q())} C. Q = It, so you need 18 000 ÷ 3600 = 5 A h.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"With the 3 349 mAh battery, find a screen-on time that keeps the average power draw under 2 W.","check":"()=>s.mAh===3349&&Pavg()<2","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(E()/1000,1)} kJ over ${fmt(s.hours,1)} h is ${fmt(Pavg(),2)} W.`","no":"()=>s.mAh!==3349?\"Set the battery back to 3 349 mAh first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(Pavg(),2)} W. P = E ÷ t, so t must be more than 46 700 ÷ 2 = 23 350 s ≈ 6.5 h.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set a charger and efficiency so that 50% takes between 25 and 30 minutes (3 349 mAh).","check":"()=>s.mAh===3349&&t50()>=1500&&t50()<=1800","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(0.5*E()/1000,1)} kJ ÷ (${fmt(s.Pchg)} W × ${Math.round(s.eff*100)}%) = ${fmt(t50()/60,1)} min.`","no":"()=>s.mAh!==3349?\"Set the battery back to 3 349 mAh first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(t50()/60,1)} min. You need ${fmt(0.5*E()/1000,1)} kJ ÷ P_eff between 1500 and 1800 s, so P × efficiency between ${fmt(0.5*E()/1800,1)} and ${fmt(0.5*E()/1500,1)} W.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Level 3: with a 3.0 A load, choose an internal resistance that keeps the lost volts under 0.10 V.","check":"()=>s.Iload===3&&s.Iload*s.r<0.1","ok":"()=>`Yes: Ir = 3.0 × ${fmt(s.r,2)} = ${fmt(3*s.r,2)} V, so the terminal voltage stays at ${fmt(Vterm(),2)} V and only ${fmt(Plost(),2)} W heats the cell.`","no":"()=>s.Iload!==3?\"Set the load to 3.0 A first.\":`Not yet: Ir = ${fmt(3*s.r,2)} V. You need r < 0.10 ÷ 3.0 = 0.033 Ω.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Level 3: set the capacitor and resistor so the time constant is about 0.10 s.","check":"()=>Math.abs(tau()-0.1)<=0.01","ok":"()=>`Yes: τ = RC = ${fmt(s.R)} × ${sci(s.C*1e-6,2)} = ${fmt(tau(),3)} s. After 0.10 s the capacitor is at 63%.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: τ = ${fmt(tau()*1000,1)} ms. τ = RC with C in farads (${fmt(s.C)} μF = ${sci(s.C*1e-6,2)} F).`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"range","id":"mAh","label":"Battery"},{"type":"range","id":"hours","label":"Screen time"},{"type":"range","id":"Pchg","label":"Charger"},{"type":"range","id":"eff","label":"Efficiency"},{"type":"range","id":"r","label":"Internal resistance"},{"type":"range","id":"Iload","label":"Load current"},{"type":"range","id":"Iscreen","label":"Screen"},{"type":"range","id":"Imodem","label":"Modem"},{"type":"range","id":"N","label":"Coil turns"},{"type":"range","id":"f","label":"Frequency"},{"type":"range","id":"gap","label":"Gap"},{"type":"select","id":"C","label":"Capacitor"},{"type":"select","id":"R","label":"Resistor"}],"shell":"kit"},"queue":{"id":"queue","title":"The queue machine","href":"m/queue.html","desc":"Checkout and ED queues you can run: random arrivals, normally distributed service times, a live waiting-time distribution, relative risk of breaching the 6-hour target with 8 beds against 12, and a probability tree with its two-way table.","blurb":"Random arrivals, normal service times, a queue you can run, and relative risk in the ED.","tags":["NCEA L2 Maths 91267","Year 12"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Maths and statistics · Probability","crumb":"NCEA Level 2 Mathematics and Statistics · AS91267 Apply probability methods in solving problems","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 Maths 91267","exam":"2026-11-19T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 19 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/mathematics-and-statistics/"},{"label":"NZQA: AS91267 standard, papers and schedules","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91267"},{"label":"Lab: the ED twin","url":"https://assembl-lab.vercel.app/ed/"},{"label":"Lab: the supermarket twin","url":"https://assembl-lab.vercel.app/store/"}],"truth":"Arrivals are Poisson (random, steady average rate); checkout service times are normal with μ = 118 s, σ = 19 s (the practice pack's figures), clipped at 20 s; ED treatment times are a mix of simple cases (exponential, mean 30 min) and 12% complex cases (mean 120 min), about 41 minutes overall (an estimate chosen so that 8 beds at 14 an hour is overloaded and 12 copes). Queues are first come, first served; a patient still inside at the end of a run who has already passed 6 hours counts as a breach, and the rest of the unfinished are left out. A seed makes a run repeatable; change it for a new run. The lab's ED and supermarket twins are fuller models with real data behind them.","footer":"Covers AS91267 (L2 probability): normal distribution probabilities, experimental distributions (shape, centre, spread), probability trees, two-way tables and conditional probability, risk and relative risk. Poisson arrivals are shown as the model behind the simulation (formally AS91586 at Level 3).","mainLabel":"Shoppers arriving at checkouts, a normal curve of service times, two ED runs side by side, and a probability tree","kupu":[{"mi":"tūponotanga","en":"probability, chance","ta":46069,"note":"","chip":["p0","prob","phelp"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"arrivals, service, ed, tree"},{"mi":"pāpono","en":"event (in probability)","ta":45828,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"arrivals, tree"},{"mi":"putanga","en":"outcome","ta":6346,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"tree"},{"mi":"tūtira","en":"queue, row, file","ta":8821,"note":"Te Aka also lists rārangi for a line or queue.","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (historical citations; no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"arrivals, wait"},{"mi":"toharite","en":"mean, average","ta":38680,"note":"Te Aka also lists toharite for the median; check which your class uses.","chip":["meanw"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (TRP 2010:292) cited in entry","stepsNote":"service, wait"},{"mi":"ine mahora","en":"standard deviation","ta":49898,"note":"","chip":["sig"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry, maths sense (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"service"},{"mi":"tauanga","en":"statistics","ta":44587,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"ed"},{"mi":"raraunga","en":"data","ta":6555,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"wait, ed"}],"steps":[{"k":"arrivals","lab":"Arrivals","cap":"Shoppers arrive at random, 1.6 a minute on average. Press play and count how many arrive in each 2-minute window. How often does nobody arrive at all?","exam":"λ = 1.6 × 2 = 3.2 for a 2-minute window, so P(0) = e^(−3.2) = 0.0408 ≈ 0.04. (Poisson is formally a Level 3 distribution; at Level 2, compare the observed distribution's shape, centre and spread with the model.)","trap":"Using λ for one minute in a two-minute question. Scale the rate to the window first.","big":"The big idea: random arrivals at a steady average rate follow a Poisson distribution, with λ = rate × window. The no-arrivals probability is e^(−λ).","kupu":["tūponotanga","pāpono","tūtira"]},{"k":"service","lab":"Service times","cap":"Checkout service times are normally distributed with μ = 118 s and σ = 19 s (the 2025 paper's style). Slide the bounds to shade a region and read the probability.","exam":"P(X > 100) = P(Z > (100 − 118) ÷ 19) = P(Z > −0.95) = 0.829. P(100 < X < 120) = P(−0.95 < Z < 0.11) = 0.829 − 0.456 = 0.373.","trap":"Forgetting that the tables give the area to the left. For P(Z > −0.95) you need 1 − Φ(−0.95), which equals Φ(0.95) by symmetry.","big":"The big idea: standardise with z = (x − μ) ÷ σ, then use the tables (or calculator) for the area. Draw the curve and shade it every time.","kupu":["toharite","ine mahora","tūponotanga"]},{"k":"wait","lab":"Waiting times","cap":"Now put them together. Arrivals at random, service times normal, a few checkouts open. Press play for a simulated hour and watch the queue; the close-up builds the distribution of waiting times.","exam":"With six checkouts and three arrivals a minute (one simulated hour, 180 shoppers), waits ran from 0 to 4.4 minutes with a median of 1.8 minutes and a middle half from 0.9 to 2.4 minutes. Opening a seventh checkout shifted the centre down to a median of 0.0 minutes (most shoppers were served at once) and narrowed the spread to a maximum of 2.3 minutes, with the distribution now strongly skewed to the right.","trap":"Comparing only the means. Say shape, centre AND spread, and note that a different random run gives slightly different numbers.","big":"The big idea: an experimental distribution. Describe it the way the standard asks: shape (usually skewed right), centre (median and mean), spread (IQR, range), and any unusual values, then compare two settings.","kupu":["tūtira","toharite","raraunga"]},{"k":"ed","lab":"The ED and relative risk","cap":"The pack's investigation: 14 arrivals an hour with 8 beds, then 12 beds. Run each for a day, save them as A and B, and compute the relative risk of breaching the 6-hour target. Your numbers will vary; that is the point.","exam":"With 8 beds, 6 of 271 patients breached 6 hours: risk 0.022. With 12 beds, 2 of 308 breached: risk 0.006. Relative risk = 0.022 ÷ 0.006 ≈ 3.4, so adding four beds cut the risk of a breach to about a third, which more than halves it. Both runs were 24 simulated hours, but the breach counts (6 and 2) are small, so another run could give a very different ratio; say so, and run longer before trusting it.","trap":"Reporting the difference in percentages as the relative risk, or quoting a ratio without the sample sizes behind it.","big":"The big idea: relative risk = risk in group A ÷ risk in group B. Say what it means in words, and give the sample sizes, because a ratio of small numbers is shaky.","kupu":["tūponotanga","raraunga","tauanga"]},{"k":"tree","lab":"Tree and table","cap":"Two stages at the checkout: which lane a shopper chooses, then whether they need help. Multiply along the branches, add across the leaves, and turn it into a two-way table.","exam":"P(help) = P(self) × P(help | self) + P(staffed) × P(help | staffed) = 0.4 × 0.3 + 0.6 × 0.1 = 0.12 + 0.06 = 0.18. P(self | help) = 0.12 ÷ 0.18 = 0.667: two-thirds of the shoppers who need help are at the self-checkouts.","trap":"Reading P(help | self) as P(self | help). The condition is the branch you are already on.","big":"The big idea: P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B | A). To get P(B) add every route to B. Conditional probability runs backwards: P(A | B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B).","kupu":["tūponotanga","pāpono","putanga"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Five challenges. Use the controls from any step; two need a simulation run. Then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: write the probability statement first (P(X > 100)), then the working, then a sentence in context with the sample size when it comes from data.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Set an arrival rate and window so the probability of no arrivals is about 0.05.","check":"()=>Math.abs(poisson(s.lam*s.win,0)-0.05)<0.006","ok":"()=>`Yes: λ = ${fmt(s.lam,1)} × ${s.win} = ${fmt(s.lam*s.win,2)}, so P(0) = e^(−${fmt(s.lam*s.win,2)}) = ${fmt(poisson(s.lam*s.win,0),3)}.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: P(0) = ${fmt(poisson(s.lam*s.win,0),3)}. You need λ = −ln 0.05 ≈ 3.0.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Service times (μ = 118 s, σ = 19 s): shade the region for P(X > 150) and read it off.","check":"()=>s.mu===118&&s.sig===19&&s.lo===150&&s.hi>=s.mu+4*s.sig","ok":"()=>`Yes: z = (150 − 118) ÷ 19 = ${fmt((150-118)/19,2)}, P = 1 − Φ(${fmt((150-118)/19,2)}) = ${fmt(1-Phi((150-118)/19),3)}.`","no":"()=>s.mu!==118||s.sig!==19?\"Set μ = 118 and σ = 19 first.\":`Set the lower bound to 150 s and slide the upper bound all the way up. Now: ${fmt(s.lo)} to ${s.hi>=s.mu+4*s.sig?\"∞\":fmt(s.hi)}.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Three arrivals a minute: open enough checkouts that the mean wait in a simulated hour is under 1 minute (run the hour, then check).","check":"()=>!!(s.lastRun&&Math.abs(s.lastRun.rate-3)<1e-9&&s.lastRun.mean<1)","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${s.lastRun.c} checkouts, mean wait ${fmt(s.lastRun.mean,2)} min over ${s.lastRun.n} shoppers. Each checkout is busy about ${Math.round(3*s.mu/60/s.lastRun.c*100)}% of the time.`","no":"()=>!s.lastRun?\"No finished hour yet. Set 3 arrivals a minute and press Run an hour (or play the Waiting times step to the end).\":Math.abs(s.lastRun.rate-3)>1e-9?\"Set the arrival rate to 3.0 a minute and run again.\":`Your run: ${s.lastRun.c} checkouts, mean wait ${fmt(s.lastRun.mean,2)} min. Each checkout can serve about ${fmt(60/s.mu*60,1)} an hour; 3 a minute is 180 an hour.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"ED: run 8 beds as A and 12 beds as B (14 an hour, at least 24 hours each) and find the relative risk of breaching 6 hours.","check":"()=>!!(s.runA&&s.runB&&s.runA.c===8&&s.runB.c===12&&s.runA.rate===14&&s.runB.rate===14&&s.runA.minutes>=1440&&s.runB.minutes>=1440)","ok":"()=>{const rr=s.runB.breach>0?s.runA.breach/s.runB.breach:Infinity;return `Yes: risk ${fmt(s.runA.breach,3)} (${s.runA.breaches} of ${s.runA.nT}) with 8 beds against ${fmt(s.runB.breach,3)} (${s.runB.breaches} of ${s.runB.nT}) with 12. RR = ${isFinite(rr)?fmt(rr,2):\"undefined (no breaches in B)\"}${isFinite(rr)?(rr>=2?\": more than halved.\":\": not halved.\"):\".\"}`}","no":"()=>`Not yet. Saved: A = ${s.runA?`${s.runA.c} beds, ${fmt(s.runA.rate)}/h, ${fmt(s.runA.minutes/60)} h`:\"nothing\"}; B = ${s.runB?`${s.runB.c} beds, ${fmt(s.runB.rate)}/h, ${fmt(s.runB.minutes/60)} h`:\"nothing\"}. Set 14 an hour, 24 hours, 8 beds → Run → Save as A; then 12 beds → Run → Save as B.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Tree: set the three chances so that P(needs help) is exactly 0.25.","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.pSelf*s.pHelpSelf+(1-s.pSelf)*s.pHelpStaff-0.25)<0.005","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.pSelf,2)} × ${fmt(s.pHelpSelf,2)} + ${fmt(1-s.pSelf,2)} × ${fmt(s.pHelpStaff,2)} = ${fmt(s.pSelf*s.pHelpSelf+(1-s.pSelf)*s.pHelpStaff,3)}.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: P(help) = ${fmt(s.pSelf*s.pHelpSelf+(1-s.pSelf)*s.pHelpStaff,3)}. Multiply along each branch and add the two \"help\" leaves.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"range","id":"lam","label":"Arrivals/min"},{"type":"range","id":"win","label":"Window"},{"type":"range","id":"lo","label":"Lower bound"},{"type":"range","id":"hi","label":"Upper bound"},{"type":"range","id":"mu","label":"μ"},{"type":"range","id":"sig","label":"σ"},{"type":"range","id":"rate","label":"Arrivals/min"},{"type":"range","id":"c","label":"Checkouts"},{"type":"button","id":"runHour","label":"Run an hour"},{"type":"range","id":"edRate","label":"Arrivals/hour"},{"type":"range","id":"beds","label":"Beds"},{"type":"range","id":"edHours","label":"Run for"},{"type":"range","id":"seed","label":"Seed"},{"type":"button","id":"runED","label":"Run the ED"},{"type":"button","id":"saveA","label":"Save as A"},{"type":"button","id":"saveB","label":"Save as B"},{"type":"range","id":"pSelf","label":"P(self-checkout)"},{"type":"range","id":"pHelpSelf","label":"P(help | self)"},{"type":"range","id":"pHelpStaff","label":"P(help | staffed)"}],"shell":"kit"},"plan":{"id":"plan","title":"The floor-plan studio","href":"m/plan.html","desc":"A DVC design brief: rearrange an ED or supermarket floor plan against fixed walls and measurable rules, save three distinct options, evaluate them, and draw the winner to scale with dimensions and a title block.","blurb":"Rearrange an ED floor plan against a brief, save three options, draw the winner to scale.","tags":["NCEA L2 DVC 91337","NCEA L3 DVC 91627","Year 12","Year 13"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Design and visual communication · Spatial design","crumb":"NCEA DVC · AS91337 (Level 2) and AS91627 (Level 3) · externally assessed portfolios","badge":{"label":"NCEA DVC 91337 · 91627 (portfolio)"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Design and Visual Communication","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/design-and-visual-communication/"},{"label":"NZQA: AS91337 (L2, generate design ideas)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91337"},{"label":"NZQA: AS91627 (L3, initiate design ideas)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91627"},{"label":"Lab: the ED twin","url":"https://assembl-lab.vercel.app/ed/"},{"label":"Lab: the supermarket twin","url":"https://assembl-lab.vercel.app/store/"}],"truth":"A simplified spatial-design exercise. The sites (30 × 20 m ED, 36 × 24 m supermarket front end), the zone sizes and the six rules are estimates chosen to make the trade-offs visible, not a real hospital or store. DVC externals are portfolio submissions marked against the standards, not exams; the rules here are a stand-in for the measurable outcomes a real brief would set. The lab's ED and supermarket twins give the simulation evidence.","footer":"Covers the thinking the DVC externals assess: a brief with stakeholder, need, constraints and measures; divergent exploration and a range of distinct design ideas (AS91337, AS91627); evaluation against the brief; and visual communication (scale, dimensions, freehand versus instrumental drawing).","mainLabel":"A top-down floor plan with movable zones, fixed doors and a panel of rule lights","kupu":[{"mi":"hoahoa","en":"to design, plan; a plan of a building","ta":1290,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"brief, explore, communicate"},{"mi":"kaihoahoa","en":"designer, architect","ta":1924,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"brief"},{"mi":"mahere","en":"map, plan, chart","ta":3350,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation); already used in the map machine","stepsNote":"explore, communicate"},{"mi":"āwhata","en":"scale (of a map or drawing)","ta":15342,"note":"","chip":["scaleTxt"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry; already used in the map machine","stepsNote":"communicate"},{"mi":"horahanga","en":"area","ta":45667,"note":"","chip":["area"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry; already used in the shape workshop","stepsNote":"explore, evaluate"},{"mi":"tauira","en":"example, pattern, model","ta":7706,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"ideate"},{"mi":"kiritaki","en":"customer, client","ta":11034,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation); already used in the market machine","stepsNote":"brief, evaluate"},{"mi":"āhuatanga hoahoa","en":"design element","ta":38908,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"ideate"}],"steps":[{"k":"brief","lab":"The brief","cap":"A DVC project starts with a brief: who it's for, what they need, what can't change, and how you'll know it worked. Read it, then choose the ED or the supermarket.","exam":"The brief identifies the stakeholder (Health NZ, judged against the 95%-within-6-hours target), the need (clear the waiting room without adding beds), the constraints (existing external walls and doors; minimum zone sizes; triage within 8 m of the entrance) and the evidence that will show success (before-and-after simulation runs).","trap":"A brief with no measurable outcome. 'Make the ED flow better' can't be evaluated; '95 % within 6 hours in the twin' can.","big":"The big idea: a brief is the contract you design against. Everything you draw later is judged by it, so write the constraints and the measure of success down first.","kupu":["hoahoa","kiritaki","kaihoahoa"]},{"k":"explore","lab":"Explore the layout","cap":"Drag the zones (or pick one and use the Nudge buttons). Dashed red means a zone breaks a rule. Move triage forward, split the fast-track flow, re-site the bays: watch the rule lights.","exam":"Moving triage to within 8 m of the entrance means arriving patients are assessed before they settle into the waiting room, and placing fast-track beside the waiting room lets low-acuity patients be streamed off without crossing the department. Resus stays beside the ambulance door because seconds matter there.","trap":"Fixing one rule and breaking two others. Check all the lights after every move; a layout is a set of relationships, not a list of rooms.","big":"The big idea: exploration is divergent: try many arrangements quickly, and let the constraints tell you which ideas have legs. Adjacency (what touches what) and distance (how far people walk) drive flow.","kupu":["hoahoa","mahere","horahanga"]},{"k":"ideate","lab":"Three options","cap":"Generate design ideas: arrange the zones one way and Save as A, then change the big moves (where triage goes, where fast-track sits) and save B and C. Different ideas, not three versions of one.","exam":"Option A moves triage next to the entrance and keeps the bays central; Option B splits a fast-track corridor along the north wall so minor cases never enter the main bays; Option C re-sites the waiting room beside reception to shorten the walk. Each was tested against the same six rules.","trap":"Three options that differ only in detail. If the thumbnails look alike, the examiner sees refinement, not ideation.","big":"The big idea: AS91337 and AS91627 reward a range of distinct ideas and the thinking between them. Thumbnails of A, B and C side by side are the visual communication the standards ask for.","kupu":["tauira","āhuatanga hoahoa"]},{"k":"evaluate","lab":"Evaluate","cap":"Score the current layout against the brief: rules met, walking distance, area per person. Then take it to the lab's twin, run the before and after, and screenshot the result as evidence.","exam":"Against the brief, Option B meets six of six rules, shortens the entrance-to-bays walk from 31 m to 22 m and gives 2.0 m² per waiting patient. In the ED twin, the same change lifted patients seen within 6 hours from 71 % to 84 % with no extra beds, so it best meets the stakeholder's need.","trap":"Evaluating against what you like instead of what the brief said. Quote the brief's measure and the number you got.","big":"The big idea: evaluation needs evidence against the brief's measure, not opinion. Numbers from the rules here, and simulation runs from the twin, make the judgement defensible.","kupu":["kiritaki","horahanga"]},{"k":"communicate","lab":"Draw it to scale","cap":"Visual communication: the plan at 1:100 with dimensions in millimetres, a title block, and the difference between a freehand ideation sketch and an instrumental drawing. Select a zone to dimension it.","exam":"The instrumental plan is drawn at 1:100, so the 30 m × 20 m site is 300 mm × 200 mm on the sheet. Dimensions are given in millimetres (triage 3000 × 3000) with extension lines clear of the outline, and the title block records the project, the option and the scale.","trap":"Mixing units on one drawing, or drawing 'roughly to scale'. Pick 1:100 or 1:50 and keep every dimension honest to it.","big":"The big idea: scale = drawing size ÷ real size. At 1:100, one metre is 10 mm on paper. Dimensions go in millimetres, outside the outline, with extension lines, and the title block says what, which option, what scale.","kupu":["mahere","āwhata","hoahoa"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Five challenges on the ED brief. Use the controls from any step, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Portfolio tip: show the thinking, not just the answer: the brief, three distinct ideas, the rules each one meets, the drawing to scale, and the evidence that it works.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"ED brief: move triage so its centre is within 8 m of the walk-in entrance.","check":"()=>s.brief===\"ed\"&&evalZones(s.zones).triage","ok":"()=>`Yes: triage is ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).triageD,1)} m from the entrance. Patients are assessed before they sit down.`","no":"()=>s.brief!==\"ed\"?\"Choose the ED brief first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).triageD,1)} m. Drag triage toward the entrance on the left wall.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Keep every zone inside the walls with no overlaps.","check":"()=>{const r=evalZones(s.zones);return r.inside&&r.nooverlap}","ok":"Yes: all inside, nothing overlapping. The dashed red outlines have gone.","no":"()=>{const r=evalZones(s.zones);return `Not yet: ${r.inside?\"\":\"a zone pokes outside the walls. \"}${r.overlaps?r.overlaps+\" overlap\"+(r.overlaps>1?\"s\":\"\")+\" to fix.\":\"\"}`}","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"ED brief: put fast-track beside the waiting room (gap ≤ 1 m) while keeping resus within 6 m of the ambulance door.","check":"()=>{const r=evalZones(s.zones);return s.brief===\"ed\"&&r.fastadj&&r.resus}","ok":"()=>`Yes: fast-track gap ${fmt(Math.max(0,evalZones(s.zones).fastGap),1)} m, resus ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).resusD,1)} m from the ambulance door.`","no":"()=>{const r=evalZones(s.zones);return s.brief!==\"ed\"?\"Choose the ED brief first.\":`Not yet: fast-track gap ${fmt(Math.max(0,r.fastGap),1)} m (need ≤ 1), resus ${fmt(r.resusD,1)} m from the ambulance door (need ≤ 6).`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Save three clearly different ED options (A, B and C), each with triage in a different place.","check":"()=>{const o=s.options;if(!(o.A&&o.B&&o.C))return false;if([o.A,o.B,o.C].some(x=>x.brief!==\"ed\"))return false;const t=k=>o[k].zones.triage;return dist([t(\"A\").x,t(\"A\").y],[t(\"B\").x,t(\"B\").y])>=3&&dist([t(\"A\").x,t(\"A\").y],[t(\"C\").x,t(\"C\").y])>=3&&dist([t(\"B\").x,t(\"B\").y],[t(\"C\").x,t(\"C\").y])>=3}","ok":"()=>`Yes: three options with triage at (${[\"A\",\"B\",\"C\"].map(k=>`${fmt(s.options[k].zones.triage.x,1)}, ${fmt(s.options[k].zones.triage.y,1)}`).join(\"), (\")}). Rules met: ${[\"A\",\"B\",\"C\"].map(k=>s.options[k].r.pass).join(\", \")} of ${B().rules.length}.`","no":"()=>{const o=s.options;return !(o.A&&o.B&&o.C)?`Saved so far: ${[\"A\",\"B\",\"C\"].filter(k=>o[k]).join(\", \")||\"none\"}. Arrange, Save as A; rearrange, Save as B; again for C.`:\"All three are saved, but triage sits in nearly the same place in two of them (move it at least 3 m).\"}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"ED brief: give the waiting room at least 1.5 m² per expected waiting patient (20 patients).","check":"()=>s.brief===\"ed\"&&evalZones(s.zones).area","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).areaV)} m² ÷ 20 = ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).areaV/20,2)} m² each.`","no":"()=>s.brief!==\"ed\"?\"Choose the ED brief first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).areaV)} m² is ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).areaV/20,2)} m² each. You need ≥ 30 m²: select the waiting room and make it wider or taller.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"select","id":"brief","label":"Brief"},{"type":"select","id":"zone","label":"Zone"},{"type":"button","id":"left","label":"← 0.5 m"},{"type":"button","id":"right","label":"→ 0.5 m"},{"type":"button","id":"up","label":"↑ 0.5 m"},{"type":"button","id":"down","label":"↓ 0.5 m"},{"type":"button","id":"wider","label":"Wider"},{"type":"button","id":"narrower","label":"Narrower"},{"type":"button","id":"taller","label":"Taller"},{"type":"button","id":"shorter","label":"Shorter"},{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"Back to the start"},{"type":"button","id":"saveA","label":"Save as A"},{"type":"button","id":"saveB","label":"Save as B"},{"type":"button","id":"saveC","label":"Save as C"},{"type":"select","id":"scale","label":"Scale"},{"type":"button","id":"copy","label":"Copy the design notes"}],"shell":"kit"},"quote":{"id":"quote","title":"The quote-to-point machine","href":"m/quote.html","desc":"Three short original texts (a story, a poem, a personal essay), a question about a specified aspect, and four slots: claim, evidence, explanation, so what. Drag a quote into the evidence slot, pick the lines that fit, watch the paragraph assemble, then self-check it against paraphrased Achieved, Merit and Excellence descriptors.","blurb":"Drag a quote into claim → evidence → explain → so what and watch the paragraph build.","tags":["NCEA L2 English 91098","Year 12","English"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"English · Studied written texts","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 English 91098","exam":"2026-11-12T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 12 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: English past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/english/"}],"truth":"All three texts are original, written for this machine, and short so the skills stand out. The 0–8 meter and the Achieved / Merit / Excellence words are the machine's estimate, paraphrased from the standard, not NZQA marking. In the exam you write about texts you studied in class.","footer":"Covers NCEA Level 2 English 91098 (Analyse specified aspect(s) of studied written text(s), supported by evidence): claims about a specified aspect, selecting evidence, explaining technique and effect, and the writer's purpose, in an essay of around 750–800 words.","mainLabel":"An original text with tappable quotes, three claim cards, four paragraph slots with a tray of draggable quotes, and a self-check list","kupu":[{"mi":"tuhinga","en":"text, writing","ta":8648,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kaituhi","en":"writer, author","ta":2031,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"taunakitanga","en":"evidence","ta":14911,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"whakamārama","en":"explanation","ta":9594,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kōwae","en":"paragraph","ta":3169,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kaupapa","en":"topic, theme, purpose","ta":2439,"note":"","chip":[]}],"steps":[{"k":"text","lab":"Read the text","cap":"A short original text and the kind of question the exam asks about a specified aspect. Tap each highlighted phrase to name its technique. Switch texts to practise on all three.","trap":"Collecting quotes you like instead of quotes that answer the question. Read the question first, then hunt.","big":"The big idea: the question names an aspect (setting, language, structure, character, theme). Every quote you choose must serve that aspect, not just be a nice line.","kupu":["tuhinga","kaituhi"]},{"k":"claim","lab":"Pick the claim","cap":"Three topic sentences. One is arguable (you can prove it), one retells the plot, one is vague. Tap the one that answers the question.","trap":"Starting with 'This essay will discuss…'. Start with the point itself.","big":"The big idea: a claim is a position on the aspect. If a marker could not disagree with it, it isn't a claim; if it only says what happens, it's a summary.","kupu":["kaupapa"]},{"k":"build","lab":"Build it","cap":"Four slots. Drag a quote into EVIDENCE (or tap it), then tap EXPLAIN and SO WHAT to pick lines. The meter rates the paragraph as you build.","trap":"An explanation that would be true of any quote ('this shows the setting is important'). If you could swap the quote and keep the sentence, it isn't explaining.","big":"The big idea: claim → evidence → explanation → so what. The explanation must be about your quote; the 'so what' says why the writer did it and what it means beyond the text.","kupu":["taunakitanga","whakamārama","kōwae"]},{"k":"check","lab":"Self-check","cap":"Read your paragraph, tick what you think it does, then see whether the machine agrees. The grade words are paraphrased in the close-up.","trap":"Ticking 'explains the effect' when the sentence only names the technique. Naming is not explaining.","big":"The big idea: Achieved analyses with evidence; Merit explains convincingly how the writer's choices work; Excellence is perceptive: it shows insight into purpose and reaches beyond the text.","kupu":["kōwae"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Switch to a text you haven't built on, make a paragraph, then press Check. Finish with the one-question check from memory.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: one text, one question, about 750–800 words, three or four paragraphs like this one. Plan the claims first; the quotes come after.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"On any text, fill EVIDENCE with a quote that fully serves the arguable claim.","check":"()=>s.claim&&find(TX().claims,s.claim).s===2&&s.ev&&TX().quotes[s.ev].s===2","ok":"Yes: an arguable claim with a quote that fits it. That is the spine of the paragraph.","no":"()=>!s.claim?\"Not yet: pick a claim first. Hint: the one someone could disagree with.\":find(TX().claims,s.claim).s!==2?\"Not yet: that claim retells or stays vague. Hint: which one takes a position on the aspect?\":!s.ev?\"Not yet: drag a quote into EVIDENCE.\":\"Not yet: that quote is a plain detail or only partly fits. Hint: pick one with a technique that does the claim's job.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"On Nine Hundred Steps, build a full paragraph the machine rates Excellence-like (7 or 8).","check":"()=>s.text===\"steps\"&&score().full&&score().n>=7","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${score().n}/8. Claim, fitting evidence, an explanation about that exact quote, and a 'so what' with a wider idea.`","no":"()=>s.text!==\"steps\"?\"Not yet: switch the text to Nine Hundred Steps.\":!score().full?\"Not yet: fill all four slots.\":`Not yet: ${score().n}/8. Hint: the close-up on the Build step shows which slot is scoring low.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"On Salt Lines, make the explanation match the quote you chose, at full strength.","check":"()=>s.text===\"salt\"&&s.ev&&s.ex&&find(TX().explains,s.ex).q===s.ev&&find(TX().explains,s.ex).s===2","ok":"Yes: the explanation is about your quote and says what the technique does.","no":"()=>s.text!==\"salt\"?\"Not yet: switch the text to Salt Lines.\":!s.ev||!s.ex?\"Not yet: fill EVIDENCE and EXPLAIN.\":\"Not yet: that explanation belongs to a different quote, or it's thin. Hint: it should mention the technique in your quote.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"select","id":"text","label":"Text"},{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"↺ Empty the slots"},{"type":"button","id":"showme","label":"Show me one"}],"shell":"kit"},"unseen":{"id":"unseen","title":"The unfamiliar text machine","href":"m/unseen.html","desc":"An original unseen passage with language features you tag in three moves: technique, effect, link to the main idea. Plain phrases tell you they are plain. A 20-minute exam clock hides the highlights, and a panel shows one tag growing into a five-move paragraph.","blurb":"Tag technique → effect → idea on an unseen text, against the exam clock.","tags":["NCEA L2 English 91100","Year 12","English"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"English · Unfamiliar texts","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 English 91100","exam":"2026-11-12T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 12 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: English past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/english/"}],"truth":"The passage is original, written for this machine. Technique names follow common classroom usage; your teacher may group them differently. The grade words are paraphrased, not NZQA marking, and the exam gives you three texts, not one.","footer":"Covers NCEA Level 2 English 91100 (Analyse significant aspects of unfamiliar written text(s) through close reading, supported by evidence): identifying language features, explaining their effects, linking them to ideas and the writer's purpose, and working at exam pace. Level 3 students can add ?level=3 to the address for 91474.","mainLabel":"An original unseen passage with tappable phrases, a tag card with technique, effect and link choices, an exam clock, and a paragraph growing in five moves","kupu":[{"mi":"tuhinga","en":"text, writing","ta":8648,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kaituhi","en":"writer, author","ta":2031,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kaupapa","en":"topic, theme, purpose","ta":2439,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kupu whakarite","en":"metaphor, figurative language","ta":45734,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"taunakitanga","en":"evidence","ta":14911,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kōwae","en":"paragraph","ta":3169,"note":"","chip":[]}],"steps":[{"k":"read","lab":"First read","cap":"An unfamiliar prose text, original to this machine. Read it twice. The highlights mark phrases doing something extra. Then tap the main idea: everything you tag later must link to it.","trap":"Mistaking the plot for the idea. 'A girl waits for a bus' is what happens; the idea is what the details keep pointing to.","big":"The big idea: decide what the text is about before you hunt for techniques. A feature with no link to an idea is just a label.","kupu":["tuhinga","kaupapa"]},{"k":"tag","lab":"Tag features","cap":"Tap a highlighted phrase, then make three moves: name the technique, its effect, and the link to the main idea. A wrong pick turns peach; try again. Plain phrases tell you they're plain.","trap":"Tagging the technique and stopping. 'The writer uses a simile' earns nothing on its own; the effect and the link are the marks.","big":"The big idea: technique → effect → idea. Naming is Achieved; explaining the effect is Merit; linking it to the idea and the writer's purpose is where Excellence starts.","kupu":["kupu whakarite","taunakitanga"]},{"k":"grow","lab":"Grow a paragraph","cap":"One finished tag becomes one paragraph in five moves. Slide the stage to watch it grow, and see which moves carry the higher grades.","trap":"Writing 'this makes the reader want to read on'. Say what the reader sees, feels or understands, and tie it to the idea.","big":"The big idea: a paragraph is a tag written out: technique and quote, effect, link to idea, why the writer wanted it, and how well it works. The last two are the ones most students skip.","kupu":["kōwae","kaituhi"]},{"k":"timer","lab":"Exam clock","cap":"The exam gives you about 20 minutes a text. The highlights are off, as on the day. Press Start, find the features yourself, and tag as many as you can before the clock runs out.","trap":"Spending the first ten minutes on a perfect first paragraph. Tag three features in your head before you write any.","big":"The big idea: under time, pick three or four features you can explain well rather than listing eight you can't. Short quotes, full explanations.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Answer each challenge, then press Check. Finish with the one-question check from memory.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: three texts (prose, non-fiction, poetry). For each, find the idea first, then tag three features you can explain all the way to the idea.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Tag the simile correctly, all three moves.","check":"()=>done(s.tags[simile])","ok":"()=>`Yes: \"${TX.feats[simile].t}\" is the simile, and you took it all the way to the idea.`","no":"()=>s.tags[simile]&&s.tags[simile].tech===\"ok\"?\"Not yet: the technique is right; finish the effect and the link.\":\"Not yet. Hint: a simile compares with 'like' or 'as'. Find that phrase and tag it.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Finish three full tags on different features.","check":"()=>nDone()>=3","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${nDone()} features tagged technique → effect → idea. That is a full answer's worth.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${nDone()} finished. Hint: a tag counts when all three moves are right.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Pick the main idea on the First read step.","check":"()=>s.idea===TX.idea.ans","ok":"Yes. The storm strips everything back; the warm cars, the dairy's lights and the bus 'lit like a kitchen' are what Mia wants.","no":"()=>s.idea?\"Not that one. Hint: which option do most of the details keep pointing to?\":\"Not yet: go to First read and tap an idea.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"↺ Clear tags"},{"type":"range","id":"stage","label":"Moves"},{"type":"button","id":"start","label":"▶ Start the clock"}],"shell":"kit"},"algebra":{"id":"algebra","title":"The algebra machine","href":"m/algebra.html","desc":"Factorising as an area model you can slide, a quadratic whose roots, vertex and discriminant move with the graph (drag the vertex), a line meeting a parabola, index laws as stacked bars, and a log solving how long a balance takes to double. Every step has a worked path and a your-turn.","blurb":"Area model, live parabola, line meets curve, index and log laws as sliders.","tags":["NCEA L2 Maths 91261","Year 12","Maths","Algebra"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Maths · Algebraic methods","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 Maths 91261","exam":"2026-11-19T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 19 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/mathematics-and-statistics/"}],"truth":"The savings example is made up for practice and compounds once a year. The area model shows negative terms as hatched tiles, a picture to help, not a proof.","footer":"Covers NCEA Level 2 Mathematics and Statistics 91261 (Apply algebraic methods in solving problems): factorising and expanding quadratics, the quadratic formula and discriminant, simultaneous equations with a line and a curve, index laws including negative and fractional exponents, and solving exponential equations with logarithms in a growth context.","mainLabel":"An area model for factorising, a parabola with its vertex and roots, a line crossing it, stacked bars for index laws and an exponential growth graph","kupu":[{"mi":"taurangi","en":"variable","ta":7765,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"whārite","en":"equation","ta":10185,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"pūtake","en":"root (of an equation)","ta":6650,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kauwhata","en":"graph","ta":2460,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"tauoti","en":"integer","ta":7596,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"taupū","en":"index, exponent, power","ta":44680,"note":"","chip":[]}],"steps":[{"k":"area","lab":"Area model","cap":"A rectangle with sides (x + p) and (x + q). Slide p and q and watch the four pieces of area add up to the expanded quadratic. Factorising is finding the sides from the area.","trap":"Getting the signs backwards: x² − x − 12 = (x − 4)(x + 3), not (x + 4)(x − 3). Check the middle term by expanding.","big":"The big idea: (x + p)(x + q) = x² + (p + q)x + pq. To factorise, find two numbers that multiply to the constant and add to the x coefficient.","kupu":["taurangi","whārite"]},{"k":"quad","lab":"Quadratic graph","cap":"y = ax² + bx + c drawn live. Drag the vertex and watch b and c change; slide a to open or flip the parabola. The roots, vertex and y-intercept all move together.","trap":"Reading the vertex x-value as b/2a. It is −b/2a: the sign matters.","big":"The big idea: a sets the shape, c is the y-intercept, and the vertex sits on x = −b/2a. Every feature of the graph is a number you can calculate from a, b and c.","kupu":["kauwhata","whārite"]},{"k":"roots","lab":"Nature of roots","cap":"Slide c up and down. The discriminant Δ = b² − 4ac tells you before you solve whether the graph crosses the x-axis twice, touches once, or misses it.","trap":"Writing Δ = b² − 4ac but substituting a negative c as if it were positive. −4(1)(−3) = +12.","big":"The big idea (Merit): Δ > 0 means two real roots, Δ = 0 means one repeated root (the vertex sits on the axis), Δ < 0 means no real roots. Say which and why.","kupu":["pūtake"]},{"k":"simul","lab":"Line meets curve","cap":"A line and a parabola. Drag the line up and down: the meeting points are where both equations are true at once. Set them equal and you get a new quadratic to solve.","trap":"Finding x and stopping. Substitute back into the line to find y, and give the points.","big":"The big idea: to solve a line and a curve simultaneously, set the two y's equal, rearrange to = 0, and solve. The discriminant of that quadratic says how many meeting points there are.","kupu":["whārite","kauwhata"]},{"k":"index","lab":"Index laws","cap":"Each block is one more multiply by the base. Multiplying powers stacks the blocks (add the exponents); dividing removes them (subtract); a power of a power repeats the stack (multiply).","trap":"Multiplying the bases: 2³ × 2² is 2⁵ = 32, not 4⁵. The base stays; only the exponents combine.","big":"The big idea: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ. A negative exponent means a reciprocal (a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ) and a fraction exponent means a root (a^½ = √a).","kupu":["taupū"]},{"k":"log","lab":"Logs and growth","cap":"A savings balance grows by a fixed percentage each year. When does it reach the target? The unknown is the power, so logs undo it. Slide the rate and the target.","trap":"Dividing the exponent out: 1.05ᵗ = 2 does not give t = 2/1.05. You need logs, or trial and improvement.","big":"The big idea (Excellence): form the model A = P(1 + r)ᵗ from the context, set it equal to the target, and take logs of both sides to bring the exponent down: t = log(target ÷ P) ÷ log(1 + r).","kupu":["taupū"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Set the controls to answer each one, then press Check. Finish with the one-question check from memory.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: show the method even when you can see the answer. For Merit, link two steps (Δ → number of roots). For Excellence, build the model from the words first.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Factorise x² − x − 12: set p and q so the area model shows it.","check":"()=>s.p+s.q===-1&&s.p*s.q===-12","ok":"Yes: 3 and −4 multiply to −12 and add to −1, so x² − x − 12 = (x + 3)(x − 4).","no":"()=>`Not yet: your model is ${poly([1,s.p+s.q,s.p*s.q])}. Hint: which pair multiplies to −12 and adds to −1?`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make the parabola touch the x-axis exactly once.","check":"()=>Math.abs(disc(s.a,s.b,s.c))<1e-9","ok":"()=>`Yes: Δ = ${fmt(disc(s.a,s.b,s.c))}, one repeated root at x = ${fmt(-s.b/(2*s.a))}, and the vertex sits on the axis.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: Δ = ${fmt(disc(s.a,s.b,s.c))}. Hint: you need b² = 4ac. Try moving c until the vertex lands on the axis.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make the line a tangent to the parabola: exactly one meeting point.","check":"()=>Math.abs(disc(s.a,s.b-s.m,s.c-s.k))<1e-9","ok":"Yes: the combined quadratic has Δ = 0, so the line just touches the curve.","no":"()=>{const d=disc(s.a,s.b-s.m,s.c-s.k);return `Not yet: the combined Δ is ${fmt(d)}, so ${d>0?\"the line cuts twice\":\"the line misses\"}. Hint: slide the line's c until Δ hits 0.`}","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"With base 2, divide two powers to get a value of 1/8.","check":"()=>s.base===2&&s.op===\"div\"&&s.ia-s.ib===-3","ok":"()=>`Yes: 2^${fmt(s.ia)} ÷ 2^${fmt(s.ib)} = 2⁻³ = 1/8.`","no":"()=>s.op!==\"div\"?\"Not yet: choose the Divide law first.\":s.base!==2?\"Not yet: set the base to 2.\":`Not yet: 2^${fmt(s.ia-s.ib)} is ${powTxt(2,s.ia-s.ib)}. Hint: 1/8 is 2 to what power?`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"range","id":"p","label":"p"},{"type":"range","id":"q","label":"q"},{"type":"range","id":"a","label":"a"},{"type":"range","id":"b","label":"b"},{"type":"range","id":"c","label":"c"},{"type":"range","id":"m","label":"line m"},{"type":"range","id":"k","label":"line c"},{"type":"select","id":"base","label":"Base"},{"type":"select","id":"op","label":"Law"},{"type":"range","id":"ia","label":"a"},{"type":"range","id":"ib","label":"b"},{"type":"range","id":"rate","label":"Rate"},{"type":"range","id":"target","label":"Target"}],"shell":"kit"},"gradient":{"id":"gradient","title":"The gradient machine","href":"m/gradient.html","desc":"Drag a tangent along a curve and read its gradient while the derivative graph assembles underneath. Turning points, rates of change for a kōwhai seedling and a draining fuel tank, and the anti-derivative as area under the rate graph with a constant you set.","blurb":"A tangent on a rail, the derivative assembling below, a seedling and a tank.","tags":["NCEA L2 Maths 91262","Year 12","Maths","Calculus"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Maths · Calculus methods","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 Maths 91262","exam":"2026-11-19T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 19 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/mathematics-and-statistics/"}],"truth":"The seedling and the fuel tank are simplified models chosen so the derivatives are tidy; real kōwhai and real tanks do not follow a quadratic exactly. Curves are plotted numerically.","footer":"Covers NCEA Level 2 Mathematics and Statistics 91262 (Apply calculus methods in solving problems): gradient at a point and the tangent, differentiating polynomials, gradient functions, turning points and their nature, rates of change in context, and anti-differentiation with a constant found from a known point. Level 3 Calculus 91578 students can add ?level=3 to the address for product, chain, trig and log rules and points of inflection.","mainLabel":"A curve with a draggable tangent point and the gradient function graph assembling underneath; a kōwhai seedling and a fuel tank with their rate graphs; the area under a rate graph building a height graph","kupu":[{"mi":"rōnaki","en":"gradient, slope","ta":13240,"note":"","chip":["grad"]},{"mi":"pākiri","en":"tangent","ta":45822,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"whakapā","en":"derivative","ta":45959,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"pāpātanga","en":"rate","ta":45823,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kauwhata","en":"graph","ta":2460,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"horahanga","en":"area","ta":45656,"note":"","chip":[]}],"steps":[{"k":"tangent","lab":"Tangent","cap":"Drag the point along the curve. The pink line is the tangent there, and its gradient is the number the whole topic is about. Each spot you visit leaves a dot on the lower graph.","trap":"Substituting into f(x) instead of f′(x) for the gradient. f(x) gives the height; f′(x) gives the slope.","big":"The big idea: the gradient of a curve at a point is the gradient of its tangent there. Differentiating gives you a formula, f′(x), that hands you that gradient for any x.","kupu":["rōnaki","pākiri"]},{"k":"deriv","lab":"Gradient function","cap":"The dots join up: the lower graph is f′(x), the gradient function. Where the curve climbs, f′ is above zero; where it falls, f′ is below. Switch curves to compare.","trap":"Forgetting to drop the power by one: x³ differentiates to 3x², not 3x³.","big":"The big idea: the power rule: xⁿ → nxⁿ⁻¹, term by term. Constants vanish because a flat line has gradient 0.","kupu":["whakapā","kauwhata"]},{"k":"turn","lab":"Turning points","cap":"Where the gradient is exactly 0 the curve turns. Drag to a turning point, then look either side: gradient from + to − is a maximum, − to + is a minimum.","trap":"Finding x and stopping. A turning point is a point: give the y-value too, and say maximum or minimum.","big":"The big idea (Merit): solve f′(x) = 0 to find turning points, then decide their nature: check the sign of f′ either side, or the sign of f″.","kupu":["rōnaki","whakapā"]},{"k":"rates","lab":"Rates of change","cap":"Two things that change over time. The gradient of the quantity graph is the rate: cm a week for a seedling, litres a minute for a tank. Drag along and read the rate underneath.","trap":"Quoting the rate with no units. 3 what, per what? Cm per week and litres per minute are part of the answer.","big":"The big idea (Excellence): a rate of change is a derivative with units. Negative means decreasing. Say what the number means in the situation.","kupu":["pāpātanga"]},{"k":"anti","lab":"Anti-derivative","cap":"Reverse it. The area under the rate graph up to week t is the height gained, and the height graph is the anti-derivative. One missing piece: where did it start? That's c.","trap":"Leaving out + c, or leaving c in when the question gives you a starting value to find it.","big":"The big idea: anti-differentiating raises each power by one and divides by the new power, plus a constant c. You need one known point to find c.","kupu":["horahanga","whakapā"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Set the controls to answer each one, then press Check. Finish with the one-question check from memory.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: write the derivative first, every time. For Merit, say what f′ = 0 tells you. For Excellence, give the rate with units and what it means.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"On the cubic, put the point where the gradient is 0 at the maximum.","check":"()=>s.curve===\"cubic\"&&near(s.x,1)","ok":"Yes: f′(1) = 3 − 12 + 9 = 0 and f″(1) = −6 < 0, so (1, 5) is the maximum.","no":"()=>s.curve!==\"cubic\"?\"Not yet: choose the cubic curve first.\":`Not yet: at x = ${fmt(s.x)} the gradient is ${fmt(C().df(s.x))}. Hint: solve 3x² − 12x + 9 = 0 and pick the one where the curve peaks.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"On the cubic, find where the gradient is exactly −3.","check":"()=>s.curve===\"cubic\"&&near(s.x,2)","ok":"Yes: 3x² − 12x + 9 = −3 gives 3(x − 2)² = 0, so x = 2.","no":"()=>s.curve!==\"cubic\"?\"Not yet: choose the cubic curve first.\":`Not yet: the gradient at x = ${fmt(s.x)} is ${fmt(C().df(s.x))}. Hint: set f′(x) = −3 and solve; it is between the two turning points.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Fuel tank: find the moment it is draining at 15 litres a minute.","check":"()=>s.ctx===\"fuel\"&&near(s.t,15,0.26)","ok":"Yes: V′(t) = t − 30 = −15 when t = 15. Draining at 15 L/min means a rate of −15.","no":"()=>s.ctx!==\"fuel\"?\"Not yet: switch the context to the fuel tank.\":`Not yet: at t = ${fmt(s.t)} the rate is ${fmt(CTX.fuel.df(s.t))} L/min. Hint: draining at 15 means V′ = −15.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"select","id":"curve","label":"Curve"},{"type":"range","id":"x","label":"x"},{"type":"select","id":"ctx","label":"Context"},{"type":"range","id":"t","label":"t"},{"type":"range","id":"c","label":"c (start)"},{"type":"toggle","id":"showAll","label":"Show the family"}],"shell":"kit"},"gradient3":{"id":"gradient3","title":"The gradient machine","href":"m/gradient.html?level=3","desc":"The gradient machine in Level 3 mode: product, chain, trig, exponential and log derivatives on five curves, turning points with the second derivative, points of inflection and concavity, and rates of change in context.","blurb":"Product, chain, trig and log derivatives, inflection points and rates, on a draggable tangent.","tags":["NCEA L3 Calculus 91578","Year 13","Maths","Calculus"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Maths · Differentiation","badge":{"label":"NCEA L3 Calculus 91578","exam":"2026-11-16T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Mon 16 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/mathematics-and-statistics/"}],"truth":"The seedling and the fuel tank are simplified models chosen so the derivatives are tidy; real kōwhai and real tanks do not follow a quadratic exactly. Curves are plotted numerically.","footer":"Covers NCEA Level 3 Calculus 91578 (Apply differentiation methods in solving problems): product, chain, trig, exponential and log derivatives, tangents, turning points and their nature, points of inflection and concavity, and rates of change in context. Add ?level=3 to the address for this mode; without it the machine covers Level 2 91262.","mainLabel":"A curve with a draggable tangent point and the gradient function graph assembling underneath; a kōwhai seedling and a fuel tank with their rate graphs; the area under a rate graph building a height graph","kupu":[{"mi":"rōnaki","en":"gradient, slope","ta":13240,"note":"","chip":["grad"]},{"mi":"pākiri","en":"tangent","ta":45822,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"whakapā","en":"derivative","ta":45959,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"pāpātanga","en":"rate","ta":45823,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kauwhata","en":"graph","ta":2460,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"horahanga","en":"area","ta":45656,"note":"","chip":[]}],"steps":[{"k":"tangent","lab":"Tangent","cap":"Drag the point along the curve. The pink line is the tangent and its gradient is f′ there. Switch to the product, trig and log curves: the same idea, harder derivatives.","trap":"Substituting into f(x) instead of f′(x). The height is not the slope.","big":"The big idea: the gradient at a point is f′(x) there, whatever rule produced f′. Differentiate first, substitute second.","kupu":["rōnaki","pākiri"]},{"k":"rules","lab":"Rules","cap":"Product, chain, trig and log derivatives, one curve each. The worked path on the right shows the rule; the lower graph is the f′ it produces.","trap":"Differentiating a product term by term: (5x·e⁻ˣ)′ is not 5·(−e⁻ˣ). Use the product rule.","big":"The big idea: product rule (uv)′ = u′v + uv′; chain rule: differentiate the outside, multiply by the derivative of the inside; (sin x)′ = cos x, (eˣ)′ = eˣ, (ln x)′ = 1/x.","kupu":["whakapā"]},{"k":"turn","lab":"Turning points","cap":"Where f′ = 0 the curve turns. Drag to a turning point and check the nature with f″: negative means a maximum, positive a minimum.","trap":"Stopping at x. Give the point and its nature, with a reason.","big":"The big idea: solve f′(x) = 0, then use the second derivative (or the sign of f′ either side) to say maximum or minimum.","kupu":["rōnaki","whakapā"]},{"k":"inflect","lab":"Inflection","cap":"The lower graph is now f″, the second derivative. Where it crosses zero and changes sign, the curve changes from bending one way to the other: a point of inflection.","trap":"Calling every f″ = 0 an inflection. It must change sign there: check either side.","big":"The big idea (Merit): f″ > 0 means concave up (a smile), f″ < 0 concave down (a frown). A point of inflection has f″ = 0 and a sign change.","kupu":["whakapā"]},{"k":"rates","lab":"Rates of change","cap":"Two things that change over time. The derivative is the rate, with units. Drag along and read the rate underneath; the sign of the second derivative says whether the rate is speeding up or slowing.","trap":"Quoting the rate with no units or sign. Negative is part of the answer.","big":"The big idea (Excellence): a rate is a derivative with units and meaning. For related rates, chain them: dV/dt = dV/dh × dh/dt.","kupu":["pāpātanga"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Set the controls to answer each one, then press Check. Finish with the one-question check from memory.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: name the rule you used. For Merit, justify the nature of each point. For Excellence, link the derivative to the situation.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"On 5x e⁻ˣ, put the point at the maximum.","check":"()=>s.curve===\"prod\"&&near(s.x,1)","ok":"Yes: f′(x) = 5e⁻ˣ(1 − x) = 0 when x = 1, and f″(1) < 0, so (1, 1.84) is the maximum.","no":"()=>s.curve!==\"prod\"?\"Not yet: choose the 5x e⁻ˣ curve first.\":`Not yet: f′(${fmt(s.x)}) = ${fmt(C().df(s.x))}. Hint: e⁻ˣ is never 0, so solve the bracket.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"On 5x e⁻ˣ, find the point of inflection.","check":"()=>s.curve===\"prod\"&&near(s.x,2)","ok":"Yes: f″(x) = 5e⁻ˣ(x − 2) changes sign at x = 2, so (2, 1.35) is the point of inflection.","no":"()=>s.curve!==\"prod\"?\"Not yet: choose the 5x e⁻ˣ curve first.\":`Not yet: f″(${fmt(s.x)}) = ${fmt(C().d2(s.x))}. Hint: differentiate f′ again and solve the bracket.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"On 4 ln(x + 1) − x, find the turning point.","check":"()=>s.curve===\"ln\"&&near(s.x,3)","ok":"Yes: 4/(x + 1) − 1 = 0 gives x + 1 = 4, so x = 3, and f″ < 0 makes it a maximum.","no":"()=>s.curve!==\"ln\"?\"Not yet: choose the ln curve first.\":`Not yet: f′(${fmt(s.x)}) = ${fmt(C().df(s.x))}. Hint: set 4/(x + 1) = 1.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"select","id":"curve","label":"Curve"},{"type":"range","id":"x","label":"x"},{"type":"select","id":"ctx","label":"Context"},{"type":"range","id":"t","label":"t"},{"type":"range","id":"c","label":"c (start)"},{"type":"toggle","id":"showAll","label":"Show the family"}],"shell":"kit"},"unseen3":{"id":"unseen3","title":"The unfamiliar text machine","href":"m/unseen.html?level=3","desc":"The unfamiliar text machine in Level 3 mode: an original reflective non-fiction piece, tags that link each feature to the writer's position, and a paragraph that grows to a critical judgement of how and why the choices work.","blurb":"Tag technique → effect → position on an unseen text, then judge how well it works.","tags":["NCEA L3 English 91474","Year 13","English"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"English · Unfamiliar texts, critical response","badge":{"label":"NCEA L3 English 91474","exam":"2026-11-11T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Wed 11 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: English past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/english/"}],"truth":"The passage is original, written for this machine. Technique names follow common classroom usage; your teacher may group them differently. The grade words are paraphrased, not NZQA marking, and the exam gives you three texts, not one.","footer":"Covers NCEA Level 3 English 91474 (Respond critically to significant aspects of unfamiliar written text(s) through close reading, supported by evidence): identifying language features, explaining effects, linking to the writer's position, and judging how and why the choices work. Add ?level=3 to the address for this mode; without it the machine covers Level 2 91100.","mainLabel":"An original unseen passage with tappable phrases, a tag card with technique, effect and link choices, an exam clock, and a paragraph growing in five moves","kupu":[{"mi":"tuhinga","en":"text, writing","ta":8648,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kaituhi","en":"writer, author","ta":2031,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kaupapa","en":"topic, theme, purpose","ta":2439,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kupu whakarite","en":"metaphor, figurative language","ta":45734,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"taunakitanga","en":"evidence","ta":14911,"note":"","chip":[]},{"mi":"kōwae","en":"paragraph","ta":3169,"note":"","chip":[]}],"steps":[{"k":"read","lab":"First read","cap":"An unfamiliar non-fiction text, original to this machine. Read it twice. The highlights mark phrases doing something extra. Then tap the main idea: everything you tag later must link to it.","trap":"Mistaking the plot for the idea. 'A girl waits for a bus' is what happens; the idea is what the details keep pointing to.","big":"The big idea: decide what the text is about before you hunt for techniques. A feature with no link to an idea is just a label.","kupu":["tuhinga","kaupapa"]},{"k":"tag","lab":"Tag features","cap":"Tap a highlighted phrase, then make three moves: name the technique, its effect, and the link to the main idea. A wrong pick turns peach; try again. Plain phrases tell you they're plain.","trap":"Tagging the technique and stopping. 'The writer uses a simile' earns nothing on its own; the effect and the link are the marks.","big":"The big idea: technique → effect → idea. Naming is Achieved; explaining the effect is Merit; linking it to the idea and the writer's purpose is where Excellence starts.","kupu":["kupu whakarite","taunakitanga"]},{"k":"grow","lab":"Grow a paragraph","cap":"One finished tag becomes one paragraph in five moves. Slide the stage to watch it grow, and see which moves carry the higher grades.","trap":"Writing 'this makes the reader want to read on'. Say what the reader sees, feels or understands, and tie it to the idea.","big":"The big idea: a paragraph is a tag written out: technique and quote, effect, link to idea, why the writer wanted it, and how well it works. The last two are the ones most students skip.","kupu":["kōwae","kaituhi"]},{"k":"timer","lab":"Exam clock","cap":"The exam gives you about 20 minutes a text. The highlights are off, as on the day. Press Start, find the features yourself, and tag as many as you can before the clock runs out.","trap":"Spending the first ten minutes on a perfect first paragraph. Tag three features in your head before you write any.","big":"The big idea: under time, pick three or four features you can explain well rather than listing eight you can't. Short quotes, full explanations.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Answer each challenge, then press Check. Finish with the one-question check from memory.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: three texts, three hours. 'Critically' means you judge the writer's choices and say what they add up to, not just that they exist.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Tag the simile correctly, all three moves.","check":"()=>done(s.tags[simile])","ok":"()=>`Yes: \"${TX.feats[simile].t}\" is the simile, and you took it all the way to the idea.`","no":"()=>s.tags[simile]&&s.tags[simile].tech===\"ok\"?\"Not yet: the technique is right; finish the effect and the link.\":\"Not yet. Hint: a simile compares with 'like' or 'as'. Find that phrase and tag it.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Finish three full tags on different features.","check":"()=>nDone()>=3","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${nDone()} features tagged technique → effect → idea. That is a full answer's worth.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${nDone()} finished. Hint: a tag counts when all three moves are right.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Pick the main idea on the First read step.","check":"()=>s.idea===TX.idea.ans","ok":"Yes. Every detail noticed on foot (the pōhutukawa, Sunil, his daughter) is set against the 'windscreen' view, and the last line reverses what 'recovering' means.","no":"()=>s.idea?\"Not that one. Hint: which option do most of the details keep pointing to?\":\"Not yet: go to First read and tap an idea.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"↺ Clear tags"},{"type":"range","id":"stage","label":"Moves"},{"type":"button","id":"start","label":"▶ Start the clock"}],"shell":"kit"},"engine":{"id":"engine","title":"The engine machine","href":"m/engine.html","desc":"Real car parts from NVIDIA's SimReady set, shown see-through and cut open: gas pressure pushing a piston, work on the stroke, torque and power at the crankshaft, friction turning kinetic energy into heat in a disc brake, and the energy stored in a strut spring.","blurb":"A piston, a crank, a disc brake and a spring, cut open: F = PA, W = Fd, torque, Ek and ½kx².","tags":["Year 10","Year 11","Year 12","Science","Physics","Forces and motion","Energy","NCEA L1 PESS 92047","NCEA L2 Physics 91171"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Physics · Forces and energy","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 Physics 91171","exam":"2026-11-18T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Wed 18 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics/"}],"truth":"A one-cylinder, four-stroke simplification: pressure is treated as constant through the stroke, the whole car is braked by one disc, and the spring is ideal. The 3D parts are NVIDIA SimReady models (CC BY 4.0, browser GLBs by CodePawl); they are the picture, not the source of any number.","footer":"Covers forces and energy for NCEA Level 1 Science/Physics (92047) and Level 2 Physics 91171: F = PA, W = Fd, torque, P = W/t, Ek = ½mv², friction, Es = ½kx².","mainLabel":"Real car parts: a piston in its cylinder, a crankshaft, a disc brake and a strut","kupu":[{"mi":"pēhanga","en":"pressure","ta":"pēhanga","note":"","chip":["P"]},{"mi":"tōpana","en":"force","ta":"tōpana","note":"","chip":["F"]},{"mi":"pūngao","en":"energy","ta":"pūngao","note":"","chip":["Ek"]},{"mi":"wera","en":"heat","ta":"wera","note":"","chip":["heat"]}],"steps":[{"k":"piston","lab":"Push","cap":"Hot gas pushes on the piston. Force = pressure × area. Turn up the pressure, or fit a wider piston, and watch the force.","trap":"Mixing units. Pressure in pascals needs area in square metres: 1 bar = 100 000 Pa, and 86 mm is 0.086 m.","big":"The big idea: pressure is force spread over an area, so F = P × A. Double the area at the same pressure and you double the force.","kupu":[]},{"k":"stroke","lab":"Stroke","cap":"The piston travels one stroke. Work = force × distance, and that work is the energy one bang gives the engine.","trap":"Using the whole bore as the distance. Distance is how far the piston moves (the stroke), not how wide it is.","big":"The big idea: work done = force × distance moved in the direction of the force, W = Fd. The result is energy, in joules.","kupu":[]},{"k":"crank","lab":"Crank","cap":"The con-rod pushes the crank pin round. Force × radius is torque; torque × spin rate is power.","trap":"Confusing torque with power. Torque is the twist (N m); power is how fast that twist does work (W = J/s).","big":"The big idea: power is how fast work is done, P = W ÷ t. Spin faster (more strokes a second) and the same work per stroke makes more power.","kupu":[]},{"k":"brake","lab":"Brake","cap":"Press play to brake. The pads rub the disc, friction does work on the car, and the kinetic energy turns into heat in the disc.","trap":"Doubling the speed doubles the stopping distance. It does not: Ek depends on v², so it is four times.","big":"The big idea: energy is conserved. ½mv² of kinetic energy becomes heat; the friction force times the stopping distance equals that energy, so d = Ek ÷ F.","kupu":[]},{"k":"strut","lab":"Spring","cap":"A coil-over strut. Squash the spring: the force grows with the squash (F = kx) and the stored energy grows with its square.","trap":"Forgetting to square x. The energy is half k x squared, so the units only work with x in metres.","big":"The big idea: a spring stores elastic potential energy, Es = ½kx². Twice the squash is four times the energy.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Set the controls so each statement is true, then press Check. Allen can give a hint.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Set the pressure and bore so the force on the piston is between 20 and 22 kN.","check":"()=>force()>=20000&&force()<=22000","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.P)} bar × ${fmt(area()*1e4,1)} cm² gives ${fmt(force()/1000,1)} kN.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(force()/1000,1)} kN. F = P × A, so raise the pressure or widen the bore (area goes with the radius squared).`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make the car's stopping distance under 25 m from 80 km/h.","check":"()=>s.v===80&&stopD()<25","ok":"()=>`Yes: with μ = ${fmt(s.mu,2)} it stops in ${fmt(stopD(),1)} m.`","no":"()=>s.v!==80?\"Set the speed to 80 km/h first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(stopD(),1)} m. d = Ek ÷ F, and F = μmg, so more grip is the only lever (mass cancels).`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Store at least 100 J in the spring with a squash of 60 mm or less.","check":"()=>s.x<=60&&Es()>=100","ok":"()=>`Yes: ½ × ${fmt(s.k*1000)} × ${fmt(s.x/1000,3)}² = ${fmt(Es())} J.`","no":"()=>s.x>60?\"Keep the squash at 60 mm or less.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(Es())} J. Es = ½kx², so with x fixed you need a stiffer spring.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"range","id":"P","label":"Pressure"},{"type":"range","id":"bore","label":"Bore"},{"type":"range","id":"stroke","label":"Stroke"},{"type":"range","id":"rpm","label":"Spin"},{"type":"range","id":"m","label":"Car mass"},{"type":"range","id":"v","label":"Speed"},{"type":"range","id":"mu","label":"Grip μ"},{"type":"range","id":"k","label":"Stiffness"},{"type":"range","id":"x","label":"Squash"},{"type":"range","id":"glass","label":"See-through"},{"type":"range","id":"cutp","label":"Cut open"}],"shell":"kit"}},"notFound":["momentum, torque, angular velocity, centripetal (vending, downhill, vinyl): no curriculum-cited entry; torohaki/ānga are general words for momentum.","capacitor (phone): two differing Te Aka entries (pūnga iahiko, pūranga iahiko), neither curriculum-cited; left for the teacher.","normal distribution, relative risk (queue): no entry found.","floor plan, scale drawing, brief, stakeholder (floor-plan studio): no entry as a compound; hoahoa covers a building plan.","artificial intelligence: Te Aka has no entry (only *atamai*, intelligent; *waea atamai*, smartphone). Phrases like \"atamai horihori\" are in use elsewhere but were not verified, so none was added. Treat this as newer and contested vocabulary.","lever (fish): Te Aka has verbs for prising or levering, but no noun for a lever. *kauwhiti* appears inside an example sentence but its own entry means something else, so it was not used.","range (statistics), amplitude (Te Aka's two entries conflict), wavelength (only \"(radio) wavelength\"), contour line (Te Aka's *rārangi hua* comes from visual arts), mutation, allele, variation, personification, hyperbole, demand, opportunity cost, trapezium, prism, values (as beliefs): no suitable verified entry found, so none was added.","toharite: Te Reo Pāngarau uses it for *mean*, but Te Aka also records it used for *median* (from Te Ara). The fish machine says so and gives *tau waenga* for median.","iramoe: Te Aka lists it as both *neutron* (Te Reo Pūtaiao) and *recessive gene*. It is used only in atoms, with a note, and left out of genes.","earthquake, volcano, tsunami, tectonic plate, magma, lava, fault, epicentre, liquefaction (Shaky Isles book): not yet looked up on Te Aka (no access from the build environment on 25 October 2026). The book says so on each Prove tab. These are the most useful terms to add next, after checking each entry."]};